export default {
  "../content/blog/accessibility-universal-design-checklist-community-events.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"An Accessibility Checklist for Community Events\"\r\nslug: accessibility-universal-design-checklist-community-events\r\ncategory: Events\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist for planning community events that more people can use, based on universal design: venue, information, communication and welcome.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist for planning community events that more people can use, based on universal design: venue, information, communication and welcome.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [accessibility, universal design, events, inclusion]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: stockWheelchairUser\r\nthumb: stockWheelchairUserThumb\r\nicon: calendar-days\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Universal design means planning so that as many people as possible can use a space or activity without special adjustments.\"\r\n  - \"Accessibility covers the venue, the information, the programme and the welcome, not only ramps.\"\r\n  - \"Ask people what helps them and listen. Do not guess.\"\r\n  - \"Small, low-cost changes often help everyone, including older people, parents with young children and people with temporary injuries.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is universal design?\"\r\n    a: \"Universal design is the practice of designing environments, products and services so that they can be used by everyone, to the greatest extent possible, without needing to be adapted. The Centre for Excellence in Universal Design promotes this approach.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is accessibility only about wheelchair users?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Accessibility also involves people who are blind or have low vision, people who are deaf or hard of hearing, autistic people, people with intellectual disability, older people and anyone who finds a space or message hard to use.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do we need a big budget?\"\r\n    a: \"Not necessarily. Many improvements are about planning: clear signs, seating, quiet spaces, plain language and welcoming helpers. Costly changes may not be possible everywhere, so be honest about what the venue offers.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Centre for Excellence in Universal Design\"\r\n    publisher: \"National Disability Authority, Ireland\"\r\n    url: \"https://universaldesign.ie/\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nAn accessible community event is one that people with different abilities can reach, understand and enjoy without having to ask for special favours. This checklist uses the ideas of universal design, promoted by the Centre for Excellence in Universal Design, to help you plan. It is general guidance, and it is not a substitute for advice from accessibility professionals or the people who will attend.\r\n\r\n## What is universal design, and why use it for events?\r\n\r\nUniversal design means making places, information and services usable by as many people as possible from the start. It avoids designing for an imaginary \"average\" person.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, around 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Many more people benefit from accessible design: an older person with tired legs, a parent with a pushchair, someone with a temporary injury. The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) names accessibility, participation and inclusion as core principles, and events are a practical place to apply them.\r\n\r\nUse the checklist below as a planning tool. Tick what you can do, and write down honestly what you cannot.\r\n\r\n## Venue and getting there\r\n\r\nLook at the whole journey, from the road to the seat.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Is there a route without steps, or a clear plan for people who cannot use steps?\r\n- [ ] Are paths wide, level and free from clutter, loose cables and wet patches?\r\n- [ ] Is there somewhere to sit and rest, indoors or in shade?\r\n- [ ] Are toilets reachable, with enough space for a helper or mobility aid?\r\n- [ ] Is there shade, shelter and drinking water for hot or rainy weather?\r\n- [ ] Have you described transport options and drop-off points in your invitation?\r\n\r\nIf the venue falls short, say so in advance. Accurate information lets people decide, and may help you find a better location next time.\r\n\r\n## Information before the event\r\n\r\nMany barriers appear before anyone arrives.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Is the invitation written in plain, short sentences?\r\n- [ ] Is key information offered in more than one format, such as printed, spoken and by phone?\r\n- [ ] Have you said clearly who is welcome, including children, older people and carers?\r\n- [ ] Do you give a simple running order, so people know what will happen?\r\n- [ ] Is there a named contact person and phone number for questions?\r\n- [ ] Have you invited people to share support needs, without making it compulsory?\r\n\r\n## Communication on the day\r\n\r\n- [ ] Are signs large, clear and placed at eye height?\r\n- [ ] Do speakers use microphones if they are available, face the audience and speak at a steady pace?\r\n- [ ] Are pictures or symbols used alongside words where they help?\r\n- [ ] Do helpers introduce themselves and explain what they are doing?\r\n- [ ] Is there time for questions and for people to answer at their own pace?\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Speaking directly to a person with a disability, not to their companion, is a simple sign of respect. Offer help, but wait for a reply before assisting.\r\n\r\n## Programme and activities\r\n\r\n- [ ] Can activities be joined in more than one way, for example seated or standing?\r\n- [ ] Are instructions demonstrated as well as explained?\r\n- [ ] Are there breaks, and is the pace flexible?\r\n- [ ] Is there a quiet corner for anyone who needs a rest from noise or crowds?\r\n- [ ] Is nobody singled out, or made to perform in front of others, unless they want to?\r\n\r\nFor more on calming the environment, see [making community events sensory-friendly](/blog/article-making-community-events-sensory-friendly).\r\n\r\n## Welcome, attitudes and helpers\r\n\r\nPhysical access is not enough if people feel unwelcome.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Have helpers been briefed to be patient, respectful and ready to listen?\r\n- [ ] Do they use person-respectful language, such as \"person with a disability\" or \"autistic person\"?\r\n- [ ] Do they know where the quiet space, first aid contact and toilets are?\r\n- [ ] Is there a simple way for people to give feedback or raise a concern?\r\n\r\n## Comparing common barriers and simple fixes\r\n\r\n| Barrier | Simple fix |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| Steps at the entrance | Use a level route or a step-free alternative and tell people in advance |\r\n| Crowded seating | Leave clear spaces and aisles for mobility aids and helpers |\r\n| Loud background noise | Reduce music, offer a quieter area and warn people about noisy moments |\r\n| Long, complex instructions | Use short steps, demonstrate and repeat |\r\n| Unclear signs | Use large text, symbols and helpers who can guide |\r\n\r\n## After the event: check and learn\r\n\r\nAsk attendees, carers and helpers what worked and what did not. Ask kindly and give people freedom to skip questions. Record two or three improvements for next time, and share the checklist with future organisers. Our guide on [gathering feedback without exploiting stories](/blog/article-collecting-event-feedback-without-exploiting-stories) explains how to do this with respect.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania supports people with disabilities and challenges, and their families and caregivers, through community-based activities. Accessible planning is one way to make inclusion practical. See how our [neuro-diversity awareness program](/programs/program-neurodiversity) supports understanding.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/annual-report-checklist-for-a-small-nonprofit.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"An Annual Report Checklist for a Small Non-Profit\"\r\nslug: annual-report-checklist-for-a-small-nonprofit\r\ncategory: Transparency\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist of what a small non-profit's annual report should contain, so supporters, families and partners can understand the work.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist of what a small non-profit's annual report should contain, so supporters, families and partners can understand the work.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [annual report, checklist, accountability, governance]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: stockReportReview\r\nthumb: stockReportReviewThumb\r\nicon: file-check-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"A good annual report is short enough to read and complete enough to trust.\"\r\n  - \"It should cover purpose, activities, learning, money, people and safeguarding.\"\r\n  - \"Plain language and clearly stated limits matter more than design.\"\r\n  - \"Small organisations can be transparent without large budgets.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How long should a small non-profit's annual report be?\"\r\n    a: \"Long enough to answer the main questions and short enough that people will read it. A clear report of a few pages, with plain headings, is better than a long brochure. Extra detail can be offered on request.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do small organisations really need an annual report?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Size does not remove the duty to explain. A short, plain report helps supporters, families and partners understand the work, and it helps the organisation itself see what it has learned.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should an annual report include photos of participants?\"\r\n    a: \"Only with clear, informed consent, and with care for dignity and privacy. Many reports work well with photos of activities, places or materials that do not identify anyone. When in doubt, leave it out.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the numbers are small?\"\r\n    a: \"Report them as they are, and explain what they do and do not show. Small, clearly explained numbers are more trustworthy than large numbers with no explanation. Never round up or blur figures to look bigger.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"UNICEF Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n---\r\nA small non-profit's annual report should tell readers why the organisation exists, what it did, what it learned, how it used money, who is responsible, and how it keeps people safe. This checklist lists those parts in plain terms, so any small group can check its own report before sharing it.\r\n\r\nYou do not need a large budget or a design team. You need honesty, structure and a willingness to be questioned.\r\n\r\n## The purpose and context\r\n\r\n- **Mission in one or two sentences:** say who the organisation serves and why.\r\n- **Where it works:** name the area in general terms, such as a district or city.\r\n- **Who leads it:** list the board or leadership and their roles.\r\n- **The problem in context:** if you quote global facts, label them as global and name the source. For example, the World Health Organization estimates that about 16% of the world's population experiences significant disability. That is context, not an achievement.\r\n\r\n## The activities\r\n\r\n- **What was actually done.** Describe programs and sessions in ordinary words.\r\n- **Who took part, in general terms.** Avoid identifying individuals without consent.\r\n- **What changed during the year.** Note new activities, stopped activities and why.\r\n- **What was planned but not done.** Naming what slipped builds trust.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Write activities as verbs. \"Ran life-skills sessions\" is clearer than \"enhanced capacity\".\r\n\r\n## The learning\r\n\r\n- **What worked, and how you know:** point to observations, feedback or records rather than feelings.\r\n- **What did not work:** name at least one difficulty.\r\n- **What you will do differently:** a report without changes of course is often a report without reflection.\r\n- **What you still do not know:** say it openly.\r\n\r\n## The money\r\n\r\nFinancial reporting can be simple and still be clear and complete. A reader should be able to see these points.\r\n\r\n1. Where funds came from, by type of source.\r\n2. How funds were used, in clear categories.\r\n3. What was left over or is being held for later use.\r\n4. Who checked the accounts, if anyone, and how.\r\n\r\nIf any of these are not yet in place, say so, and say what you plan to do. Never guess or round numbers to look better.\r\n\r\n## The people\r\n\r\n- **Staff and volunteers, described in general terms.** Explain their roles and how they are supported.\r\n- **Training.** Say what training exists for people who work with vulnerable groups.\r\n- **Decision-making.** Explain who decides what.\r\n\r\n## Safety, consent and dignity\r\n\r\nFor work with children, people with disabilities and older people, this section is essential.\r\n\r\n- **Safeguarding approach.** State how the organisation protects people and how concerns can be raised.\r\n- **Consent for stories and images.** Explain your rules.\r\n- **Language.** Use respectful, person-first or identity-first wording that people prefer, and avoid pity.\r\n- **Privacy.** Say how personal information is handled.\r\n\r\nOur article on [why consent matters in non-profit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explains why this belongs in every report.\r\n\r\n## The comparison and the future\r\n\r\n- **Compare with last year, if there is one.** Say what is the same and what differs.\r\n- **Plans for the coming year.** Label them clearly as plans.\r\n- **How readers can respond.** Give a contact route and invite questions.\r\n\r\n## Making the report easy to use\r\n\r\nA report nobody can open or understand has not really been shared. Small choices help.\r\n\r\n- **Use plain headings.** Readers should find the money, the people and the learning without hunting.\r\n- **Keep files light and readable.** Choose a format that opens on a phone, since many readers do not use a computer.\r\n- **Explain terms once.** Spell out abbreviations and technical words the first time they appear.\r\n- **Offer other ways to read it.** Where possible, provide a short summary, a translation or a spoken explanation for people who find long documents hard.\r\n- **Date it.** Say which period the report covers and when it was published.\r\n\r\n## What to leave out, and what to fix later\r\n\r\nBeing complete does not mean including everything. Leave out personal details about children, families or older people, and anything shared in confidence. Do not add a number just because it sounds good.\r\n\r\nMistakes will happen. Decide in advance how you will handle them.\r\n\r\n1. Keep a note of what changed between versions.\r\n2. Correct errors openly, and say what was wrong.\r\n3. Tell people who received the earlier version, where you can.\r\n4. Use the correction to improve next year's process.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** A visible correction usually builds more trust than a hidden one. Readers know that small organisations make mistakes. They notice how those mistakes are handled.\r\n\r\n## The final check before publishing\r\n\r\nBefore you share the report, read it as a stranger would.\r\n\r\n- Are all numbers explained and sourced?\r\n- Are plans clearly separated from results?\r\n- Are limits stated?\r\n- Could any person be identified against their wishes?\r\n- Is the language plain enough to translate easily?\r\n- Is there a clear way to ask questions?\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nThe Foundation was founded in 2025 and does not currently publish numeric results. Its [Impact page](/impact) lists the four things it intends to report on: reach, learning, practice and experience. Reporting will be shared on this website as it becomes available. We will use a checklist like this one to hold ourselves to account, and we welcome your questions through the [contact page](/contact).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/autism-adhd-down-syndrome-explained-simply.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Autism, ADHD and Down Syndrome Explained Simply\"\r\nslug: autism-adhd-down-syndrome-explained-simply\r\ncategory: Neuro-diversity\r\nexcerpt: \"A plain-language, respectful introduction to autism, ADHD and Down syndrome: what each is, how they differ, and how communities can support people.\"\r\ndescription: \"A plain-language, respectful introduction to autism, ADHD and Down syndrome: what each is, how they differ, and how communities can support people.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [autism, adhd, down-syndrome, explainer]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/autism-adhd-down-syndrome-explained-simply.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/autism-adhd-down-syndrome-explained-simply-thumb.webp\r\nicon: brain\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Autism and ADHD are differences in how the brain develops and works, while Down syndrome is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21.\"\r\n  - \"Each condition is a spectrum or range. No two people are the same.\"\r\n  - \"Support works best when it is matched to the person's strengths and needs.\"\r\n  - \"Only qualified professionals can assess or diagnose. Families should never feel alone in seeking that help.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Can a person have more than one of these conditions?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Autism and ADHD can occur together, and a person with Down syndrome may also be autistic or have ADHD. This is why a full assessment by qualified professionals matters, so support fits the whole person.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is Down syndrome an illness?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Down syndrome is a genetic condition, not an illness. It is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21. People with Down syndrome may have particular health or learning needs, and they can also learn, work, form friendships and enjoy full lives.\"\r\n  - q: \"Where can a family get an assessment?\"\r\n    a: \"A doctor, paediatrician or qualified specialist can guide you toward assessment. Each family's situation is different, so start with a health professional you trust. Eagle's Wings offers general awareness and guidance, not diagnosis.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"ADHD\"\r\n    publisher: \"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.cdc.gov/adhd/index.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n---\r\nAutism and ADHD are natural differences in how the brain develops and works. Down syndrome is a genetic condition caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21. All three are lifelong, all vary widely from person to person, and all are best understood through a person's strengths as well as their support needs.\r\n\r\n## Why explain these together?\r\n\r\nFamilies and teachers often meet these words at the same time, and they can feel confusing. This article gives a simple starting point. It is general education, not medical advice, and it cannot replace an assessment by a qualified professional.\r\n\r\nNotice that we say \"a person with autism\" or \"an autistic person\". Both are used. It is respectful to ask what someone prefers.\r\n\r\n## What is autism?\r\n\r\nAutism is a difference in how a person communicates, interacts with others and experiences the world. The World Health Organization describes it as a wide spectrum, and estimates that [about 1 in 100 children worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism. Support needs vary greatly. Some autistic people need little daily support, while others need a lot.\r\n\r\nCommon signs can include:\r\n\r\n- Preferring routine and finding change stressful.\r\n- Being very sensitive, or less sensitive, to sound, light, touch or taste.\r\n- Communicating in different ways, such as fewer words, gestures, pictures or devices.\r\n- Deep interest in particular topics.\r\n\r\nThese are not flaws. Many autistic people bring honesty, focus and creativity to their families and communities.\r\n\r\n## What is ADHD?\r\n\r\nADHD stands for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. The CDC describes it as a condition that can make it harder to pay attention, control impulses or manage high levels of energy. It often begins in childhood and can continue into adult life.\r\n\r\nA child with ADHD may:\r\n\r\n- Find it hard to stay with a task that is not exciting.\r\n- Move a lot, or feel restless when sitting for long.\r\n- Act before thinking, or interrupt often.\r\n- Be very energetic, curious and quick to notice new things.\r\n\r\nADHD is not caused by poor parenting or laziness. Structure, movement breaks, clear instructions and encouragement often help. Any decision about treatment or medication belongs with a doctor.\r\n\r\n## What is Down syndrome?\r\n\r\nDown syndrome happens when a person is born with an extra copy of chromosome 21. It is not caused by anything a parent did, and it is not contagious. People with Down syndrome usually have some degree of learning difference, and may also have particular health needs, so regular health care is important.\r\n\r\nAlongside this, people with Down syndrome are often warm, sociable and determined. With early support, education and inclusion, many learn new skills, build friendships and take part in daily life and work.\r\n\r\n## How do they compare?\r\n\r\n| | Autism | ADHD | Down syndrome |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| What it is | A difference in communication, social interaction and sensory experience | A difference in attention, activity level and impulse control | A genetic condition from an extra copy of chromosome 21 |\r\n| Range | Wide spectrum | Varies by person and setting | Varies by person |\r\n| Often helpful | Predictable routines, clear communication, sensory comfort | Structure, movement breaks, short clear tasks | Early support, patient teaching, regular health care |\r\n| Assessed by | Qualified professionals | Qualified professionals | Health professionals |\r\n\r\nThis table is a simplification. Real people do not fit neatly into columns.\r\n\r\n## What helps, whatever the label?\r\n\r\nSome support ideas work across all three:\r\n\r\n1. **Learn the person, not only the label.** Ask what they enjoy and what is hard for them.\r\n2. **Use clear, simple words.** Show as well as tell, using gestures or pictures.\r\n3. **Keep routines predictable,** and prepare people for change.\r\n4. **Celebrate effort and small steps.**\r\n5. **Include them.** Games, chores, meals and classes are all chances to belong.\r\n6. **Care for caregivers.** Rest and community support matter.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** If you are worried about a child's development, write down what you notice, such as how they play, speak or react to sounds. Bring these notes to a doctor or specialist. It makes the conversation easier.\r\n\r\n## Are these conditions only a challenge?\r\n\r\nNo. Challenges are real, and families deserve support. But the strengths-based view reminds us that difference and ability live together. The [1.3 billion people, about 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) whom WHO estimates experience significant disability are not a separate group. They are part of every community.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania provides awareness and education on autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability, and supports families and caregivers. We do not diagnose or treat. We encourage families to seek qualified assessment and offer general guidance and community. Learn more on the [Neuro-diversity program page](/programs/program-neurodiversity), read about [education and therapy guidance](/programs/program-education-therapy), or try our related article on [communication tips that help](/blog/article-communication-tips-for-neurodivergent-children).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/boundaries-confidentiality-respect-supporting-children-older-people.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Boundaries, Confidentiality and Respect: A Volunteer Checklist\"\r\nslug: boundaries-confidentiality-respect-supporting-children-older-people\r\ncategory: \"Volunteering\"\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist for volunteers supporting children and older people: healthy boundaries, privacy, respectful language and when to ask for help.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist for volunteers supporting children and older people: healthy boundaries, privacy, respectful language and when to ask for help.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [boundaries, confidentiality, safeguarding, respect]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: /photos/blog/boundaries-confidentiality-respect-supporting-children-older-people.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/boundaries-confidentiality-respect-supporting-children-older-people-thumb.webp\r\nicon: hand-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Boundaries protect both the person supported and the volunteer.\"\r\n  - \"Treat everything you learn about a person or family as private unless told otherwise.\"\r\n  - \"Use language and actions that show dignity, and always ask before helping.\"\r\n  - \"If something worries you, tell the responsible coordinator promptly.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Can I take photos of the people I support?\"\r\n    a: \"Only if the organisation's rules allow it and you have clear permission from the person or their family. When in doubt, do not take or share photos. Ask your coordinator before posting anything online.\"\r\n  - q: \"What should I do if a child or older person tells me something worrying?\"\r\n    a: \"Stay calm and listen kindly. Do not promise to keep it secret and do not investigate yourself. Tell the responsible coordinator as soon as possible, following the organisation's safeguarding guidance.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is it wrong to become friendly with the people I support?\"\r\n    a: \"Warmth is welcome. The key is keeping your role clear. Be kind, but avoid private arrangements, gifts or contact outside the organisation's channels unless the organisation agrees.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Violence against children\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Ageing and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ageing-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nHealthy boundaries, strict confidentiality and everyday respect keep children and older people safe and keep volunteers on solid ground. Use the checklist below before, during and after every volunteering session. It is general guidance. Your organisation's own safeguarding rules always take priority.\r\n\r\n## Why do boundaries matter?\r\n\r\nChildren and older people, especially those with disabilities or who live with dementia, may depend on others for daily support. That dependence can create an imbalance of power, even when everyone means well. Boundaries are the agreed limits that keep the relationship safe, clear and respectful.\r\n\r\nBoundaries are not coldness. You can be warm, patient and cheerful while still keeping a clear role.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 1: Personal boundaries\r\n\r\nTick each one before a session.\r\n\r\n- [ ] I know my role and what tasks I have agreed to do.\r\n- [ ] I will not do personal care, lifting or exercise support unless I am trained and assigned to it.\r\n- [ ] I will not be alone with a child or vulnerable person in a private space unless the organisation's rules clearly allow it.\r\n- [ ] I will not give gifts, money or favours outside the organisation's arrangements.\r\n- [ ] I will not make private arrangements with families or share my personal contact details unless the organisation agrees.\r\n- [ ] I will not give medical advice or suggest changes to medication or treatment.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 2: Confidentiality\r\n\r\nWhat you see and hear is not yours to share.\r\n\r\n- [ ] I treat names, health information, family situations and behaviour as private.\r\n- [ ] I do not discuss people I support in public places, with friends or on social media.\r\n- [ ] I ask before taking any photo or video, and I follow the organisation's rules on what may be shared.\r\n- [ ] I store notes safely and do not leave documents where others can read them.\r\n- [ ] I share concerns only with the responsible coordinator.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Confidentiality has a limit. If someone may be at risk of harm, you must pass the concern on to the responsible person. Never promise a child or older person that you will keep a worrying disclosure secret.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 3: Respectful language and behaviour\r\n\r\nRespect is shown in small choices.\r\n\r\n- [ ] I speak to the person directly, at their eye level, in a calm tone.\r\n- [ ] I use person-respectful words such as \"person with a disability\", \"autistic person\" or \"person with autism\", and \"person living with dementia\".\r\n- [ ] I ask before I help and I offer choices.\r\n- [ ] I avoid talking about someone in front of them as if they were not there.\r\n- [ ] I do not use baby talk with adults, even when they are older or living with dementia.\r\n- [ ] I avoid pity, praise that sounds surprised (\"You did that so well!\") and labels that reduce a person to a condition.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 4: Safety in activities\r\n\r\n- [ ] I follow supervision instructions at all times.\r\n- [ ] I do not lead water, swimming or exercise activities unless I am assigned and trained.\r\n- [ ] I know who to call in an emergency.\r\n- [ ] I tell a coordinator if someone seems unwell, distressed or unsafe.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization notes that children and older people can face a greater risk of violence and neglect, which is one reason clear safeguards matter. Volunteers are part of the protective circle around them.\r\n\r\n## Comparing healthy and unhealthy patterns\r\n\r\n| Healthy pattern | Warning sign |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Warm, consistent, clear role | Special favourites or secret friendships |\r\n| Asking permission before helping | Taking over without asking |\r\n| Sharing concerns with the coordinator | Keeping worries to yourself |\r\n| Following photo and sharing rules | Posting about people online |\r\n| Admitting what you do not know | Giving advice beyond your training |\r\n\r\n## Checklist 5: After the session\r\n\r\n- [ ] I report any incident, injury or concern the same day.\r\n- [ ] I leave personal information about people at the venue or in safe storage.\r\n- [ ] I reflect on what went well and what to ask about next time.\r\n- [ ] I look after my own wellbeing and talk with a coordinator if a session was upsetting.\r\n\r\n## What if I am not sure?\r\n\r\nAsk. No responsible organisation will criticise a volunteer for checking. Asking protects the person you support and protects you.\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) is built on dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination and respect for difference. Each item on this list is a small way of living those principles.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation exists to empower, encourage and educate people with special needs and older people, and to support their families. Respect and dignity are part of that mission. Anyone interested in volunteering should expect to learn and follow clear guidance about conduct and privacy.\r\n\r\nLearn more on the [volunteers page](/volunteers), read about our [mission and values](/about#mission), or start a conversation through [Get Involved](/get-involved). You can also read our [privacy](/privacy) information.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/breaking-a-chore-into-small-teachable-steps.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Breaking a Chore into Small Teachable Steps\"\r\nslug: breaking-a-chore-into-small-teachable-steps\r\ncategory: Life skills\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical method for teaching chores like washing clothes or mopping by splitting them into small steps, with examples families can adapt at home.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical method for teaching chores like washing clothes or mopping by splitting them into small steps, with examples families can adapt at home.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [life-skills, task-analysis, chores, teaching]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/breaking-a-chore-into-small-teachable-steps.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/breaking-a-chore-into-small-teachable-steps-thumb.webp\r\nicon: sparkles\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Large chores feel easier when broken into short, clear, ordered steps.\"\r\n  - \"Teach one step at a time, and use a prompt that you can slowly reduce.\"\r\n  - \"Pictures, gestures or words can be used, depending on what helps the person.\"\r\n  - \"Praise effort and progress, and keep sessions calm, short and safe.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How small should each step be?\"\r\n    a: \"Small enough that the person can succeed with little or no help. If a step causes stress or errors, split it again. Steps can be joined together later as confidence grows.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should I teach the first step first, or the last?\"\r\n    a: \"Both work. Some people learn well from starting at the beginning. Others like finishing the last step themselves, because completing the job feels rewarding. Choose what suits the person.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the person refuses?\"\r\n    a: \"Pause and check for tiredness, discomfort or confusion. Offer a choice, try a different time, or make the step smaller. Refusal is communication and deserves a respectful response.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism spectrum disorder\"\r\n    publisher: \"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.cdc.gov/autism/index.html\"\r\n---\r\nTo teach a chore, break it into small steps in a fixed order, teach one step at a time, and give only as much help as the person needs. This method is often called task analysis, and families can use it at home with everyday tools like a bucket, a mop or a basin of washing.\r\n\r\n## Why break chores into steps?\r\n\r\nA chore that looks simple to an adult often hides many decisions. Washing clothes, for example, involves sorting, wetting, soaping, scrubbing, rinsing, wringing and hanging. If we ask a young person to \"do the laundry\", we may be asking for all of it at once.\r\n\r\nSmall steps reduce that load. They also make it clear where a person needs help, so support is targeted rather than general. Many autistic people find clear, predictable sequences easier to follow, though every person is different.\r\n\r\n## Step by step: how to build a teaching plan\r\n\r\n### 1. Choose one chore that has meaning\r\n\r\nPick something useful in your household, such as mopping a floor, washing a few clothes, or watering plants. Real purpose is motivating.\r\n\r\n### 2. Do the chore yourself and write the steps\r\n\r\nPerform the chore slowly. Write down each action in the order you do it. Use short phrases that start with a verb.\r\n\r\n### 3. Check the steps for hidden parts\r\n\r\nAsk what the person must already know. Where is the water? How do they know the floor is clean? Add those steps if needed.\r\n\r\n### 4. Decide how to show the steps\r\n\r\nOptions include:\r\n\r\n- Spoken words, one step at a time.\r\n- Photographs or drawings placed in order.\r\n- A simple written list for people who read.\r\n- A demonstration with the person watching, then joining.\r\n\r\nChoose the option the person understands best. You can also mix them.\r\n\r\n### 5. Teach one step, then add the next\r\n\r\nStart with one step, practise it until it feels comfortable, and then link the next. Keep sessions short. It is better to finish while things are going well.\r\n\r\n### 6. Reduce help slowly\r\n\r\nBegin with the level of help that lets the person succeed. That might be hand-over-hand help. Then move to pointing, then a picture, then a quiet reminder, and finally no prompt.\r\n\r\n### 7. Celebrate progress\r\n\r\nNotice effort, not just perfection. A smile, a thank-you or a shared cup of tea can be enough.\r\n\r\n## Example: washing a few clothes\r\n\r\nThis is a general illustration, not a fixed rule. Change it to suit your home and the person.\r\n\r\n1. Collect the dirty clothes in a basket.\r\n2. Put a small amount of water in the basin.\r\n3. Add a little soap and mix.\r\n4. Place one item in the water.\r\n5. Rub the cloth gently between the hands.\r\n6. Rinse the item in clean water.\r\n7. Squeeze out the water.\r\n8. Hang the item on the line.\r\n9. Put the basin away and dry your hands.\r\n\r\nNotice that each line is one action. If squeezing is hard because of hand strength, you might teach it with a lighter cloth first, or share the step.\r\n\r\n## Example: mopping a small room\r\n\r\n1. Move small items off the floor.\r\n2. Fill the bucket with a little water.\r\n3. Wet the mop and wring it.\r\n4. Mop from the far corner towards the door.\r\n5. Rinse the mop when it looks dirty.\r\n6. Let the floor dry, and walk carefully until then.\r\n7. Empty the bucket and hang the mop.\r\n\r\nA wet floor can be slippery, so walk slowly and keep the area clear.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Keep one set of steps in the same place, such as beside the washing basin. Consistency helps the person learn without depending on a particular helper.\r\n\r\n## What if it is not going well?\r\n\r\nEvery learner has hard days. Try these ideas:\r\n\r\n- Make the step smaller.\r\n- Change the time of day.\r\n- Offer a choice of two tools or two tasks.\r\n- Check for noise, heat or discomfort.\r\n- Take a break and return later.\r\n\r\nIf a person seems distressed often, or you have worries about their health or behaviour, speak with a doctor, therapist or other qualified professional. This article offers general education only.\r\n\r\n## Keeping it dignified\r\n\r\nTeach in private if the person prefers. Speak to them as you would to any peer. Avoid comparing them with siblings or neighbours. Chores are shared work, and a young person who contributes is a valued member of the household.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nBreaking tasks into steps is part of how life-skills learning is often approached. Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania offers structured life-skills support, especially for young people with autism who are 12 and above, and we involve families so that skills continue at home. Learn more on our [life skills program page](/programs/program-life-skills), and read our article on [building everyday independence](/blog/article-building-everyday-independence-one-skill-at-a-time).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/building-everyday-independence-one-skill-at-a-time.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Building Everyday Independence One Skill at a Time\"\r\nslug: building-everyday-independence-one-skill-at-a-time\r\ncategory: Life skills\r\nexcerpt: \"Independence grows from small, practised skills. Learn what life skills are, why they matter for autistic young people, and how families can support them.\"\r\ndescription: \"Independence grows from small, practised skills. Learn what life skills are, why they matter for autistic young people, and how families can support them.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [life-skills, independence, autism, routines]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/building-everyday-independence-one-skill-at-a-time.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/building-everyday-independence-one-skill-at-a-time-thumb.webp\r\nicon: sparkles\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Independence is built from many small skills, each practised in real settings, not learned all at once.\"\r\n  - \"Life skills include daily living, communication, community participation and self-care.\"\r\n  - \"Support should match each person's strengths, pace and preferences, and fade slowly as confidence grows.\"\r\n  - \"Independence does not mean doing everything alone. Choosing, asking for help and taking part all count.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What are life skills?\"\r\n    a: \"Life skills are the everyday abilities that help a person take part in home and community life. They include washing, dressing, preparing food, cleaning, handling money, communicating needs and moving safely around familiar places.\"\r\n  - q: \"At what age should life-skills teaching begin?\"\r\n    a: \"Small tasks can begin early, matched to a child's age and interests. Structured support often becomes especially useful in the teenage years. Eagle's Wings focuses on young people with autism who are 12 and above.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does independence mean a person needs no help?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Independence means having choice and control over your own life. Many people, with or without disability, rely on others for some tasks. Asking for help well is itself a valuable skill.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should a professional be involved?\"\r\n    a: \"Where possible, yes. An occupational therapist or another qualified professional can assess needs and suggest approaches. This article is general education, not personal advice.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n---\r\nIndependence is not a single milestone. It is a collection of small skills, such as folding clothes, sweeping a floor or asking for help, that a person practises until they feel confident. When support is patient and respectful, each skill adds to a person's choice and dignity.\r\n\r\n## What do we mean by life skills?\r\n\r\nLife skills are the practical abilities that let a person take part in everyday life. They are often grouped into a few areas:\r\n\r\n- **Daily living:** washing, dressing, preparing simple meals, doing laundry, cleaning and tidying.\r\n- **Communication:** expressing needs, making choices, greeting people and asking for help.\r\n- **Community participation:** using shared spaces, following a routine, moving safely, and joining group activities.\r\n- **Self-care and wellbeing:** personal hygiene, rest, and noticing how the body and feelings are doing.\r\n\r\nNone of these areas is more important than the others. A young person may be confident with gardening and still need time with money or travel. That is normal for everyone.\r\n\r\n## Why does independence matter?\r\n\r\nIndependence is closely linked to dignity. The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on principles such as autonomy, participation and inclusion. Learning life skills is one practical way to bring those principles into daily life.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization notes that [about 1 in 100 children worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism, and that support needs vary greatly. Some autistic people live largely independently, while others need daily support. Both are valid. The aim is not to make anyone \"less autistic\". The aim is to widen a person's choices.\r\n\r\nNote that both \"autistic person\" and \"person with autism\" are used by different people. It is polite to ask which a person prefers.\r\n\r\n## How do skills actually grow?\r\n\r\nSkills rarely appear from one explanation. They usually develop through steps that repeat.\r\n\r\n### Start with what matters to the person\r\n\r\nA skill is easier to learn when it connects to something the person cares about. Someone who loves plants may enjoy watering and weeding. Someone who likes a clean, orderly room may be motivated by mopping. Interests are a doorway to learning.\r\n\r\n### Break tasks into small parts\r\n\r\nBig tasks can feel overwhelming. Splitting a task into small, clear steps makes it easier to learn and to teach. Our article on [breaking a chore into small teachable steps](/blog/article-breaking-a-chore-into-small-teachable-steps) shows how.\r\n\r\n### Practise in real settings\r\n\r\nPractising laundry with real clothes, or watering with a real garden, teaches more than a demonstration. Real settings also help skills carry over into everyday life.\r\n\r\n### Fade support slowly\r\n\r\nSupport may begin with hand-over-hand help, then move to a gesture, then a picture, then only a nearby presence. Each person moves at their own speed, and moving back a step is not failure.\r\n\r\n## What does helpful support look like?\r\n\r\nThe table below compares two common approaches.\r\n\r\n| Approach that builds independence | Approach that can limit it |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| Offers choices and waits for a response | Decides for the person |\r\n| Teaches one step at a time | Demonstrates the whole task once |\r\n| Celebrates effort and progress | Focuses only on mistakes |\r\n| Reduces help gradually | Removes help suddenly or never |\r\n| Respects privacy and preferences | Ignores how the person feels |\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Doing a task for someone is sometimes faster, but it can quietly teach them that they are not expected to try. A little extra time now often creates more choice later.\r\n\r\n## How can families begin at home?\r\n\r\nHome is often the best classroom. A few gentle ideas:\r\n\r\n1. Pick one small task that fits the household, such as carrying a bucket, wiping a table or feeding a pet.\r\n2. Do the task at the same time each day, so it becomes a routine.\r\n3. Use words, pictures or demonstration in the way that works best for the person.\r\n4. Give the person a real role, so the task has meaning for the family.\r\n5. Notice and name what went well.\r\n\r\nExtended families and neighbours can help too. In many Tanzanian communities, children and young people already share household tasks. Making room for a young person with a disability to share in these tasks is a simple form of inclusion.\r\n\r\n## What should we remember about safety and rights?\r\n\r\nSkills should never be taught in ways that are unsafe or humiliating. Watch for tiredness and stress. Keep hot water, sharp tools and chemicals under adult supervision until the person and their support team agree it is safe. If you have questions about a particular young person's needs, speak with a doctor, therapist or other qualified professional.\r\n\r\nAlso remember that a person has the right to say no, to rest, and to change their mind. Independence includes choosing.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania offers structured life-skills support, especially for young people with autism who are 12 and above. Our approach is to respect each person's pace, build on strengths, and involve families. You can read more on our [life skills program page](/programs/program-life-skills), or [contact us](/contact) if you would like to ask a question.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/choosing-a-volunteer-role-that-fits-your-skills-and-time.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"How to Choose a Volunteer Role That Fits Your Skills and Time\"\r\nslug: choosing-a-volunteer-role-that-fits-your-skills-and-time\r\ncategory: \"Volunteering\"\r\nexcerpt: \"A step-by-step way to match your skills, energy and schedule to a volunteer role you can keep up, so the people you support can rely on you.\"\r\ndescription: \"A step-by-step way to match your skills, energy and schedule to a volunteer role you can keep up, so the people you support can rely on you.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [volunteering, skills, time, planning]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/choosing-a-volunteer-role-that-fits-your-skills-and-time.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/choosing-a-volunteer-role-that-fits-your-skills-and-time-thumb.webp\r\nicon: hand-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Start by listing your skills, your real available time and your limits.\"\r\n  - \"A smaller role you can keep is better than a big one you drop.\"\r\n  - \"Ask the organisation what roles exist instead of assuming.\"\r\n  - \"Review your fit after a while. It is fine to adjust.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Do I need special qualifications to volunteer?\"\r\n    a: \"Not always. Many kinds of support need only reliability, respect and willingness to learn. Some tasks do need training or qualifications. Ask the organisation which roles have requirements and follow what they tell you.\"\r\n  - q: \"How much time should I offer?\"\r\n    a: \"Offer what you can honestly keep up. People who depend on routines are better served by a modest, steady commitment than by a large one that ends suddenly. You can always increase later.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if I choose a role and it is not right for me?\"\r\n    a: \"Tell the coordinator early and kindly. Roles can be adjusted, and an honest conversation is far better than quietly disappearing, which can leave people without support they expected.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"United Nations Volunteers\"\r\n    publisher: \"UN Volunteers\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unv.org/\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nThe best volunteer role is one that uses what you are good at, fits the time you have and matches what the organisation needs. Work through these steps in order and you will avoid the common mistake of promising more than you can deliver. The people you support rely on you being there.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: List what you can offer\r\n\r\nTake a sheet of paper and write down your skills. Include the obvious ones, such as teaching, cooking or driving, and the quieter ones, such as patience, listening, tidiness or being calm under pressure.\r\n\r\nDo not limit yourself to your job. Someone who runs a household well may be excellent at organising a busy activity space. Someone who is a good listener may bring real comfort to an older person who enjoys company.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Be honest about your time\r\n\r\nMany volunteers begin with enthusiasm and then run out of hours. Look at your week as it really is: work, study, family duties, travel time in Dar es Salaam traffic, rest.\r\n\r\nAsk yourself:\r\n\r\n- How many hours can I give regularly, without stress?\r\n- Are my free times predictable, or do they change every week?\r\n- Could I still show up during a busy month?\r\n\r\nChoose a pattern you can keep for a long stretch, not just a few weeks. Consistency matters because many people supported by community work, including children and older people, feel more secure with familiar faces.\r\n\r\n## Step 3: Know your limits and your comfort zone\r\n\r\nEveryone has limits. Some people find hospital-like settings hard. Some are not comfortable with physical support such as lifting. Some are still learning how to speak respectfully about disability.\r\n\r\nNone of this rules you out. It simply shapes where you begin. Be plain with the coordinator about what you are not ready for. Roles that involve personal care, physical exercise or water activities need proper training and supervision, so never take these on informally.\r\n\r\n## Step 4: Ask what roles exist\r\n\r\nDo not assume what the organisation needs. Ask. Roles change with programs and seasons, and a helpful idea for one group may be unnecessary for another.\r\n\r\nYou can read about current opportunities on the [volunteers page](/volunteers) and learn what the [programs](/programs) involve so your questions are informed. Then contact the team through the [Get Involved](/get-involved) page.\r\n\r\n## Step 5: Compare roles side by side\r\n\r\nIf more than one option appeals, a short comparison helps.\r\n\r\n| Question | Role A | Role B |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Does it use a skill I enjoy? | | |\r\n| Can I keep the schedule? | | |\r\n| Is training available? | | |\r\n| Who supervises me? | | |\r\n| Do I feel comfortable with the tasks? | | |\r\n\r\nFill in your own answers. Whichever role gets the most confident answers is usually the best start.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** If you are unsure, start with the smaller commitment. It is easy to add hours later. It is much harder to repair trust after a dropped promise.\r\n\r\n## Step 6: Prepare and begin\r\n\r\nOnce you agree on a role, get the basics clear. Find out where to go, who to report to, what to wear and what the safety expectations are. Learn the respectful language the organisation uses when speaking about people with disabilities and older people. Ask what is confidential and what you must not photograph or share.\r\n\r\n## Step 7: Review and adjust\r\n\r\nAfter a few sessions, pause and reflect. Are you enjoying the work? Are you being useful? Do you feel supported? Talk with your coordinator. It is normal to change tasks, reduce hours or take on more once you understand the work.\r\n\r\n## Common mistakes to avoid\r\n\r\n- Choosing a role because it sounds impressive rather than because it fits.\r\n- Ignoring travel time and rest.\r\n- Staying silent when a task feels unsafe or beyond your training.\r\n- Treating volunteering as a way to fix people instead of a way to support them.\r\n\r\n## Why fit matters so much\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [1.3 billion people, about 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Families and caregivers often provide most of the daily support. When volunteers are well matched and dependable, they can give those families some breathing space. When volunteers are poorly matched or unreliable, they can add pressure.\r\n\r\nThe UN Volunteers programme presents volunteering as a two-way exchange that benefits communities and volunteers alike. Choosing a good fit is how both sides gain.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation supports people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, along with their families and caregivers. We encourage anyone interested in volunteering to think carefully about skills and time before committing.\r\n\r\nVisit the [volunteers page](/volunteers) to learn more, and use the [Get Involved](/get-involved) page when you are ready to start a conversation.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/collecting-event-feedback-without-exploiting-stories.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Collecting Event Feedback Without Exploiting People's Stories\"\r\nslug: collecting-event-feedback-without-exploiting-stories\r\ncategory: Events\r\nexcerpt: \"A values-based view on asking for feedback after community events: how to learn from people while protecting their dignity, privacy and choice.\"\r\ndescription: \"A values-based view on asking for feedback after community events: how to learn from people while protecting their dignity, privacy and choice.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [feedback, dignity, consent, events]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/collecting-event-feedback-without-exploiting-stories.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/collecting-event-feedback-without-exploiting-stories-thumb.webp\r\nicon: calendar-days\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Feedback exists to improve the service, not to collect emotional stories for marketing.\"\r\n  - \"People should choose whether to answer, what to share and how it is used.\"\r\n  - \"Ask about the event and how it worked, not about private pain or personal history.\"\r\n  - \"Protect privacy and never publish identifiable stories without informed, freely given permission.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Why is it risky to ask for stories?\"\r\n    a: \"Stories can be deeply personal, and people may feel pressure to share them in order to receive help or goodwill. Asking for feedback about the event, rather than personal hardship, keeps the focus on improvement and protects dignity.\"\r\n  - q: \"How do we ask children or people who need support to give feedback?\"\r\n    a: \"Use simple, friendly methods and involve a trusted parent, carer or advocate if the person wishes. Offer choices such as pictures, spoken answers or short forms, and never require a reply. Stop if the person seems uncomfortable.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can we use anonymous feedback?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Anonymous forms and comment boxes lower pressure and encourage honesty. Remove names and small details that could identify someone before sharing results, and report themes rather than individual comments.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n  - title: \"World report on disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564182\"\r\n---\r\nGood feedback helps an organisation improve, but it should never turn people's lives into content. Our view is simple: ask about the event, not about people's private pain, and always let people decide what to share. This piece is a values-based perspective, not legal advice.\r\n\r\n## Why do we want feedback in the first place?\r\n\r\nFeedback is how organisers learn whether an event was welcoming, clear and safe. It shows where the seating was awkward, the instructions were confusing or a quiet space was needed. Without it, planners repeat the same mistakes.\r\n\r\nThe principle of \"nothing about us without us\", closely linked to the UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), says people with disabilities should be involved in decisions that affect them. Asking for honest views is part of that respect. The UN Convention also emphasises dignity, autonomy and participation, and those values apply to how we ask as well as what we ask.\r\n\r\n## Where does feedback go wrong?\r\n\r\nThe problem begins when feedback drifts into story collecting. An organiser may want a moving quote for a report or social post. A person may feel they must share something painful to appear grateful, or to keep receiving help.\r\n\r\nThat imbalance matters. When people depend on a service, they may not feel free to say no. Stories collected under pressure can be unfair, and once shared publicly they are hard to take back.\r\n\r\nCommon warning signs include:\r\n\r\n- Asking people to describe their hardest moments for the sake of a report.\r\n- Suggesting that a story is the \"price\" of support.\r\n- Using a person's image or words in ways they did not expect.\r\n- Highlighting pity or dramatic \"before and after\" pictures.\r\n\r\n## What does respectful feedback look like?\r\n\r\n### Focus on the event, not the person\r\n\r\nAsk about things the organisers control. Was the information clear? Could you move around easily? Was there a place to rest? Did helpers feel approachable? These questions produce useful, practical answers without asking anyone to expose their private life.\r\n\r\n### Make it optional\r\n\r\nSay clearly that feedback is voluntary and that answering, or not answering, will have no effect on any support. Give people time. Some will prefer to reply later or at home.\r\n\r\n### Offer different ways to respond\r\n\r\nNot everyone finds writing easy. Provide choices:\r\n\r\n1. A short form with simple questions and a rating with pictures.\r\n2. A conversation with a trusted helper.\r\n3. A comment box that does not require a name.\r\n4. A way for a parent or carer to respond, if the person prefers.\r\n\r\n### Include people who are less often heard\r\n\r\nChildren, people with intellectual disability, and older people living with dementia may not be asked as often. Involve them in ways that suit them, with support from someone they trust, and pay attention to non-verbal signs of comfort or discomfort. Respect a \"no\" in any form.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Before you ask a question, ask yourself: would I be comfortable answering this in front of my neighbours? If not, do not ask it.\r\n\r\n## How should we handle privacy and consent?\r\n\r\nTreat feedback as personal information.\r\n\r\n- Collect only what you need, and explain why.\r\n- Store it safely and limit who can read it.\r\n- Remove names and identifying details when reporting results.\r\n- Report themes, such as \"more seating was requested\", not individual quotes, unless a person gives clear, informed permission.\r\n- Ask separately before using any words or images in public, and allow people to change their mind.\r\n\r\nOur article on [why consent matters in nonprofit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explores this in more depth, and our [privacy page](/privacy) explains how we think about personal information.\r\n\r\n## What should we do with feedback once we have it?\r\n\r\nFeedback that is collected and ignored is a form of disrespect. Read the responses, look for patterns and choose a few changes to make. Tell participants, in general terms, what you learned and what will change. This \"you said, we did\" approach builds trust and shows that people were heard.\r\n\r\nWhere feedback is difficult to hear, resist the urge to explain it away. Discomfort often points to something worth fixing. Our guide to [accessibility in community events](/blog/article-accessibility-universal-design-checklist-community-events) can help you turn comments into improvements.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania believes in rights, dignity and community inclusion. We think learning from people and protecting them are not opposites. To learn about our commitments, read about our [mission, vision and values](/about#mission).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/communication-tips-for-neurodivergent-children.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Communication Tips That Help Neurodivergent Children\"\r\nslug: communication-tips-for-neurodivergent-children\r\ncategory: Neuro-diversity\r\nexcerpt: \"Seven practical, respectful steps for parents, teachers and caregivers to communicate more clearly with autistic children and children with ADHD or Down syndrome.\"\r\ndescription: \"Seven practical, respectful steps for parents, teachers and caregivers to communicate more clearly with autistic children and children with ADHD or Down syndrome.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [communication, autism, parenting, how-to]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/communication-tips-for-neurodivergent-children.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/communication-tips-for-neurodivergent-children-thumb.webp\r\nicon: brain\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Clear, calm, simple communication helps many neurodivergent children feel safe and understood.\"\r\n  - \"Communication is more than speech. Gestures, pictures, objects and behaviour all carry meaning.\"\r\n  - \"Give time, show as well as tell, and prepare children for change.\"\r\n  - \"A speech-language therapist or other qualified professional can tailor advice to your child.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What if my child does not speak?\"\r\n    a: \"Many children communicate in other ways, such as gestures, pointing, pictures, sounds or devices. Respond to every attempt to communicate. A qualified speech-language therapist can advise on the best approach for your child.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should I stop my child from repeating words or phrases?\"\r\n    a: \"Repeating words or phrases can be a way of coping, practising language or communicating. Try to understand what it means for your child before trying to change it. A therapist can help you decide what support is useful.\"\r\n  - q: \"How do I help my child during a meltdown?\"\r\n    a: \"Stay calm, reduce noise and crowds, keep your child safe, and use few words. A meltdown is usually overwhelm, not misbehaviour. Talk about it later, once your child is calm. Seek professional advice if it happens often.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.cdc.gov/autism/index.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n---\r\nClear communication for neurodivergent children starts with slowing down, using simple words, and showing what you mean as well as saying it. Follow the steps below and treat every gesture, sound or picture as a real message. These are general tips, not therapy, and every child is different.\r\n\r\n## Before you begin: what is communication?\r\n\r\nTalking is only one form of communication. Children may point, lead you by the hand, draw, use pictures, sign, hum, or use behaviour to tell you something. When we accept all of these as real communication, children feel heard.\r\n\r\nThe tips below suit many autistic children and many children with ADHD or Down syndrome. Adjust them to the child in front of you.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: Get ready to connect\r\n\r\nBefore speaking, make sure your child can take in what you say.\r\n\r\n- Move to their level, so you are not standing over them.\r\n- Say their name, or gently signal that you want to talk.\r\n- Reduce background noise if you can. Turn off the radio or move away from the crowd.\r\n- Do not insist on eye contact. Many autistic children listen well while looking elsewhere.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Use short, clear words\r\n\r\nLong sentences can be hard to follow. Say what you mean directly.\r\n\r\n- Instead of \"Would you like to maybe start thinking about getting ready?\", say \"Shoes on, please.\"\r\n- Use the same words for the same routines, so they become familiar.\r\n- Say what to do, not only what not to do. \"Walk slowly\" is clearer than \"Don't run.\"\r\n- Be careful with jokes and sayings, which some children take literally.\r\n\r\n## Step 3: Show as well as tell\r\n\r\nMany children understand pictures, objects and actions better than words alone.\r\n\r\n1. Hold up the cup when you ask about water.\r\n2. Use simple drawings or photos for daily routines, such as wake up, wash, eat, school.\r\n3. Point, mime or demonstrate the action.\r\n4. Let your child show you as well, by pointing to a picture or object.\r\n\r\n## Step 4: Give time to answer\r\n\r\nSome children need extra time to process a question and form a reply. Ask once, then wait quietly. Count slowly in your head. If you repeat the question too fast, the child may need to start again.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Offer two choices, such as \"Rice or ugali?\" Choices are easier than open questions and give your child a sense of control.\r\n\r\n## Step 5: Prepare for change\r\n\r\nSudden changes can cause real distress. Help your child know what is coming.\r\n\r\n- Tell them the plan for the day, using pictures if helpful.\r\n- Give warnings before a change: \"Two more minutes, then we tidy.\"\r\n- Explain when something unexpected will happen, in calm and simple words.\r\n- Keep a familiar comfort item nearby.\r\n\r\n## Step 6: Respond to stress with calm\r\n\r\nWhen a child is overwhelmed, they may cry, shout, run, cover their ears or shut down. This is usually a sign the world feels too much, not bad behaviour.\r\n\r\n- Stay calm and lower your voice.\r\n- Move to a quieter place if possible.\r\n- Use very few words.\r\n- Make sure your child is safe.\r\n- Talk about what happened later, when everyone is calm.\r\n\r\nNotice patterns. If a certain time, place or sound leads to distress, you can adjust it next time. The [sensory-friendly checklist](/blog/article-sensory-friendly-home-and-community-checklist) in this series can help.\r\n\r\n## Step 7: Celebrate every attempt\r\n\r\nPraise effort, not only success. \"I like how you showed me the picture\" builds confidence. Follow your child's interests too. If they love cars, use cars to practise words, turns and play.\r\n\r\n## Things to avoid\r\n\r\nSome habits can make communication harder:\r\n\r\n- Shouting or repeating the same words louder.\r\n- Speaking about the child as if they cannot hear.\r\n- Comparing them with siblings or other children.\r\n- Forcing hugs, eye contact or speech.\r\n- Using threats or shame.\r\n\r\n## When to seek extra help\r\n\r\nIf you are worried about your child's speech, hearing or understanding, speak to a doctor or a qualified speech-language therapist. They can assess and suggest tools that suit your child. Do not wait until you feel sure. Early support is valuable, and asking is never a mistake.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania offers education and guidance for families and caregivers on neurological conditions, therapy exercises and specialised parenting. We share general information and encourage professional support where needed. Learn about our [Education and therapy guidance](/programs/program-education-therapy), our [Caregiver training](/programs/program-caregiver-training), or visit the [Neuro-diversity program](/programs/program-neurodiversity).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Turning Community Awareness Into Everyday Inclusion\"\r\nslug: community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion\r\ncategory: Inclusion\r\nexcerpt: \"Awareness is a start. This perspective explains how communities can move from knowing about disability to including people in daily life, work and belonging.\"\r\ndescription: \"Awareness is a start. This perspective explains how communities can move from knowing about disability to including people in daily life, work and belonging.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [inclusion, awareness, community, participation]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion-thumb.webp\r\nicon: megaphone\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Awareness tells people that disability exists; inclusion changes what happens at the school gate, the market, the workplace and the family table.\"\r\n  - \"Participation means a person has a real role and a real voice, not only a seat in the room.\"\r\n  - \"Small, repeated changes in habits and spaces usually matter more than one big campaign.\"\r\n  - \"Inclusion works best when people with disabilities and their families help shape decisions.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is the difference between awareness and inclusion?\"\r\n    a: \"Awareness means people know about disability and the barriers people face. Inclusion means those barriers are actually reduced, so people with disabilities take part in school, work, worship and community life with the same dignity as everyone else.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can a small community group really make a difference?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Many barriers are local: a step with no ramp, a meeting nobody explains, a friendship group that never invites. Small groups can change habits and spaces close to home, and those changes are felt every day.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who should lead inclusion work?\"\r\n    a: \"People with disabilities and their families should have a real say in what changes and how. Others can support, listen and remove barriers, but inclusion done without the people affected often misses what matters most to them.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and work\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Labour Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.ilo.org/global/topics/disability-and-work/lang--en/index.htm\"\r\n---\r\nAwareness is knowing that disability exists and that people face barriers. Inclusion is what happens next: those barriers get smaller, and people take part in daily life with the same dignity as everyone else. This article is a viewpoint on how communities can make that move.\r\n\r\n## Why is awareness not enough?\r\n\r\nAwareness days, posters and talks have value. They start conversations and break silence. But a person with a disability does not live inside a campaign. They live in a street, a classroom, a congregation and a household.\r\n\r\nIf a child understands that a classmate has a disability but the classroom still has no room for that classmate to take part, nothing has changed for the classmate. Knowing is the first step. Changing habits and spaces is the second, and it is the harder one.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, roughly 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. That is a large share of every community. Including them is not a special project. It is part of building a community that works for everyone.\r\n\r\n## What does everyday inclusion look like?\r\n\r\nInclusion is easiest to recognise in ordinary moments. It is less about big gestures and more about who is expected, who is asked and who is missed.\r\n\r\n- A neighbour is greeted by name, not spoken about over their head.\r\n- A meeting is explained in plain words, and questions are welcome.\r\n- A child with a disability is invited to the game, the prayer group or the birthday, and helped to join in if they want.\r\n- A family caring for someone at home is asked what would help, rather than told what they should do.\r\n- A path, doorway or seat is planned with different bodies in mind from the start.\r\n\r\nNone of these needs a large budget. They need attention and the habit of asking, \"Who might be left out here, and what would help?\"\r\n\r\n## Inclusion versus a seat in the room\r\n\r\nThere is a difference between being present and taking part. A person can sit in a room and still have no voice, no role and no one speaking to them. Real participation has three parts.\r\n\r\n### Access\r\n\r\nThe person can physically and practically get there, and can understand what is happening. That may mean a ramp, a quieter space, clear language or someone who explains.\r\n\r\n### Voice\r\n\r\nThe person is asked for their views and those views change decisions. Where a person cannot easily speak for themselves, the people who know them well are listened to, and the person's own signs and preferences are respected.\r\n\r\n### Contribution\r\n\r\nThe person is seen as someone who gives as well as receives. Every person has strengths, interests and something to offer, whether it is a skill, a sense of humour, a task they enjoy or simply being a good friend.\r\n\r\n## Who is missing from the conversation?\r\n\r\nToo often, decisions about people with disabilities are made without them. Families and caregivers are sometimes left out as well, even though they carry much of the daily work.\r\n\r\nOne helpful test for any community plan is to ask who was in the room when it was made. If people with disabilities, older people and caregivers were not, the plan probably has gaps. The disability rights movement has long used the phrase \"nothing about us without us\", and it is a good rule for any group that wants to include others.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on dignity, participation and inclusion. It gives communities a shared language for why this matters. You can read our plain-language guide in [the CRPD explainer](/blog/article-un-convention-rights-persons-with-disabilities-explained).\r\n\r\n## How do workplaces and livelihoods fit in?\r\n\r\nBelonging is not only social. Having meaningful work, or a way to earn, is part of independence and respect. The International Labour Organization's material on disability and work is a useful starting point for employers and community groups thinking about inclusive workplaces. It treats decent work for people with disabilities as a matter of rights and of good practice for everyone.\r\n\r\nFor a small business or an informal trade, inclusion may look simple: a clear description of tasks, a willingness to adjust how something is done, and support that matches the person rather than assumptions about them. Employers and trainers should always seek advice from qualified professionals and from the person themselves about what support works.\r\n\r\n## What can one community do this month?\r\n\r\nBig change is built from small, repeated steps. A group does not need to wait for funding or a formal plan.\r\n\r\n1. Choose one place where people gather, such as a school, a place of worship or a neighbourhood association.\r\n2. Ask people with disabilities and their families what gets in the way, and listen without defending.\r\n3. Pick one or two barriers that can be changed soon.\r\n4. Change them, then ask again whether it helped.\r\n5. Share what you learned so others can do the same.\r\n\r\nFor practical prompts, see our [inclusion checklist for schools, faith groups and neighbourhoods](/blog/article-inclusion-checklist-schools-faith-groups-neighbourhoods). For everyday words that respect people, see the [guide to respectful language](/blog/article-respectful-language-about-disability-guide).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, works toward a vision in which people with special needs and disabilities are valued equally and take part actively rather than being isolated. Community inclusion is one of our four pillars, alongside rights and dignity, empowerment and independence, and early support.\r\n\r\nYou can learn more about our [neuro-diversity awareness program](/programs/program-neurodiversity) and find ways to take part, respectfully and safely, on our [Get Involved page](/get-involved).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/csr-done-well-beyond-drop-in-projects.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Doing CSR Well: Why Steady Beats Drop-In Projects\"\r\nslug: csr-done-well-beyond-drop-in-projects\r\ncategory: Partnerships\r\nexcerpt: \"A perspective on corporate social responsibility: why patient, needs-led support does more good than one-day projects, and what to do instead.\"\r\ndescription: \"A perspective on corporate social responsibility: why patient, needs-led support does more good than one-day projects, and what to do instead.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [csr, partnerships, sustainability, inclusion]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/csr-done-well-beyond-drop-in-projects.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/csr-done-well-beyond-drop-in-projects-thumb.webp\r\nicon: handshake\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Drop-in projects often serve the giver's calendar more than the community's needs.\"\r\n  - \"Steady, needs-led support builds trust and lasting benefit.\"\r\n  - \"People with disabilities should shape the plan, not only receive it.\"\r\n  - \"Small, reliable commitments can be better than large, short-lived ones.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is corporate social responsibility?\"\r\n    a: \"It is the idea that a business should consider its effect on people and society, not only its profit. In practice it includes how a company treats staff, communities and the environment, and how it supports causes.\"\r\n  - q: \"Are one-day projects always bad?\"\r\n    a: \"No. A single activity can be welcome if the community asked for it and it is well prepared. The concern is when one-off events replace real listening or steady commitment.\"\r\n  - q: \"How can a company avoid making things worse?\"\r\n    a: \"Ask first, listen, start small, protect people's dignity and privacy, and commit for the long term. Be ready to change the plan if the people involved say it is not helping.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"World report on disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564182\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and work\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Labour Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.ilo.org/global/topics/disability-and-work/lang--en/index.htm\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nGood corporate social responsibility (CSR) is patient. It listens first, commits over time and lets the community lead. Short, drop-in projects can feel generous to the giver, but they often leave little behind. This piece offers a values-based view of how companies can do better.\r\n\r\n## What is a drop-in project?\r\n\r\nA drop-in project is a one-time activity planned mainly around the visitor's schedule. Think of a team arriving for a day, doing a task, taking photos and leaving. Nobody in such a story is acting badly. Most people involved want to help.\r\n\r\nThe problem is one of design. If the plan starts with what the company wants to do, rather than what the community needs, the result may be pleasant but shallow. This is an illustrative picture, not a description of any specific organisation.\r\n\r\n## Why can short-term help fall short?\r\n\r\nSupport for people with disabilities, older people and their families is rarely a single-event matter. Skills, confidence and routines build slowly. Caregivers need steady guidance. Children who benefit from predictable routines can find sudden visitors unsettling.\r\n\r\nA one-day visit may also create work for staff and caregivers, who must host, explain and supervise. If the visit has no follow-up, the effort can feel wasted on both sides.\r\n\r\n## What does the evidence and rights framework suggest?\r\n\r\nThe WHO [World report on disability](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564182) describes how barriers in services, attitudes and environments limit participation. Lasting change usually means tackling those barriers through sustained effort, not through occasional gestures.\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) puts participation and dignity at its centre. In practice, that suggests people with disabilities and their families should have a say in what support looks like, rather than being passive recipients.\r\n\r\n## What does steadier CSR look like?\r\n\r\n### Listen before you plan\r\n\r\nAsk the non-profit what would help, and ask how people with disabilities and caregivers see their own needs. Be prepared to hear that the answer is not what you expected.\r\n\r\n### Start small and stay\r\n\r\nA modest commitment that continues is more useful than a large one that ends. Reliability lets a non-profit plan. It also builds the trust on which better work depends.\r\n\r\n### Share skills, not only money\r\n\r\nBusinesses hold skills in finance, logistics, design, technology and human resources. Sharing these can strengthen an organisation in ways a single gift cannot. See our explainer on [ways organisations can partner](/blog/article-ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us).\r\n\r\n### Look at your own doors, too\r\n\r\nThe International Labour Organization's page on [disability and work](https://www.ilo.org/global/topics/disability-and-work/lang--en/index.htm) reminds us that inclusion also concerns workplaces. A company that supports disability causes while overlooking accessibility in its own hiring and premises is missing part of the picture.\r\n\r\n## How should we treat people in the process?\r\n\r\nDignity is not optional. Some simple habits help:\r\n\r\n- Do not ask people to perform gratitude for a camera.\r\n- Get informed consent before sharing any photo or story.\r\n- Describe people by their abilities and the support they use, not by pity.\r\n- Accept that some activities are better left unphotographed.\r\n\r\nOur legacy article on [why consent matters in nonprofit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explores this further.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Before a project, ask: \"If nobody ever saw this, would we still do it, and would it still help?\"\r\n\r\n## Is it ever right to do a one-off?\r\n\r\nYes, sometimes. A single event can be valuable when the community asked for it, when it is well prepared and when it fits within a longer relationship. The concern is not the length of an activity but its purpose and follow-through.\r\n\r\nA helpful test is whether the people the activity claims to serve would choose it. If you cannot tell, that is a sign to ask.\r\n\r\n## Measuring what matters\r\n\r\nCompanies often want numbers. But the clearest measure of good CSR is whether relationships improve and whether people served say the support is useful. Be wary of impressive figures that cannot be checked. Report what actually happened, including what you learned and what you would change.\r\n\r\nFor a first look at how to check that a partnership is sound, see our [partner due-diligence checklist](/blog/article-partner-due-diligence-checklist-both-sides).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nWe are a young non-profit, and we value partners who are willing to listen, start modestly and stay. If that sounds like your organisation, please [write to us](/contact). We would be glad to begin with a conversation about what would really help.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/disability-myths-and-facts.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Disability Myths and Facts: Clearing Up Common Misunderstandings\"\r\nslug: disability-myths-and-facts\r\ncategory: Inclusion\r\nexcerpt: \"Answers to common questions and myths about disability, from whether it is rare to whether people need pity, so communities can respond with respect.\"\r\ndescription: \"Answers to common questions and myths about disability, from whether it is rare to whether people need pity, so communities can respond with respect.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [myths, disability, stigma, awareness]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/disability-myths-and-facts.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/disability-myths-and-facts-thumb.webp\r\nicon: megaphone\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Disability is common: the WHO estimates about 1.3 billion people, around 16% of the world, experience significant disability.\"\r\n  - \"Disability is not a punishment, a contagion or a reason to hide someone away.\"\r\n  - \"People with disabilities have goals, feelings and abilities; support needs vary greatly.\"\r\n  - \"Myths cause harm mainly by lowering expectations and leaving people out.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Is disability rare?\"\r\n    a: \"No. The World Health Organization estimates that about 1.3 billion people, roughly 16% of the world's population, experience significant disability. It touches almost every family and community, and it can happen at any age.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can you catch a disability from someone?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Disability is not contagious. Conditions such as autism, Down syndrome and dementia cannot be passed by touch, sharing food or being near someone. Staying away from a person is never a safe or kind response.\"\r\n  - q: \"Where can I find reliable information?\"\r\n    a: \"Look for established sources such as the World Health Organization, UNICEF, the United Nations and national health services, and speak with qualified professionals about specific concerns. Avoid rumours and advice that promises quick cures.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n---\r\nMost myths about disability are simply untrue, and they lead to fear, hiding and lowered expectations. The short answers below explain what is known and why it matters for families and communities.\r\n\r\n## Myth: Disability is rare\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Disability is a normal part of human life. The World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, around 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Many disabilities are not visible, and people may acquire disabilities through illness, injury or ageing.\r\n\r\nThis matters because when we picture disability as rare, we plan schools, paths and services as though it were. When we plan for the reality, everyone benefits.\r\n\r\n## Myth: Disability is a punishment or a curse\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Disability is not a punishment, a curse or anyone's fault. Causes vary widely and are often not known. Down syndrome, for instance, is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21. It is not caused by anything a parent did or did not do.\r\n\r\nBlame hurts families twice: first by adding guilt, and second by pushing them to hide the person rather than seek support. A family that feels judged is less likely to ask for help.\r\n\r\n## Myth: You can catch it, so keep your distance\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Autism, Down syndrome, intellectual disability, ADHD and dementia are not contagious. A child with a disability does not put other children at risk. Playing, eating and learning together is safe.\r\n\r\nSome conditions do have health needs that call for care, and families should follow advice from qualified health workers. But a disability itself is never a reason for exclusion.\r\n\r\n## Myth: People with disabilities cannot learn, work or contribute\r\n\r\n**Fact:** People with disabilities learn, work, make friends, hold opinions and contribute to their communities. Support needs vary, sometimes greatly, and the right support makes a real difference.\r\n\r\nThe International Labour Organization treats decent work for people with disabilities as a matter of rights. Employers and community groups can ask what support helps a person do a task well rather than assuming what they cannot do.\r\n\r\n## Myth: Autism looks the same in everyone\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Autism is a spectrum. The WHO reports that [about 1 in 100 children worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism, and that support needs vary greatly. Some autistic people need substantial daily support. Others live independently. Many have strong interests and skills.\r\n\r\n\"If you've met one autistic person, you've met one autistic person\" is a helpful reminder. Avoid guessing what a person can or cannot do from a label. Anyone concerned about a child's development should seek a qualified assessment.\r\n\r\n## Myth: Older people with memory loss are just \"getting old\"\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Dementia is not a normal part of ageing. It is a group of conditions that affect memory, thinking and daily life. WHO reports that [more than 55 million people worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia) live with dementia, and Alzheimer's disease is the most common form.\r\n\r\nA person living with dementia still has feelings, history and dignity. If you notice changes in memory or behaviour, encourage a check with a doctor. Do not try to diagnose or treat at home.\r\n\r\n## Myth: People with disabilities need pity, or a cure\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Most people with disabilities want what others want: respect, friendship, purpose and a fair chance. Pity creates distance. Miracle stories create pressure. Neither is inclusion.\r\n\r\nSome people benefit from therapy, education support or assistive tools. That is different from a \"cure\" narrative. Be careful with anyone who promises miracle treatments, especially if they ask for money. Speak with a qualified health professional first.\r\n\r\n## Myth: Inclusion is too costly or too complicated\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Some changes do need investment, but many are simple: clear explanations, patience, welcoming attitudes and flexible ways of taking part. Planning for many kinds of people from the start is usually easier and cheaper than changing things later.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on dignity, non-discrimination, participation and accessibility. It treats inclusion as a matter of rights, not charity.\r\n\r\n## What can I do when I hear a myth?\r\n\r\nStay calm and share a fact, not a lecture. You might say, \"I read that about 1.3 billion people live with disability, so it is quite common.\" Suggest reliable sources. Most of all, model respect. Use the person's name and speak directly to them. Our [respectful language guide](/blog/article-respectful-language-about-disability-guide) offers wording you can use.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania is a non-profit based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam. We work for a community where people with disabilities are valued equally and where families and caregivers are supported, not blamed.\r\n\r\nLearn more about autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability through our [neuro-diversity awareness program](/programs/program-neurodiversity), or explore how to [get involved](/get-involved). You can also read about [moving from awareness to everyday inclusion](/blog/article-community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/doing-therapy-exercises-safely-at-home.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"How to Do Therapy Exercises Safely at Home\"\r\nslug: doing-therapy-exercises-safely-at-home\r\ncategory: Family guidance\r\nexcerpt: \"Simple steps for practising a therapist's exercises at home with your child safely, calmly and consistently, while always following the professional's plan.\"\r\ndescription: \"Simple steps for practising a therapist's exercises at home with your child safely, calmly and consistently, while always following the professional's plan.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [therapy, exercise, home-practice, safety, parenting]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/doing-therapy-exercises-safely-at-home.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/doing-therapy-exercises-safely-at-home-thumb.webp\r\nicon: book-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Home practice should only follow the plan given by your child's qualified therapist.\"\r\n  - \"Set up a safe space, keep sessions short and supervise closely at all times.\"\r\n  - \"Stop and contact the therapist if your child shows pain, distress or anything unexpected.\"\r\n  - \"Make practice positive, playful and predictable so your child feels safe.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Can I invent extra exercises for my child at home?\"\r\n    a: \"It is best not to. Exercises are chosen for a child's particular needs and health. Adding or changing them without advice may be unsafe or unhelpful. If you have ideas, share them with the therapist, who can adjust the plan.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if my child refuses to do the exercises?\"\r\n    a: \"Stay calm and do not force it. Try a different time, make it more playful, or reduce the length. Tell the therapist about the difficulty, because they can suggest adjustments that suit your child.\"\r\n  - q: \"How often should we practise?\"\r\n    a: \"Follow the schedule your therapist gives you. Regular, gentle practice usually works better than occasional long sessions, but the right amount depends on your child, so ask the professional to be specific.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Physical activity\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Children with disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n---\r\nPractising therapy exercises at home can help a child build skills, but only when it is safe and follows the plan of a qualified therapist. The steps below explain how to prepare, practise and stay alert to your child's comfort. If you are ever unsure, pause and ask the therapist.\r\n\r\n## Why does home practice matter?\r\n\r\nTherapy sessions are usually short and happen only from time to time. Daily life is where children live, play and learn. Gentle practice at home, in familiar surroundings, lets a child use new skills in real situations, with people who love them.\r\n\r\nHome practice does not replace the therapist. It is a partnership: the therapist designs and adjusts the plan, and the family carries it into everyday life.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization notes that regular physical activity is good for health, and that children and adolescents should average about 60 minutes of activity each day. Exercises prescribed for a child with a specific condition are different from general activity, so always follow your child's own plan.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: Be clear about the plan\r\n\r\nBefore you start at home, make sure you can answer these questions. If not, ask the therapist.\r\n\r\n- Which exercises or activities should we do?\r\n- How should each one look when done correctly?\r\n- How long, how often and at what time of day?\r\n- What equipment, if any, do we need?\r\n- What signs mean we should stop?\r\n- Who do I call with questions?\r\n\r\nAsk the therapist to demonstrate with your child and let you try while they watch. A short written or drawn note makes it easier to remember later.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Prepare a safe space\r\n\r\n- Choose a flat, clear area with enough room. Remove sharp objects, loose rugs and anything that could trip your child.\r\n- Use a soft, non-slip surface if your therapist recommends it.\r\n- Keep the area calm, with fewer distractions such as loud television.\r\n- Have water nearby, and make sure your child is comfortably dressed.\r\n- Never leave your child alone during exercises. An adult should be close enough to help at all times.\r\n\r\nAny activity involving water, climbing, stairs, wheels or balance needs especially close supervision, and only when the therapist has approved it.\r\n\r\n## Step 3: Get your child ready\r\n\r\nChildren take part more willingly when they feel safe and know what is coming.\r\n\r\n1. Tell your child what you will do, in simple words.\r\n2. Show a picture, toy or demonstration if that helps them understand.\r\n3. Choose a time when your child is rested and has eaten, not right when they are tired, hungry or upset.\r\n4. Offer a small choice, such as which exercise to start with, so they feel included.\r\n\r\n## Step 4: Practise gently and keep it short\r\n\r\n- Start slowly and follow the therapist's instructions exactly.\r\n- Use a calm, encouraging voice. Praise effort, not only success.\r\n- Keep sessions to the length recommended. It is fine to end early if your child is tired.\r\n- Take breaks when needed.\r\n- Make it playful where the therapist agrees, with songs, counting games or taking turns.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Progress is often slow and uneven. A hard day does not mean failure. Consistency and kindness matter more than perfection.\r\n\r\n## Step 5: Watch for warning signs\r\n\r\nStop the activity and contact your child's therapist or doctor if you notice any of the following:\r\n\r\n- Signs of pain, or your child says something hurts\r\n- Unusual tiredness, dizziness or breathing difficulty\r\n- Sudden change in colour, strength or behaviour\r\n- Strong distress that does not settle with comfort\r\n- Anything that worries you\r\n\r\nIf there is an emergency, seek urgent medical help immediately instead of waiting for a therapy appointment.\r\n\r\n## Step 6: Note what happens and share it\r\n\r\nKeep a simple notebook. After each session, write a few words:\r\n\r\n- What we did\r\n- What went well\r\n- What was hard\r\n- Any questions\r\n\r\nBring the notes to your next appointment. Your observations help the therapist adjust the plan to your child.\r\n\r\n## What should families avoid?\r\n\r\n- **Copying exercises from videos or other children.** Every child's needs differ.\r\n- **Pushing through distress.** Comfort and trust matter.\r\n- **Comparing progress with other children.** Each child develops in their own way.\r\n- **Changing medicines or treatments.** Only a doctor should advise on this.\r\n- **Doing it alone when you are exhausted.** Ask another trusted adult to share the routine.\r\n\r\n## Who can help with home practice?\r\n\r\nOther family members can learn the routine too. Grandparents, older siblings and neighbours may be able to support, as long as they understand the safety rules and follow the same plan. Share the note and show them the exercises together, ideally with the therapist's approval.\r\n\r\nThis guide is general education, not medical or clinical advice. Your child's qualified therapist or doctor knows their needs and is the right person to decide which exercises are safe.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania offers education and therapy guidance, including support for families with therapy exercises and specialised parenting. To learn more, visit the [Education and Therapy Guidance page](/programs/program-education-therapy). You may also find our article on [supporting families with consistent care routines](/blog/article-supporting-families-with-consistent-care-routines) helpful, and our [contact page](/contact) if you have questions.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/four-pillars-of-our-vision-in-daily-practice.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"The Four Pillars of Our Vision in Daily Practice\"\r\nslug: four-pillars-of-our-vision-in-daily-practice\r\ncategory: Foundation\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist showing what community inclusion, rights and dignity, independence and early support can look like in everyday life.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist showing what community inclusion, rights and dignity, independence and early support can look like in everyday life.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [vision, inclusion, dignity, early-support]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: galleryExtra1\r\nthumb: galleryExtra1\r\nicon: building-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Our vision rests on four pillars: community inclusion, rights and dignity, empowerment and independence, and early support.\"\r\n  - \"Each pillar can be turned into simple questions anyone can ask at home, at school or in the community.\"\r\n  - \"The pillars describe how we aim to work. They are not claims about results.\"\r\n  - \"Families should always involve qualified professionals for assessment and therapy decisions.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What are the four pillars of the Eagle's Wings vision?\"\r\n    a: \"They are community inclusion, rights and dignity, empowerment and independence, and early support. Together they describe a life where people with disabilities are active, respected, growing in independence and supported early.\"\r\n  - q: \"Are the pillars only for the Foundation's staff?\"\r\n    a: \"No. They are useful for anyone: families, teachers, neighbours and community leaders. The checklist in this article can be used at home or in a group without any special training.\"\r\n  - q: \"What does early support include?\"\r\n    a: \"It includes early intervention therapies, family counselling and caregiver education. A qualified professional should guide any assessment or therapy, and this article is educational only.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does independence mean doing everything alone?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Independence means having choices and the skills to take part in daily life, with support where needed. Most people, with or without disability, depend on others in some ways.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and work\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Labour Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.ilo.org/global/topics/disability-and-work/lang--en/index.htm\"\r\n  - title: \"Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n---\r\nThe Eagle's Wings vision rests on four pillars: community inclusion, rights and dignity, empowerment and independence, and early support. Each pillar is only useful if it shows up in ordinary days. This checklist turns them into questions you can ask yourself at home, in a classroom or in the neighbourhood.\r\n\r\nIt is a reflection tool, not an audit and not a scorecard. You do not need to tick every box. Use it to notice what is already going well and where to start.\r\n\r\n## Pillar one: is this person included in community life?\r\n\r\nCommunity inclusion means that people with disabilities are active and contributing members of their community, not isolated from it. In Tanzania, where extended family and neighbours are so important, this can be very practical.\r\n\r\nAsk yourself:\r\n\r\n- [ ] Is the person invited to family gatherings, celebrations and community activities?\r\n- [ ] Are there roles the person can take on, such as helping with a meal, greeting guests or looking after a plant?\r\n- [ ] Can the person reach the places where others gather, such as the home, school, place of worship or market?\r\n- [ ] Do the people around them talk *with* them, not only *about* them?\r\n- [ ] Are neighbours and relatives given honest, respectful information, so misunderstanding does not lead to avoidance?\r\n\r\n## Pillar two: are rights and dignity respected?\r\n\r\nRights and dignity mean that every person is treated as equal in value and has a voice in decisions about their life. The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination, participation and inclusion, accessibility and respect for difference.\r\n\r\nAsk yourself:\r\n\r\n- [ ] Do we ask the person what they want before deciding for them?\r\n- [ ] Do we use respectful words, such as \"person with a disability\", and avoid pity or labels?\r\n- [ ] Do we protect privacy, including personal care and health information?\r\n- [ ] Do we ask permission before sharing a photo or a story?\r\n- [ ] If someone is treated unfairly, is there a trusted adult they can tell?\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** A simple habit is to pause before helping and ask, \"Would you like some help with that?\" Then wait for the answer.\r\n\r\n## Pillar three: is independence growing?\r\n\r\nEmpowerment and independence include vocational training, independent-living skills and income. The aim is not to do everything alone. The aim is more choice and more skill over time.\r\n\r\nAsk yourself:\r\n\r\n- [ ] Is the person learning one small daily-living skill at a time, such as washing, tidying or preparing food, with patient practice?\r\n- [ ] Are tasks broken into steps and shown, not only explained?\r\n- [ ] For older youth and adults, are there chances to learn a trade or a useful skill?\r\n- [ ] Does the person have some say over how they spend their time and, where possible, their money?\r\n- [ ] Do we celebrate effort, not only perfect results?\r\n\r\nThe ILO recognises that people with disabilities have the right to decent work. Preparing for that starts long before adulthood, with confidence built in small everyday tasks. Our program on [life skills](/programs/program-life-skills) explores this idea for autistic young people.\r\n\r\n## Pillar four: is support early enough?\r\n\r\nEarly support includes early intervention therapies, family counselling and caregiver education. Getting help early is often easier than waiting, and it helps the whole family.\r\n\r\nAsk yourself:\r\n\r\n- [ ] If a child seems to be developing differently, have we sought an assessment from a qualified professional, such as a doctor or therapist?\r\n- [ ] Do parents and caregivers have a place to talk about worries and feelings?\r\n- [ ] Are caregivers learning how to support the person, rather than being left to guess?\r\n- [ ] Do we know the trusted health and social-care contacts in our area?\r\n\r\nPlease note that only qualified professionals can diagnose, recommend therapy or advise on medication. Nothing here replaces that.\r\n\r\n## How the four pillars work together\r\n\r\nThe pillars support one another. A child who receives early support is more likely to gain skills. A person with more skills can take part more fully. A community that includes people is more likely to respect their rights. And when dignity is respected, people feel safe enough to try new things.\r\n\r\n| Pillar | One everyday question |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Community inclusion | Who is missing from this gathering, and why? |\r\n| Rights and dignity | Did we ask the person first? |\r\n| Empowerment and independence | What is one small step towards doing this together? |\r\n| Early support | Who could we ask for guidance today? |\r\n\r\n## A note about honesty\r\n\r\nWe share these pillars as our approach and hope. We do not claim that they have produced particular results. They are the standards by which we hope to be judged.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nThese four pillars guide how we plan our work. Read more about them, together with our mission and values, on the [mission, vision and values](/about#mission) page. If you want to see how the pillars connect to our activities, our [programs](/programs) page is a good place to start.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/from-life-skills-to-work-and-earning-an-income.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"From Life Skills to Work and Earning an Income\"\r\nslug: from-life-skills-to-work-and-earning-an-income\r\ncategory: Life skills\r\nexcerpt: \"A values-based view of how everyday skills can lead toward meaningful work and income for people with disabilities, and what communities can do to help.\"\r\ndescription: \"A values-based view of how everyday skills can lead toward meaningful work and income for people with disabilities, and what communities can do to help.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [work, income, independence, inclusion, life-skills]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/from-life-skills-to-work-and-earning-an-income.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/from-life-skills-to-work-and-earning-an-income-thumb.webp\r\nicon: sparkles\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Everyday skills such as routine, teamwork and communication are early building blocks for work.\"\r\n  - \"Work can mean many things: employment, self-employment, family enterprise or supported activity.\"\r\n  - \"The ILO recognises disability and work as a topic of decent work and inclusion.\"\r\n  - \"Communities and employers play a role, and adjustments can be simple.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Can every person with a disability work?\"\r\n    a: \"People have different strengths and support needs. Many people with disabilities work, and others take part in different ways. The goal is choice, with the right support and adjustments, not pressure.\"\r\n  - q: \"Where do life skills fit into work?\"\r\n    a: \"Skills such as following a routine, using tools safely, communicating needs and working with others transfer to many settings. They are not the only ingredients, but they form a foundation.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is income the only measure of success?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Income supports choice, but belonging, purpose and contribution also matter. Each person and family can define what a good working life looks like.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and work\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Labour Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.ilo.org/global/topics/disability-and-work/lang--en/index.htm\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nWork is more than a wage. It gives people purpose, routine, connection and a chance to contribute. We believe the path from everyday life skills toward work and income should be open to people with disabilities, on their own terms and with the right support.\r\n\r\n## Why should we talk about work at all?\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, roughly 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. That is a very large group of people with talent, ideas and the wish to take part in community life.\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) recognises participation and inclusion as core principles. The International Labour Organization also has a topic area on disability and work. In short, the wish to earn a living, or to contribute through work, is not a special request. It is part of being a citizen.\r\n\r\nAt Eagle's Wings, our vision includes empowerment and independence: vocational training, independent-living skills and income. This article explains our thinking in general terms.\r\n\r\n## How do everyday skills connect to work?\r\n\r\nMany workplace abilities begin at home. A young person who waters plants each morning is learning routine and responsibility. Someone who helps fold laundry is practising sequencing and care. Carrying water, sweeping and cooking teach safe use of tools and physical stamina.\r\n\r\n| Everyday skill | Work-related foundation |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| Following a daily routine | Reliability and time awareness |\r\n| Doing a chore in steps | Following instructions and finishing tasks |\r\n| Asking for help | Communication with supervisors and colleagues |\r\n| Caring for a garden | Patience and responsibility for living things |\r\n| Tidying a shared space | Working alongside others |\r\n| Handling small purchases | Early money understanding |\r\n\r\nThese are not guarantees, and they are not a checklist that a person must complete before they are \"ready\". They are simply connections worth noticing. Our article on [building everyday independence](/blog/article-building-everyday-independence-one-skill-at-a-time) explores how such skills grow.\r\n\r\n## What does work look like in practice?\r\n\r\nWork can take many forms, and there is no single right answer.\r\n\r\n- **Employment:** a paid role with an employer, with agreed adjustments.\r\n- **Self-employment:** a small business, such as gardening, craft, tailoring or food preparation.\r\n- **Family enterprise:** taking a real part in a family shop, farm or workshop.\r\n- **Supported activity:** structured work with regular support, in a group or workplace setting.\r\n- **Volunteering and community roles:** contribution that builds skills and confidence.\r\n\r\nEvery option deserves respect. What matters is choice, safety, fair treatment and, where earning is possible, fair pay.\r\n\r\n## What stands in the way?\r\n\r\nBarriers are often outside the person. They include low expectations, lack of accessible transport or training, limited information for families, and fear among employers. When we treat disability as only a personal problem, we miss these barriers.\r\n\r\nSimple adjustments can help. Examples include clear written or pictorial instructions, a quiet place to work, predictable schedules, or extra time to learn a task. These are reasonable ideas, not legal advice or promises about local rules.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Being asked \"what helps you do your best work?\" is often more useful than any assumption about what a person cannot do.\r\n\r\n## What can families and communities do?\r\n\r\n1. Give young people real household and community roles early.\r\n2. Notice strengths and interests, and follow them.\r\n3. Speak with local businesses, faith groups and neighbours about inclusion.\r\n4. Help young people practise real tasks with real feedback.\r\n5. Involve the young person in every decision about their future.\r\n6. Seek advice from qualified professionals about training, assessment and safety.\r\n\r\nCommunities in Dar es Salaam and across Tanzania are rich in trades and small enterprises. Inclusion often begins with one neighbour or business owner willing to welcome a new person and learn together.\r\n\r\n## A word of caution\r\n\r\nWork should never be forced. Some people will not want to work, or may not be able to for health reasons. Some may need long-term support. Their dignity and value do not depend on earning. A responsible approach avoids pressure, avoids promises about jobs, and keeps the person's wellbeing first.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania sees life skills as one step in a longer journey toward independence, and we hold income and vocational training as part of our vision. Today our program work focuses on structured life-skills support, especially for young people with autism who are 12 and above. Read more on our [life skills program page](/programs/program-life-skills) or explore our [mission, vision and values](/about#mission).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/gentle-movement-at-home-for-older-adults.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Gentle Movement at Home for Older Adults: A Step-by-Step Guide\"\r\nslug: gentle-movement-at-home-for-older-adults\r\ncategory: \"Healthy ageing\"\r\nexcerpt: \"A simple, safety-first way to build gentle movement into an older person's day at home, guided by WHO activity advice and a check with a health professional.\"\r\ndescription: \"A simple, safety-first way to build gentle movement into an older person's day at home, guided by WHO activity advice and a check with a health professional.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [exercise, older-adults, balance, home-safety]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/gentle-movement-at-home-for-older-adults.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/gentle-movement-at-home-for-older-adults-thumb.webp\r\nicon: heart-pulse\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Check with a doctor, nurse or physiotherapist before starting, especially after illness or a fall.\"\r\n  - \"The WHO advises adults at least 150–300 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity per week, and older adults should add balance and strength work about 3 days a week.\"\r\n  - \"Start small, stay near a stable support, and stop if there is pain, dizziness or breathlessness.\"\r\n  - \"Making movement part of daily routine and doing it together helps it last.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How do I know if an exercise is safe?\"\r\n    a: \"No one can judge this from an article. A doctor, nurse or physiotherapist can advise on what suits the person's health. As a general rule, use a stable support, wear non-slip footwear and stop at once if there is pain, dizziness or unusual breathlessness.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the older person uses a wheelchair or walking aid?\"\r\n    a: \"Movement is still possible. The WHO says people living with disability should be as active as their abilities allow. A physiotherapist can suggest seated arm and leg movements and show how to use the aid safely.\"\r\n  - q: \"How much activity is enough?\"\r\n    a: \"The WHO recommends at least 150–300 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week for adults, plus balance and strength work for older adults about 3 days a week. Anyone unable to reach this should do what they safely can.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Physical activity\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity\"\r\n  - title: \"Ageing and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ageing-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nGentle movement at home can help an older person stay strong, steady and independent. Begin with a health check, choose safe and familiar movements, and build up slowly. This guide explains how, using activity advice from the World Health Organization (WHO).\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** This is general education, not medical advice. Please check with a doctor, nurse or physiotherapist before starting, especially if the person has a heart, lung, joint or balance problem, has recently been ill, or has had a fall.\r\n\r\n## Why does movement matter in older age?\r\n\r\nRegular movement supports muscles, bones, balance, sleep and mood. It also helps people keep doing daily tasks such as getting up from a chair, walking to the market or climbing steps. Being inactive for long periods makes these tasks harder over time.\r\n\r\nThe WHO says adults, including adults living with disability, should do at least [150–300 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) per week. Older adults should also add balance and strength activities, about 3 days a week. Not everyone can reach these amounts, and the WHO is clear that some activity is better than none.\r\n\r\n## What do you need before you begin?\r\n\r\nYou do not need equipment. Gather a few simple things:\r\n\r\n- A sturdy chair without wheels, placed against a wall.\r\n- A clear, dry, well-lit space with no loose mats or cables.\r\n- Closed, flat, non-slip shoes, or bare feet on a non-slippery floor if a professional has advised it.\r\n- A cup of water and a place to rest.\r\n- Another person nearby, especially at the start.\r\n\r\n## Step-by-step: a gentle routine\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Talk with a health professional\r\n\r\nAsk which movements are suitable and which to avoid. Mention any pain, dizziness, heart condition, joint replacement or medicine that may affect balance. Write down the advice.\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Warm up while seated\r\n\r\nSit tall in the chair. Slowly roll the shoulders, turn the head gently from side to side, circle the wrists and ankles, and open and close the hands. Breathe normally. Warming up prepares the body and lets you notice how the person feels that day.\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Walk or march\r\n\r\nWalking is a natural way to build the aerobic activity the WHO recommends. Walk around the house, along a veranda or in a safe yard. If walking is hard, march gently on the spot while seated. Aim for a pace where the person can still talk.\r\n\r\n### Step 4: Add strength\r\n\r\nStrength work can use everyday actions:\r\n\r\n1. Stand up from a chair and sit down slowly, using the arms if needed.\r\n2. Lift a light, familiar object, such as a small water bottle, to shoulder height while seated.\r\n3. Rise onto the toes while holding the back of the chair.\r\n4. Press the hands gently against the wall, as if doing a standing push-up.\r\n\r\nDo a few repeats and rest. Increase slowly over weeks, not days.\r\n\r\n### Step 5: Practise balance\r\n\r\nAlways hold a stable support and have someone close by.\r\n\r\n- Stand with feet together and hold on. Then let go with one finger only if it feels safe.\r\n- Shift weight slowly from one foot to the other.\r\n- Walk heel to toe along a wall, holding it lightly.\r\n\r\nBalance work is most useful for lowering the risk of falls, which is why the WHO includes it for older adults.\r\n\r\n### Step 6: Cool down and stretch gently\r\n\r\nFinish with slow walking or seated breathing. Stretch the calves, arms and back gently, without bouncing or forcing. Offer water.\r\n\r\n## When should you stop?\r\n\r\nStop and seek help if the person feels chest pain, sharp or new pain, dizziness, faintness, unusual breathlessness, or confusion. If it feels like an emergency, get medical help right away. Do not encourage anyone to push through discomfort.\r\n\r\n## How can you make it a habit?\r\n\r\nAttach movement to things that already happen. Stretch after morning tea. Walk after a meal. Put on music the person loves. Invite a grandchild or neighbour to join in, because company makes the routine more enjoyable and safer.\r\n\r\nCelebrate effort rather than performance. A person who moves a little each day is doing something worthwhile. If interest fades, change the activity. Gardening, sweeping the yard, and light dancing all count as movement.\r\n\r\nFor food that supports energy for activity, see our guide to [simple, affordable nutrition habits for older people](/blog/article-simple-affordable-nutrition-habits-for-older-people). For the wider picture, read [healthy ageing: nutrition, movement and meaningful activity](/blog/article-healthy-ageing-nutrition-movement-meaningful-activity).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania offers elderly care support that includes physical exercise guidance alongside nutrition and mental stimulation, in a way that respects each person's abilities and pace. Find out more on our [elderly care program](/programs/program-elderly-care) page, or [get in touch](/contact) with questions.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/getting-involved-with-eagles-wings-faq.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Getting Involved with Eagle's Wings: Your Questions Answered\"\r\nslug: getting-involved-with-eagles-wings-faq\r\ncategory: Foundation\r\nexcerpt: \"Answers to common questions about volunteering, donating, partnering and contacting Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, with links to the right pages.\"\r\ndescription: \"Answers to common questions about volunteering, donating, partnering and contacting Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, with links to the right pages.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [get-involved, volunteering, donate, contact]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/getting-involved-with-eagles-wings-faq.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/getting-involved-with-eagles-wings-faq-thumb.webp\r\nicon: building-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"There are several ways to take part: volunteering, donating, partnering, sharing what you learn, or simply getting in touch.\"\r\n  - \"The Get Involved, Volunteers, Donate and Contact pages hold the current details.\"\r\n  - \"We ask everyone to respect dignity, privacy, consent and safety.\"\r\n  - \"Nothing here is a promise of a particular outcome; please check the site pages for the latest information.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How can I get involved with Eagle's Wings?\"\r\n    a: \"You can volunteer, donate, explore partnership, or help spread respectful awareness. The Get Involved page gives an overview, and the Volunteers, Donate and Contact pages give more detail on each route.\"\r\n  - q: \"How do I contact the Foundation?\"\r\n    a: \"Use the Contact page on this website, call +255 749 044 723, or follow @Eaglewings.tz on Instagram. We are based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, Tanzania.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do I need special qualifications to volunteer?\"\r\n    a: \"Please read the Volunteers page for current information. In general, respect, reliability and willingness to follow safeguarding and consent guidance matter more than any single skill.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is my privacy protected if I contact you?\"\r\n    a: \"Please read the Privacy page to see how information is handled. Avoid sharing sensitive health details about another person in a first message.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"United Nations Volunteers\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations Volunteers\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unv.org/\"\r\n---\r\nYou can get involved with Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania by volunteering, donating, exploring a partnership, sharing respectful awareness, or simply getting in touch. This page answers the questions people ask most often and points you to the website pages that hold the up-to-date details.\r\n\r\nWe are a non-profit founded in 2025 and based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam. Our work supports people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and their families and caregivers.\r\n\r\n## What are the ways to get involved?\r\n\r\nThere is no single right way. Here is a simple overview.\r\n\r\n| Way to take part | What it involves | Where to start |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Volunteer | Giving time and care, following our guidance | [Volunteers](/volunteers) |\r\n| Donate | Supporting the work financially | [Donate](/donate) |\r\n| Partner | Working together as an organisation | [Contact](/contact) |\r\n| Learn and share | Reading, and sharing respectful information | [Blog](/blog) |\r\n| Ask a question | Getting in touch | [Contact](/contact) |\r\n\r\nThe [Get Involved](/get-involved) page brings these routes together.\r\n\r\n## Can I volunteer, and what will it be like?\r\n\r\nThe [Volunteers](/volunteers) page describes current opportunities and what we ask of volunteers. Please read it first, because information may change.\r\n\r\nA few principles apply to everyone who helps:\r\n\r\n- **Dignity first.** People are not \"cases\" or photo subjects. They are people with the right to be respected.\r\n- **Consent and privacy.** Ask before photographing or sharing anything, and follow what you are told.\r\n- **Safety.** Water and exercise activities need trained supervision. A volunteer should not take on tasks that need a professional.\r\n- **Reliability.** Turning up as agreed matters, especially to children and older people who value routine.\r\n\r\nOur related article, [responsible volunteering in community work](/blog/article-responsible-volunteering-in-community-work), explores this in more depth. The UN Volunteers programme also has useful general information about volunteering.\r\n\r\n## How can I donate?\r\n\r\nThe [Donate](/donate) page explains the current ways to give. If anything is unclear, please ask through the contact page before you give. We encourage you to expect honesty from any organisation you support, including us. We describe our values and approach rather than promising particular numbers or outcomes.\r\n\r\n## How can I contact the Foundation?\r\n\r\nYou have several options:\r\n\r\n- Use the [Contact](/contact) page on this website.\r\n- Call **+255 749 044 723**.\r\n- Follow **@Eaglewings.tz** on Instagram.\r\n- Visit us in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, Tanzania (please contact us first).\r\n\r\nPlease do not send private medical information about someone else in a first message. If you or a family member needs a diagnosis, treatment or urgent help, speak with a doctor, nurse, therapist or social worker.\r\n\r\n## What if I want to join as an organisation or partner?\r\n\r\nOrganisations are welcome to get in touch through the [Contact](/contact) page. We believe partnerships should share values: dignity, inclusion, transparency and safety. Our article on [responsible partnerships with the Foundation](/blog/article-responsible-partnerships-with-the-foundation) covers this in more detail.\r\n\r\n## Can I help without giving money or time?\r\n\r\nYes. Awareness matters. You can:\r\n\r\n- Learn about disability, autism and dementia from reliable sources such as the World Health Organization.\r\n- Use respectful language, such as \"person with a disability\" or \"person living with dementia\".\r\n- Invite a person with a disability to community events, and make sure they can take part comfortably.\r\n- Share our articles with a friend or relative who may find them useful.\r\n\r\nThe WHO estimates that [about 1.3 billion people](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health), or roughly 16% of the world's population, experience significant disability. In any community, someone near you is likely to be affected, directly or through family.\r\n\r\n## What if I have a question about careers or events?\r\n\r\nThe [Careers](/careers) page is the best place to look for current information. We only announce something there when it is confirmed. For everything else, our [FAQ](/faq) page may already answer your question.\r\n\r\n## What should I keep in mind before I reach out?\r\n\r\n- Be clear about what you can offer, and about what you are hoping to learn.\r\n- Be patient. We are a young foundation and want to reply carefully.\r\n- Respect privacy, including your own. See our [Privacy](/privacy) page.\r\n- Remember that our articles are educational, and are not medical, legal or clinical advice.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nWe welcome people who share our respect for dignity and inclusion. If you are ready to start, visit the [Get Involved](/get-involved) page, or read our [mission, vision and values](/about#mission) to see whether our purpose feels like a good fit.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/healthy-ageing-nutrition-movement-meaningful-activity.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Healthy Ageing: Nutrition, Movement and Meaningful Activity\"\r\nslug: healthy-ageing-nutrition-movement-meaningful-activity\r\ncategory: \"Healthy ageing\"\r\nexcerpt: \"Healthy ageing rests on three everyday habits: enough good food, regular gentle movement and activities that give life purpose. Here is how they work together.\"\r\ndescription: \"Healthy ageing rests on three everyday habits: enough good food, regular gentle movement and activities that give life purpose. Here is how they work together.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [healthy-ageing, nutrition, movement, older-people]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/healthy-ageing-nutrition-movement-meaningful-activity.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/healthy-ageing-nutrition-movement-meaningful-activity-thumb.webp\r\nicon: heart-pulse\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Healthy ageing is about staying able to do what matters to you, not about having no health conditions.\"\r\n  - \"Food, movement and meaningful activity support each other, so small steps in one area help the others.\"\r\n  - \"The WHO suggests older adults add balance and strength activities on about 3 days a week.\"\r\n  - \"Activities should fit the person's interests and abilities, and a health professional should guide anyone with medical concerns.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What does healthy ageing actually mean?\"\r\n    a: \"The WHO describes healthy ageing as developing and keeping the ability to do the things that matter to a person as they grow older. It is less about the absence of illness and more about staying capable, connected and included in daily life.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is it too late to start moving more in older age?\"\r\n    a: \"It is generally not too late to begin gentle activity. Start slowly, choose movements that feel safe, and speak with a doctor, nurse or physiotherapist first if you have a health condition or have been inactive for a long time.\"\r\n  - q: \"How can families support an older relative without taking over?\"\r\n    a: \"Ask what the person enjoys and wants to keep doing. Offer help with the difficult parts, such as shopping or transport, while leaving choices and familiar tasks with the older person. Support works best when it protects dignity.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do older people need special foods?\"\r\n    a: \"Most older people benefit from the same varied, mostly fresh diet described in WHO guidance. Some people have swallowing, dental or medical needs, so a health professional can advise on anything specific.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Ageing and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ageing-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Decade of Healthy Ageing\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/initiatives/decade-of-healthy-ageing\"\r\n  - title: \"Physical activity\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity\"\r\n---\r\nHealthy ageing means staying able to do the things that matter to you for as long as possible. Three habits support this more than most: eating well, moving regularly and taking part in activities that feel meaningful. None of them needs expensive equipment, and all of them can be adjusted to the person.\r\n\r\n## What is healthy ageing?\r\n\r\nMany people picture healthy ageing as living without illness. The World Health Organization (WHO) uses a broader idea. It focuses on functional ability, which means being able to move around, meet your own needs, keep learning, build relationships and contribute to your community.\r\n\r\nThis is an encouraging view. A person can live with a long-term health condition or a disability and still age well if they have the support, food, activity and company they need. The United Nations and the WHO have named 2021–2030 the [Decade of Healthy Ageing](https://www.who.int/initiatives/decade-of-healthy-ageing), a call for governments, communities and families to work together on this goal.\r\n\r\n## Why do food, movement and purpose belong together?\r\n\r\nEach habit supports the others.\r\n\r\n- Good food gives the body energy to move and the brain what it needs to stay alert.\r\n- Movement helps appetite, sleep, balance and mood.\r\n- Meaningful activity gives a reason to get up, to prepare a meal or to go for a walk.\r\n\r\nWhen one area slips, the others often follow. An older person who stops cooking may eat less well. Someone who stops visiting friends may move less. That is why looking at the whole day is more useful than treating each habit separately.\r\n\r\n## Nutrition: simple, varied and regular\r\n\r\nThe WHO healthy diet guidance encourages a variety of foods: vegetables and fruit, legumes such as beans and lentils, nuts, whole grains, and moderate amounts of animal foods. It also recommends limiting salt, sugar and unhealthy fats, and drinking enough safe water.\r\n\r\nFor older people, a few practical points matter:\r\n\r\n- **Regular meals.** Appetite can fall with age, so small, frequent meals may work better than a few large ones.\r\n- **Easy-to-eat textures.** If chewing or swallowing is hard, softer foods can help. Ask a dentist, doctor or nurse for advice, especially if the problem is new.\r\n- **Fluids.** Thirst can become less noticeable, so offering drinks through the day is a kind habit.\r\n- **Shared meals.** Eating with others often improves both appetite and mood.\r\n\r\nLocal, seasonal foods are often the most affordable and the most familiar, which makes them easier to enjoy.\r\n\r\n## Movement: gentle, regular and safe\r\n\r\nThe WHO physical-activity guidelines say adults, including adults living with disability, should do at least [150–300 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) per week. Older adults should also add balance and strength activities, about 3 days a week, to help prevent falls.\r\n\r\n\"Moderate\" means the person can talk but not sing. Walking, gentle dancing, gardening and light housework can all count. Where a person cannot do that much, the WHO advice is to be as active as their abilities and conditions allow, because some activity is better than none.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Check with a doctor, nurse or physiotherapist before starting a new exercise routine, especially after illness, a fall, or if the person has heart, joint or balance problems.\r\n\r\nOur companion article on [gentle movement at home for older adults](/blog/article-gentle-movement-at-home-for-older-adults) gives step-by-step ideas.\r\n\r\n## Meaningful activity: what gives the day shape?\r\n\r\nMeaningful activity is anything that gives a person a sense of purpose, identity or enjoyment. It is personal. For one person it may be tending a small garden. For another it may be telling stories to grandchildren, singing, sorting beans, praying with a community or advising younger relatives.\r\n\r\nGood questions to ask are:\r\n\r\n1. What did this person love doing before?\r\n2. What can they still do, with or without a little help?\r\n3. Who do they enjoy spending time with?\r\n\r\nTry to avoid taking over tasks because it is faster. Keeping a role, even a small one, protects dignity. You can read more about this in our piece on [keeping older people mentally stimulated and socially connected](/blog/article-keeping-older-people-mentally-stimulated-and-connected).\r\n\r\n## How can families and communities help?\r\n\r\nIn Dar es Salaam and across Tanzania, older people are often supported by extended family and neighbours. That closeness is a strength. A few ways to use it well:\r\n\r\n- Share the load, so one caregiver is not doing everything.\r\n- Invite the older person into family meals and decisions.\r\n- Keep walkways clear and well lit to reduce the risk of falls.\r\n- Notice changes in eating, mood, memory or mobility and encourage a check-up with a qualified health worker.\r\n\r\nCaregivers need care too. Rest, information and support make it easier to be patient and kind.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania supports older people, including those living with Alzheimer's and dementia, and their families. Our elderly care support focuses on nutrition, physical exercise and mental stimulation guidance, always with respect for the person's choices and abilities. To learn more, visit our [elderly care program](/programs/program-elderly-care) or [contact us](/contact).\r\n\r\nThis article is general education, not medical advice. Please consult a qualified health professional about any individual concern.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/how-a-partnership-starts-and-what-an-agreement-covers.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"How a Partnership Starts and What an Agreement Covers\"\r\nslug: how-a-partnership-starts-and-what-an-agreement-covers\r\ncategory: Partnerships\r\nexcerpt: \"A general how-to guide to starting a partnership with a non-profit: first conversations, shared goals and the topics a clear written agreement usually covers.\"\r\ndescription: \"A general how-to guide to starting a partnership with a non-profit: first conversations, shared goals and the topics a clear written agreement usually covers.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [partnerships, agreements, how-to, planning]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/how-a-partnership-starts-and-what-an-agreement-covers.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/how-a-partnership-starts-and-what-an-agreement-covers-thumb.webp\r\nicon: handshake\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Most partnerships begin with a conversation about needs, not a contract.\"\r\n  - \"A clear written agreement helps both sides remember what was agreed.\"\r\n  - \"Typical topics include purpose, roles, timing, money, communication, safeguarding and how to end.\"\r\n  - \"This guide is general. Binding agreements need qualified legal advice.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Do we need a written agreement?\"\r\n    a: \"Writing down what was agreed helps everyone remember and avoids misunderstandings. The form can be simple. For anything binding, or involving money or property, get advice from a qualified legal professional.\"\r\n  - q: \"How long does it take to start a partnership?\"\r\n    a: \"It varies. Trust and clarity take time, and rushing usually causes problems later. Allow room for conversations, questions and changes before anything is finalised.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can an agreement be changed later?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes, if both sides agree. Good agreements include a way to review progress and adjust, because needs and capacity can change.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nA partnership usually starts with a simple conversation about needs and purpose, moves through questions and planning, and is then written down in a clear agreement. This guide describes that path in general terms. It is not a description of any one organisation's process, and it is not legal advice.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: Clarify your own reasons\r\n\r\nBefore contacting a non-profit, be clear about why you want to partner. Do you want to share a skill? Support a particular kind of work? Involve your staff?\r\n\r\nWrite down what you can offer and what you cannot. Honesty here saves everyone time. If the main goal is publicity, it is better to say so early, so that it can be discussed openly.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Make a first contact\r\n\r\nReach out in a simple, respectful way. Introduce your organisation, explain your interest and ask whether a conversation would be welcome. Avoid arriving with a finished plan. A non-profit may have limited time, so be patient about replies.\r\n\r\nMany organisations list contact routes on a website. For Eagle's Wings Foundation, those are on the [Contact page](/contact).\r\n\r\n## Step 3: Listen to the needs\r\n\r\nThe most valuable early conversation is about what would help. Ask questions like:\r\n\r\n- What are your current priorities?\r\n- What is hardest to resource at the moment?\r\n- How do people with disabilities and caregivers shape your plans?\r\n- What should we avoid doing?\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) emphasises participation. A good partner keeps that in mind when listening.\r\n\r\n## Step 4: Check that you fit together\r\n\r\nBoth sides should look at values, safeguards and capacity. Our [partner due-diligence checklist](/blog/article-partner-due-diligence-checklist-both-sides) gives questions to work through. Take time. The World Health Organization estimates that [about 16% of people worldwide live with significant disability](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health), so many people may be affected by how well a partnership is built.\r\n\r\n## Step 5: Agree a small starting point\r\n\r\nRather than committing to everything, agree on a modest first step. This might be a short activity, a skills session or a limited contribution. It lets both sides learn how they work together before promising more.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** A gradual start is a strength, not a weakness. Trust is built through small things done reliably.\r\n\r\n## Step 6: Write down what was agreed\r\n\r\nA written agreement records shared understanding. It can be formal or simple, depending on the size and nature of the partnership. Topics that agreements commonly cover, in general terms, include:\r\n\r\n1. **Purpose:** what the partnership aims to achieve.\r\n2. **Roles:** who does what, and who decides.\r\n3. **Contribution:** what each side offers, whether goods, skills, time or funds.\r\n4. **Timing:** start points, review points and how long it lasts.\r\n5. **Communication:** contact people, how often you will talk and how you will handle disagreements.\r\n6. **Safeguarding and consent:** expectations for protecting people, and for photos, stories and personal information.\r\n7. **Use of names and images:** how each side's name and materials may be used, and who approves this.\r\n8. **Reporting:** what will be shared and in what form, in the spirit of openness supported by initiatives such as the [IATI Standard](https://iatistandard.org/en/).\r\n9. **Review and ending:** how to check progress, make changes or bring the partnership to a respectful close.\r\n\r\nYour own agreement may include more or fewer points. The details of any legal terms depend on the parties and the place, so please take advice from a qualified legal professional before signing anything binding.\r\n\r\n## Step 7: Start, review and adjust\r\n\r\nOnce underway, check in regularly. Ask what is working, what is difficult and what should change. Be ready to adapt. If something is not helping the people it is meant to serve, be willing to stop or rethink.\r\n\r\nKeep records of what happens, and report honestly. Our article on [transparent reporting as programs grow](/blog/article-transparent-reporting-as-programs-grow) explains why this matters.\r\n\r\n## What if it does not go ahead?\r\n\r\nSometimes the right outcome is that a partnership is not the right fit. That is fine. A respectful conversation that ends without an agreement is better than an uneasy agreement that causes harm. You may find another way to help later.\r\n\r\nFor a wider look at the kinds of contribution you might discuss, see [ways organisations can partner](/blog/article-ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nThis guide is general and describes how many partnerships begin. It does not set out any specific process or terms of ours. If you would like to explore working together, please [contact us](/contact). We will start by listening.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/how-five-program-areas-fit-together.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"How Our Five Program Areas Fit Together\"\r\nslug: how-five-program-areas-fit-together\r\ncategory: Foundation\r\nexcerpt: \"A simple guide to the five Eagle's Wings program areas, how they connect, and why a young foundation chooses to start focused.\"\r\ndescription: \"A simple guide to the five Eagle's Wings program areas, how they connect, and why a young foundation chooses to start focused.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [programs, caregivers, life-skills, elderly-care]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/how-five-program-areas-fit-together.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/how-five-program-areas-fit-together-thumb.webp\r\nicon: building-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Eagle's Wings has five program areas: neuro-diversity awareness, elderly care support, education and therapy guidance, life skills and caregiver training.\"\r\n  - \"The areas overlap, because a person's needs, family and community are connected.\"\r\n  - \"We start focused so that quality, safety and honesty come before size.\"\r\n  - \"Program information here is educational and does not replace professional advice.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What are the five program areas?\"\r\n    a: \"Neuro-diversity awareness, elderly care support, education and therapy guidance, life skills programs and caregiver training. Each has its own page on this website with more detail.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do I need to choose only one program area?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Many needs overlap. A family might find that learning about a condition, practising a life skill and receiving caregiver guidance all matter. Start with the area closest to your question.\"\r\n  - q: \"Are the programs medical treatment?\"\r\n    a: \"No. They offer education and guidance. Diagnosis, therapy plans and medication decisions belong with qualified professionals such as doctors, therapists and nurses.\"\r\n  - q: \"Why does the Foundation start focused?\"\r\n    a: \"Doing fewer things well is safer and more honest than spreading thin. It lets a young foundation listen, learn and improve before it takes on more.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia\"\r\n---\r\nEagle's Wings has five program areas: neuro-diversity awareness, elderly care support, education and therapy guidance, life skills, and caregiver training. They are designed to fit together, because a person's needs, their family and their community are all connected. This guide shows, step by step, how to find the area that is closest to your question.\r\n\r\nThe information is educational. It is not medical, legal or clinical advice. Please speak with a qualified professional about diagnosis, treatment or medication.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: Start with the person and the question\r\n\r\nBefore looking at any list, describe what you hope to understand or improve. It might be a question about how a child communicates. It might be worry about an older relative's memory, a wish to help a young person become more independent, or a need for your own support as a caregiver.\r\n\r\nWriting down the question in a sentence helps you pick the right doorway. Keep the person's strengths in mind too, not only the difficulties.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Meet the five program areas\r\n\r\nHere is a quick overview.\r\n\r\n1. **[Neuro-diversity awareness](/programs/program-neurodiversity)** covers Autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability. It focuses on understanding, respect and reducing stigma.\r\n2. **[Elderly care support](/programs/program-elderly-care)** offers guidance on nutrition, physical exercise and mental stimulation for older people, including those living with Alzheimer's and dementia.\r\n3. **[Education and therapy guidance](/programs/program-education-therapy)** explains neurological conditions, therapy exercises and specialised parenting in plain language.\r\n4. **[Life skills programs](/programs/program-life-skills)** give structured life-skills support for children with autism, especially young people 12 and above.\r\n5. **[Caregiver training](/programs/program-caregiver-training)** helps caregivers build confidence and look after their own wellbeing.\r\n\r\n## Step 3: See where they overlap\r\n\r\nThe areas are not separate boxes. Here are some generic examples, offered only as illustrations.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** These are imagined examples, not real people. Imagine a family with an autistic teenager. They might read about autism in the awareness area, practise cooking steps in the life-skills area, and join caregiver training to share worries with others.\r\n>\r\n> Imagine an adult child caring for a parent living with dementia. They might use elderly care guidance for nutrition and gentle movement, and caregiver training for their own rest and stress.\r\n\r\nThe WHO notes that [more than 55 million people worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia) live with dementia, and that [about 1 in 100 children](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism. These figures show how many families may be looking for understanding, and why connected guidance can be useful.\r\n\r\n## Step 4: Choose one small next step\r\n\r\nYou do not need to master everything at once. Pick one action:\r\n\r\n- Read the page for the program area closest to your question.\r\n- Write down two questions to bring to a doctor or therapist.\r\n- Try one small change at home, such as a simple daily routine.\r\n- Contact us through our [contact page](/contact) if you have a question about the Foundation.\r\n\r\nPhysical activity of any kind should be supervised and suited to the person. Water activities in particular need close supervision.\r\n\r\n## Step 5: Involve professionals where it matters\r\n\r\nOur program areas offer education and guidance, not diagnosis or treatment. If you are worried about a child's development, an older person's memory or a change in health, please see a qualified professional. We would rather you ask early than wait.\r\n\r\n## Why do we start focused?\r\n\r\nIt might seem that a foundation should offer everything at once. We take a different view. Eagle's Wings was founded in 2025, and we believe that a young organisation earns trust by doing a limited number of things carefully.\r\n\r\nStarting focused means:\r\n\r\n- **Quality before quantity.** Each area is prepared properly before we stretch further.\r\n- **Safety first.** Activities such as swimming and exercise need trained supervision.\r\n- **Promises we can keep.** We say what we do, and we do not claim what we cannot show.\r\n- **Room to listen.** We can adapt to what families and caregivers tell us.\r\n\r\nWe explain this thinking further in [how Eagle's Wings approaches responsible program growth](/blog/article-responsible-program-growth).\r\n\r\n## Where the areas meet our vision\r\n\r\nEach area supports one or more of the four pillars in our vision: community inclusion, rights and dignity, empowerment and independence, and early support. Awareness supports inclusion and dignity. Life skills support independence. Education, therapy guidance and caregiver training support early support. See [the four pillars in daily practice](/blog/article-four-pillars-of-our-vision-in-daily-practice) for a closer look.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nThe five program areas are five doors into one house. You can enter through whichever one matches your question, and you will find the others connected inside. Browse them all on our [programs](/programs) page.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/how-to-raise-a-safeguarding-concern-safely.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"How to Raise a Safeguarding Concern Safely\"\r\nslug: how-to-raise-a-safeguarding-concern-safely\r\ncategory: Safeguarding\r\nexcerpt: \"Answers to common questions about what to do if you are worried about a child, older person or person with a disability, and how to speak up safely.\"\r\ndescription: \"Answers to common questions about what to do if you are worried about a child, older person or person with a disability, and how to speak up safely.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [safeguarding, concerns, reporting, protection]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/how-to-raise-a-safeguarding-concern-safely.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/how-to-raise-a-safeguarding-concern-safely-thumb.webp\r\nicon: shield-check\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"If you are worried about someone's safety, speak up early. You do not need proof.\"\r\n  - \"Tell the organisation's leadership, and where needed the relevant local authorities.\"\r\n  - \"If someone is in immediate danger, get them to safety and call emergency services.\"\r\n  - \"Do not investigate, confront anyone or promise to keep a secret.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What counts as a safeguarding concern?\"\r\n    a: \"Anything that makes you worry a child, older person or person with a disability may be harmed, neglected, exploited or put at risk. It may be something you saw, something you were told, or a pattern that has troubled you over time.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do I need proof before I speak up?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Your role is to share what you noticed, not to prove it. Investigating is the job of leadership, qualified professionals and the relevant local authorities, so please do not try to gather evidence on your own.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who should I tell?\"\r\n    a: \"Start with the organisation's leadership or its named welfare contact. Where a person may be at risk of harm, the relevant local authorities should also be involved. Ask leadership or seek qualified advice about the correct route where you live.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if someone is in immediate danger?\"\r\n    a: \"Put their safety first. Move them away from the danger if you can do so safely, and call emergency services. Then tell the organisation's leadership as soon as possible so that proper follow-up can happen.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the concern is about a leader or a helper?\"\r\n    a: \"Do not stay silent because of who it is. Take it to another leader or a person above them, and, where needed, to the relevant local authorities. Seek qualified advice about the right process.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Child maltreatment\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/child-maltreatment\"\r\n  - title: \"Violence against children\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nIf you are worried about a child, an older person or a person with a disability, speak up early. Tell the organisation's leadership, and where a person may be at risk, the relevant local authorities. If someone is in immediate danger, get them to safety and call emergency services. This FAQ answers common questions. It is general guidance, not legal advice, and it does not replace advice from qualified professionals.\r\n\r\n## Why is it hard to speak up?\r\n\r\nMany people hesitate. They fear being wrong, causing trouble, upsetting a colleague or not being believed. Some worry they will make things worse for the person they want to protect.\r\n\r\nThese worries are natural. But the World Health Organization explains that child maltreatment and violence against children can seriously harm health and wellbeing, and that harm can continue if nobody acts. A concern shared early gives others a chance to help. A concern kept quiet leaves a person on their own.\r\n\r\n## What counts as a concern?\r\n\r\nYou do not need to be certain. A concern can be:\r\n\r\n- something you saw or heard\r\n- something a person told you\r\n- a change in someone's behaviour or mood that troubles you\r\n- unsafe practice by a helper, such as being alone and unobserved with a child\r\n- a person who appears to be neglected, exploited or afraid\r\n\r\nIf you are unsure whether it is serious enough, share it anyway. Leadership can decide what happens next.\r\n\r\n## What should I do first?\r\n\r\n### If someone is in immediate danger\r\n\r\nTheir safety comes first. If you can do so safely, move them away from the danger, then call emergency services. Tell the organisation's leadership as soon as you can.\r\n\r\n### If it is not an emergency\r\n\r\n1. Stay calm and make sure the person is safe and comfortable.\r\n2. Write down what you saw or heard, as soon as possible, in plain and factual words. Note the date, time and place.\r\n3. Report it to the organisation's leadership or its named welfare contact, as soon as you can.\r\n4. Keep the matter private. Share it only with those who need to know.\r\n\r\n## What should I say if a child or person tells me something?\r\n\r\nListen calmly. Let them speak in their own words and at their own pace. Then:\r\n\r\n- Thank them for telling you, and tell them they did the right thing.\r\n- Do not ask leading questions, or press for details.\r\n- Do not promise to keep it secret. Explain kindly that you may need to tell someone whose job is to help keep them safe.\r\n- Do not confront the person they are talking about.\r\n- Tell the responsible person and write down what was said.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Your job is to listen and pass it on. Working out what has happened is for leadership, qualified professionals and the relevant local authorities.\r\n\r\n## Who should I tell?\r\n\r\nStart with the organisation's leadership or its named welfare contact. Every organisation working with children, older people or people with disabilities should have one, and should make sure helpers and families know who they are.\r\n\r\nWhere a person may be at risk of harm, the relevant local authorities should also be involved. This is normally handled by the organisation's leadership, but if you are worried that nothing will be done, you may need to raise it yourself. Because the correct route varies by place, ask leadership for their process or seek qualified advice about how it works where you live. We do not list specific offices or numbers here because they should be confirmed locally.\r\n\r\n## What if the concern is about a leader or a helper?\r\n\r\nDo not stay silent because of who it is. If the concern involves the person you would normally tell, take it to another leader, someone above them, or where needed the relevant local authorities. Trust in an organisation depends on nobody being above scrutiny.\r\n\r\n## Will I get in trouble for speaking up?\r\n\r\nA good organisation welcomes concerns raised honestly and in good faith, and protects the person who raised them. You should not be punished for speaking up. If you do not feel safe doing so inside the organisation, seek qualified advice about your options.\r\n\r\n## What happens after I report?\r\n\r\nLeadership should acknowledge your concern, decide on next steps, involve the relevant authorities where needed, and keep records securely. You may not be told all the details, because of privacy. That does not mean nothing is being done.\r\n\r\n## How can organisations make it easier?\r\n\r\n- Name a welfare contact and share their details.\r\n- Explain the process to helpers, families and participants.\r\n- Make it clear that concerns are welcome and taken seriously.\r\n- Keep records private and secure.\r\n- Review the process after any concern.\r\n\r\nRelated reading includes [safeguarding basics for community organisations](/blog/article-safeguarding-basics-for-community-organisations) and [keeping children and vulnerable adults safe during activities](/blog/article-keeping-children-and-vulnerable-adults-safe-during-activities).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania works with people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and their families and caregivers. If you have a worry connected to our activities, please [contact us](/contact) and speak to our leadership. You can also read how personal information is handled on our [privacy page](/privacy).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/how-to-read-a-nonprofit-report-as-a-donor.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"How to Read a Non-Profit Report and Ask Good Questions\"\r\nslug: how-to-read-a-nonprofit-report-as-a-donor\r\ncategory: Transparency\r\nexcerpt: \"A step-by-step guide for donors on reading a non-profit's report calmly, spotting vague claims and asking respectful, useful questions.\"\r\ndescription: \"A step-by-step guide for donors on reading a non-profit's report calmly, spotting vague claims and asking respectful, useful questions.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [donors, reports, questions, accountability]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/how-to-read-a-nonprofit-report-as-a-donor.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/how-to-read-a-nonprofit-report-as-a-donor-thumb.webp\r\nicon: file-check-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Read for clarity: what was done, for whom, and how it is known.\"\r\n  - \"Be careful with big totals that come with no explanation.\"\r\n  - \"Good questions are respectful, specific and open.\"\r\n  - \"An honest answer such as we do not know yet is a good sign.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Is it rude to ask a non-profit questions about its report?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Good organisations welcome questions because they show that supporters care. Ask politely, be specific and give the organisation time to answer. How it responds also tells you something about its openness.\"\r\n  - q: \"What is a red flag in a report?\"\r\n    a: \"Common warning signs include only good news, big claims with no source, no mention of limits, unclear use of money, and stories that expose individuals. One sign alone is a reason to ask, not to accuse.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do I need accounting skills to read a report?\"\r\n    a: \"No. You mainly need patience. Look for whether the money story is clear: where it came from, how it was used and who checked it. If you cannot follow it, that is a fair question to ask.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if a young organisation has little to report?\"\r\n    a: \"That is normal. A young organisation can still be transparent by explaining what it plans, how it will measure its work and what it does not yet know. Be cautious of any group that claims large results with no evidence.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"World report on disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564182\"\r\n---\r\nTo read a non-profit's report well, look for what was done, for whom, how it is known, and what was hard. Then ask short, respectful questions about anything unclear. You do not need special training, only patience and curiosity.\r\n\r\nHere is a simple process any donor can follow.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: Read the purpose first\r\n\r\nStart with the mission. Can you say in one sentence who the organisation serves and why? If you cannot, note that. A report that starts with vague words such as \"transforming lives\" without saying how is asking you to trust before you understand.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Look at what was actually done\r\n\r\nFind the section on activities. Ask yourself:\r\n\r\n- Are the activities described in ordinary words?\r\n- Do I understand what a session, service or visit looks like?\r\n- Is it clear who the work is for?\r\n\r\nBe careful when a report lists outcomes without describing the work that led to them. The work is the part you are supporting.\r\n\r\n## Step 3: Check the numbers\r\n\r\nNumbers are useful, but only when explained. For each one, ask these questions.\r\n\r\n1. What was counted?\r\n2. Who counted it, and how?\r\n3. Over what period?\r\n4. What does the number not tell us?\r\n\r\nWatch for global figures used as if they were the organisation's own results. For example, the World Health Organization estimates that [about 1 in 100 children worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism. That number describes the world. It is not the achievement of any local group.\r\n\r\n### Small numbers are not a weakness\r\n\r\nA small, clearly explained number is often more useful than a big, vague one. Be wary of round, impressive totals that come with no detail.\r\n\r\n## Step 4: Look for learning and limits\r\n\r\nFind where the report says what did not go well. Good reports include this. They also say what will change.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Search the report for words like \"challenge\", \"learned\", \"changed\" and \"limit\". If none appear, that itself is worth a question.\r\n\r\n## Step 5: Follow the money\r\n\r\nYou do not need to be an accountant. Look for a simple story.\r\n\r\n- Where did funds come from?\r\n- How were they used, in categories you can understand?\r\n- Was anything held back for later?\r\n- Did anyone outside the organisation check the accounts?\r\n\r\nIf the money story is missing or unclear, ask for it. A fair organisation will explain what it can, and will say what it cannot yet do.\r\n\r\n## Step 6: Check how people are treated\r\n\r\nA report about people with disabilities or older people should respect them. Look for consent, privacy and dignified language.\r\n\r\nBe cautious if you see:\r\n\r\n- Full names or faces of children with no mention of consent\r\n- Pity language such as \"suffering\" or \"helpless\"\r\n- Miracle stories that make people sound like symbols\r\n\r\nThe [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) is built on dignity, autonomy and participation. Reports should reflect that spirit. See also our article on [why consent matters in non-profit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling).\r\n\r\n## Step 7: Ask good questions\r\n\r\nGood questions are specific, calm and open. Compare these two.\r\n\r\n| Less helpful | More helpful |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| \"Is this all true?\" | \"How did you decide what to count in this section?\" |\r\n| \"Where does the money go?\" | \"Could you explain how the funds in this category were used?\" |\r\n| \"Why so little?\" | \"What do you hope to be able to report on next time?\" |\r\n\r\nSend your questions in writing if you can, so the answer is clear. Allow time for a reply. Then note the answer, and note the tone.\r\n\r\n## How should you read the answers?\r\n\r\nAn honest answer may be \"we do not know yet\" or \"we made a mistake and this is what we did\". These are good signs. Be cautious of answers that change the subject, become defensive or offer only more praise.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nThe Foundation was founded in 2025 and does not currently publish numeric results. Its [Impact page](/impact) lists four things it intends to report on: reach, learning, practice and experience. Reporting will be shared on this website as it becomes available. Please read our work with the questions above, and [contact us](/contact) with any question you have.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/hygiene-routines-with-dignity-and-privacy-checklist.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"A Checklist for Hygiene Routines with Dignity and Privacy\"\r\nslug: hygiene-routines-with-dignity-and-privacy-checklist\r\ncategory: Life skills\r\nexcerpt: \"A respectful checklist for teaching personal hygiene routines to young people with disabilities, with privacy, choice and safety at the centre.\"\r\ndescription: \"A respectful checklist for teaching personal hygiene routines to young people with disabilities, with privacy, choice and safety at the centre.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [hygiene, dignity, privacy, self-care, life-skills]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: stockHandWashing\r\nthumb: stockHandWashingThumb\r\nicon: sparkles\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Hygiene is taught best when privacy, choice and respect come first.\"\r\n  - \"Use simple, ordered steps and consistent routines that fit the household.\"\r\n  - \"Support should be gradual and matched to the person, and never shaming.\"\r\n  - \"Talk with a health professional about medical, skin or dental concerns.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How do I keep a young person's dignity when helping with personal care?\"\r\n    a: \"Explain what you will do before you do it, keep the door closed, cover the body where possible, and use calm, respectful words. Let the person do as much as they can and ask permission before touching.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who should help with private care?\"\r\n    a: \"Ideally a trusted adult the young person and family agree on, and who is comfortable in the role. Families may also discuss preferences with a professional. Never leave a young person alone with someone unsafe.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the person dislikes water, soap or brushing?\"\r\n    a: \"Sensory discomfort is common. Try changing temperature, texture or product, and introduce the step slowly. If it stays difficult, ask a therapist or health professional for guidance.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Oral health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/oral-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nGood hygiene routines protect health and support confidence, but how we teach them matters as much as what we teach. Use this checklist to build routines that keep privacy, choice and dignity at the centre. It is general education, not medical advice.\r\n\r\n## Why does dignity matter in personal care?\r\n\r\nPersonal care is intimate. A young person who feels embarrassed or rushed may resist, even when they understand the task. Dignity is also a matter of rights. The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) is built on respect for autonomy and dignity, and everyday care is where those values become real.\r\n\r\nOlder children and teenagers, including those aged 12 and above, are also growing and changing. They deserve accurate, calm information and appropriate privacy.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 1: Before you start\r\n\r\n- [ ] Choose a calm time, not when the person is hungry, tired or upset.\r\n- [ ] Prepare everything you need beforehand: soap, towel, clean clothes, toothbrush.\r\n- [ ] Check water temperature with your own hand.\r\n- [ ] Choose a private space and close the door or curtain.\r\n- [ ] Tell the person what will happen, using words, pictures or gestures they understand.\r\n- [ ] Ask who they would like to help, if a choice is possible.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 2: During the routine\r\n\r\n- [ ] Let the person do as much as they can by themselves.\r\n- [ ] Offer choices, such as which soap or which towel.\r\n- [ ] Ask before touching, and explain each step as you go.\r\n- [ ] Cover parts of the body that are not being washed.\r\n- [ ] Use neutral, respectful words, and never joke about the body.\r\n- [ ] Watch for signs of discomfort, such as pulling away or distress, and pause if needed.\r\n- [ ] Keep the pace slow and steady.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Some people find water, textures or smells overwhelming. This is a sensory difference, not stubbornness. Small changes such as softer cloths or unscented soap may help.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 3: Building a routine\r\n\r\nRoutines help because they make the day predictable. Consider the following:\r\n\r\n1. Link each hygiene task to an event, such as brushing teeth after breakfast.\r\n2. Keep the order the same each day.\r\n3. Use a picture strip or short written list where helpful.\r\n4. Place items where the person can reach them.\r\n5. Practise one step until it feels easy before adding another.\r\n\r\nOur article on [breaking a chore into small teachable steps](/blog/article-breaking-a-chore-into-small-teachable-steps) explains this method in more detail.\r\n\r\n## Common hygiene areas at a glance\r\n\r\n| Area | What to think about |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| Hand washing | Water, soap, rubbing all surfaces, rinsing, drying. Often linked to meals and toilet use. |\r\n| Teeth and mouth | Gentle brushing, a toothbrush the person likes, regular routine. The WHO has fact sheets on oral health. |\r\n| Bathing | Privacy, safe floors, water temperature, adult supervision as needed. |\r\n| Toilet routines | Privacy, comfort, clear steps, and patience with accidents. |\r\n| Clothing and laundry | Clean clothes, sorting, washing, hanging. |\r\n| Hair and nails | Sensory comfort, gentle tools, agreed routines. |\r\n\r\n## Checklist 4: Safety and health\r\n\r\n- [ ] Never leave a person alone in water if supervision is needed.\r\n- [ ] Keep hot water, razors and chemicals out of reach unless safe use has been taught and agreed.\r\n- [ ] Use non-slip surfaces where possible.\r\n- [ ] Check skin and teeth gently, and mention any concerns to a doctor, nurse or dentist.\r\n- [ ] Follow any advice from your health professional about equipment or positioning.\r\n- [ ] Never use hygiene tasks as punishment or as a way to shame.\r\n\r\n## What if there are challenges?\r\n\r\nRefusal, distress or repeated accidents are signals. They may point to pain, fear, sensory overload or a need for clearer teaching. Try the smallest possible change first. If the problem persists, or you notice pain, sores, infection or sudden changes, speak with a qualified health professional. Medication, medical treatment and diagnosis are matters for them, not for this checklist.\r\n\r\n## Safeguarding and trust\r\n\r\nEveryone who supports a young person with personal care should be trusted, respectful and known to the family. Teach young people, in ways they can understand, that their body belongs to them and that they can say \"stop\". Let them know they can tell a trusted adult if something feels wrong. If you have any worry about a child's safety, the WHO has information on [child maltreatment](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/child-maltreatment), and you can seek help from a trusted local professional or authority.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania supports life-skills learning, including self-care, with respect for privacy and each person's pace. Families and caregivers are part of the team. You can find out more on our [life skills program page](/programs/program-life-skills) or read about our [caregiver training](/programs/program-caregiver-training).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/inclusion-checklist-schools-faith-groups-neighbourhoods.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"An Inclusion Checklist for Schools, Faith Groups and Neighbourhoods\"\r\nslug: inclusion-checklist-schools-faith-groups-neighbourhoods\r\ncategory: Inclusion\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical, low-cost checklist that schools, faith communities and neighbourhood associations can use to welcome people with disabilities and their families.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical, low-cost checklist that schools, faith communities and neighbourhood associations can use to welcome people with disabilities and their families.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [checklist, schools, community, accessibility, inclusion]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: stockClassroom\r\nthumb: stockClassroomThumb\r\nicon: megaphone\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Inclusion starts with asking people with disabilities and their families what would help.\"\r\n  - \"Many useful changes are low-cost: clear communication, flexible roles, welcoming habits.\"\r\n  - \"Check three areas: people and attitudes, spaces and information, and activities and roles.\"\r\n  - \"Review regularly; inclusion is a habit, not a one-time task.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Do we need a big budget to be inclusive?\"\r\n    a: \"Not usually. Many of the most valuable changes cost little: warm greetings, plain explanations, patience, flexible seating and asking families what helps. Larger changes, such as building work, can be planned step by step as resources allow.\"\r\n  - q: \"Where should we start?\"\r\n    a: \"Start by listening. Invite people with disabilities, older people and caregivers to tell you what gets in the way. Choose one or two barriers you can change soon, act on them and then ask if it helped.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if we get something wrong?\"\r\n    a: \"Mistakes are part of learning. Apologise, ask what would be better and try again. What matters is that people see you are willing to listen and change.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Centre for Excellence in Universal Design\"\r\n    publisher: \"National Disability Authority, Ireland\"\r\n    url: \"https://universaldesign.ie/\"\r\n  - title: \"Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n---\r\nA welcoming community is built from many small habits. Use this checklist to look honestly at how your school, faith group or neighbourhood association includes people with disabilities, older people and caregivers, then choose a few things to improve.\r\n\r\n## How to use this checklist\r\n\r\nWork through it with a small group, ideally including people with disabilities and their families. Tick what you already do, and mark what you could try. Do not aim to tick everything at once. Choose two or three changes, act, and revisit in a few months.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, about 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Whatever your community's size, some of those people are likely already part of it, or would join if the door were more open.\r\n\r\n## Part one: people and attitudes\r\n\r\nAttitudes shape whether someone feels welcome before they ever reach the door.\r\n\r\n- [ ] We greet people with disabilities and their caregivers by name and speak to the person directly.\r\n- [ ] Leaders and volunteers know respectful language. See our [guide to respectful language](/blog/article-respectful-language-about-disability-guide).\r\n- [ ] We ask what support is helpful instead of assuming.\r\n- [ ] Children and adults are taught that difference is normal and that bullying and teasing are not accepted.\r\n- [ ] Families are never blamed or shamed for a person's disability.\r\n- [ ] We keep personal information private and ask permission before sharing photos or stories.\r\n- [ ] We know who to speak to if someone is being treated badly.\r\n\r\n## Part two: spaces and information\r\n\r\nSimple planning can make places usable by more people. The idea of universal design, which means designing things to work for as many people as possible from the start, is a helpful guide.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Entrances, paths and seating have been looked at by people who use wheelchairs, walking aids or need to rest often.\r\n- [ ] There is a quiet, calmer space for anyone who finds noise, crowds or bright light overwhelming.\r\n- [ ] Important information is given in plain words, spoken and written, and repeated where needed.\r\n- [ ] Signs are clear and simple, and pictures are used where helpful.\r\n- [ ] Seating is flexible, and people can move or stand if they need to.\r\n- [ ] Toilets and water points are safe and reachable.\r\n- [ ] Emergency plans consider people who may need extra time or help to leave.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Walk through your space as if you had a pushchair, a walking stick or a child who dislikes loud sounds. You will spot barriers quickly.\r\n\r\n## Part three: activities and roles\r\n\r\nAccess is not only about getting in. It is about taking part and contributing.\r\n\r\n### For schools\r\n\r\n- [ ] Teachers explain tasks in more than one way, such as showing, telling and doing.\r\n- [ ] Children are given time and different ways to show what they know.\r\n- [ ] Peers are encouraged to include one another in play, sport and group work.\r\n- [ ] Families and teachers share information regularly and respectfully.\r\n- [ ] Staff seek guidance from qualified professionals about individual learning needs.\r\n\r\n### For faith groups\r\n\r\n- [ ] Services and gatherings are welcoming to people who move, speak or respond differently.\r\n- [ ] People with disabilities are invited to take part in real roles, such as welcoming, music, reading, serving or organising, if they wish.\r\n- [ ] Older people, including those living with memory loss, are visited and included with patience and dignity.\r\n- [ ] Caregivers are offered support and understanding, not only prayers.\r\n\r\n### For neighbourhood associations\r\n\r\n- [ ] Meetings are held at times and places that most people can reach.\r\n- [ ] Notices are shared in several ways, including by word of mouth for people who do not read easily.\r\n- [ ] People with disabilities and older residents are asked for their views on local plans.\r\n- [ ] Neighbours are encouraged to look out for one another, respectfully and without taking over.\r\n\r\n## Part four: voice and follow-up\r\n\r\n- [ ] People with disabilities, older people and caregivers help make decisions.\r\n- [ ] We have a simple way for anyone to share ideas or concerns.\r\n- [ ] We choose one or two changes at a time and check whether they helped.\r\n- [ ] We celebrate progress and learn from what did not work.\r\n- [ ] We remember that we are not experts in medical care, and we direct people to qualified health, therapy and social work professionals when advice is needed.\r\n\r\n## What if we can only do a little?\r\n\r\nThat is fine. A community that starts with three good habits is further along than one that plans everything and starts nothing. Begin with listening, add one physical or communication change and choose one activity to make more welcoming.\r\n\r\nFor a wider view of why this matters, read our [perspective on everyday inclusion](/blog/article-community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion). To understand the rights behind it, see the [CRPD explainer](/blog/article-un-convention-rights-persons-with-disabilities-explained).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nCommunity inclusion is one of the four pillars of Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, a non-profit based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam. We hope people with disabilities are active, contributing members of their communities rather than isolated ones.\r\n\r\nLearn more about our [neuro-diversity awareness program](/programs/program-neurodiversity), or find respectful ways to support this work on our [Get Involved page](/get-involved).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/keeping-a-simple-daily-observation-log.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"How to Keep a Simple Daily Observation Log\"\r\nslug: keeping-a-simple-daily-observation-log\r\ncategory: Caregiving\r\nexcerpt: \"A step-by-step guide to a short daily log of sleep, meals, mood and routines that helps caregivers share clear information with doctors and therapists.\"\r\ndescription: \"A step-by-step guide to a short daily log of sleep, meals, mood and routines that helps caregivers share clear information with doctors and therapists.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [caregiving, observation, record-keeping, professionals]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/keeping-a-simple-daily-observation-log.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/keeping-a-simple-daily-observation-log-thumb.webp\r\nicon: users-round\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"A daily log takes only a few minutes and turns memory into clear facts.\"\r\n  - \"Write what you see and hear, not guesses about causes or diagnoses.\"\r\n  - \"Use the same simple headings every day so patterns become easy to spot.\"\r\n  - \"Bring the log to appointments, and keep it private and safe.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How long should the log take each day?\"\r\n    a: \"A few minutes is enough. Choose four or five headings, write short notes, and stop. A log you can keep up is more useful than a detailed one you abandon after a week.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do I need to be able to write well?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Short words, ticks, symbols or drawings all work. You can also ask a family member to write for you, or record a short voice note if that suits you better.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should I write down what I think is causing a problem?\"\r\n    a: \"Write what happened first. If you have a guess, mark it clearly as a guess. Professionals need the facts, and they are the ones qualified to assess causes.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who should see the log?\"\r\n    a: \"Share it with the qualified professionals involved in the person's care, and with family members who help, if the person agrees. Keep it somewhere private, because it holds personal information.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n---\r\nTo keep a useful daily observation log, write a few short, factual notes at the same time each day: what happened, when, and what helped. Keep it brief and use the same headings each day. In a few weeks you will have a clear record that a doctor, nurse or therapist can read quickly.\r\n\r\n## Why keep a log at all?\r\n\r\nWhen you visit a professional, it is hard to remember the last few weeks. Under pressure, people forget details or mix up days. A log solves this. It also helps you see patterns you might miss, such as a hard afternoon that always follows a poor night's sleep.\r\n\r\nA log is not a test and it is not a diagnosis. It simply records what you saw. The professional decides what it means.\r\n\r\n## Step by step: how to start\r\n\r\n### 1. Choose a notebook or a place\r\n\r\nUse a school exercise book, a phone note or a simple folder. Pick one place only. Keep it where you can reach it easily, and keep it private.\r\n\r\n### 2. Pick four or five headings\r\n\r\nChoose only what matters for this person. Common headings include:\r\n\r\n- Sleep\r\n- Meals and drinks\r\n- Mood and energy\r\n- Activity and rest\r\n- Anything different today\r\n\r\nYou can change the headings later. Start small.\r\n\r\n### 3. Set a fixed time\r\n\r\nChoose a time when you are calm, for example after the evening meal. A habit is easier to keep when it is tied to something that already happens every day.\r\n\r\n### 4. Write facts, not conclusions\r\n\r\nCompare these two entries:\r\n\r\n| Less helpful | More helpful |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| \"Bad day, refused everything.\" | \"Refused breakfast. Ate lunch when sitting with the family. Cried after the visitors arrived.\" |\r\n| \"Was aggressive.\" | \"Pushed the cup away and shouted after the radio was switched on.\" |\r\n| \"Slept badly.\" | \"Awake several times in the night. Up early.\" |\r\n\r\nThe second column tells the reader what actually happened, and what was going on around it.\r\n\r\n### 5. Note what helped\r\n\r\nIf something calmed the person, write it down. A familiar song, a quiet room, a walk or a favourite food might be the most useful line in the whole log.\r\n\r\n### 6. Mark questions\r\n\r\nKeep a small section for questions you want to ask at the next visit. Write them as they come to mind, so you do not forget them.\r\n\r\n## What should you not write?\r\n\r\nSome things do not belong in the log:\r\n\r\n- **Guessed diagnoses.** Leave those to qualified professionals.\r\n- **Blame.** Avoid words that judge the person or the family.\r\n- **Medicine changes.** Never change medicine on your own. If you have concerns about medicine, contact the doctor or nurse who prescribed it.\r\n- **Unnecessary private details.** Write only what helps with care.\r\n\r\nUse respectful words. Write \"the person refused\" or \"the person became upset\", not labels such as \"difficult\" or \"naughty\".\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** If writing feels like too much, use ticks and one-word notes. Consistent small notes are better than long entries you cannot keep up.\r\n\r\n## How do you share the log with professionals?\r\n\r\nBefore an appointment, read through the recent pages and mark the main points. You might say, \"Over the last few weeks, sleep has been broken most nights, and afternoons seem hardest.\" Then hand over the log so the professional can look at the details.\r\n\r\nAsk the person for their agreement where possible. A person with a disability or an older person has a right to privacy and to be involved in decisions about their own information. Explain what you are writing and why.\r\n\r\nAlso consider who else might use the log:\r\n\r\n- Family members who share care can add notes, so nothing is lost when different people are present.\r\n- Teachers or therapists may find it helpful if the person agrees.\r\n- Keep the book in a private place and do not share it outside the care team without permission.\r\n\r\n## How do you keep going?\r\n\r\nMany logs fail after a week because they were too big. To keep going:\r\n\r\n1. Reduce the number of headings.\r\n2. Link the log to a daily habit.\r\n3. Share the task with another family member.\r\n4. Skip a day without guilt and simply continue the next day.\r\n\r\nIf the log itself becomes a source of stress, simplify it. It should support you, not add to your load. Our article on [why caregivers need support too](/blog/article-why-caregivers-need-support-too) explores this further.\r\n\r\n## Where does the log fit in wider care?\r\n\r\nThe log works alongside a steady daily pattern. If you are building one, see [supporting families with consistent care routines](/blog/article-supporting-families-with-consistent-care-routines). A routine gives structure to the day, and the log shows how the day actually went.\r\n\r\nIf you are unsure when a concern is serious enough to raise, read [when and how to seek professional guidance](/blog/article-when-and-how-to-seek-professional-guidance-faq). In an emergency, contact emergency services or the nearest health facility straight away instead of writing in the log.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania supports families and caregivers of people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people. Caregiver training is one of our five program areas, and it is built around practical skills, such as clear communication with professionals. Find out more on the [caregiver training program page](/programs/program-caregiver-training).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/keeping-children-and-vulnerable-adults-safe-during-activities.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Keeping Children and Vulnerable Adults Safe During Activities\"\r\nslug: keeping-children-and-vulnerable-adults-safe-during-activities\r\ncategory: Safeguarding\r\nexcerpt: \"A step-by-step guide to safer group activities: supervision, two-adult good practice, water and outings, and recording attendance for every session.\"\r\ndescription: \"A step-by-step guide to safer group activities: supervision, two-adult good practice, water and outings, and recording attendance for every session.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [supervision, activities, water-safety, outings]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/keeping-children-and-vulnerable-adults-safe-during-activities.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/keeping-children-and-vulnerable-adults-safe-during-activities-thumb.webp\r\nicon: shield-check\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Safer activities start with planning: know who is coming, what support they need and who is responsible.\"\r\n  - \"Having at least two responsible adults present is a widely recommended good practice, and it protects everyone.\"\r\n  - \"Water and outings need extra supervision, and qualified advice where relevant.\"\r\n  - \"Record attendance at the start and end, and make sure every person is accounted for.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Why have two adults present during activities?\"\r\n    a: \"It is a general good practice that protects both participants and helpers. It means no adult is alone and unobserved with a child or a person who needs support, and there is always someone to help in an emergency.\"\r\n  - q: \"How much supervision does water need?\"\r\n    a: \"More than most activities. Water needs close, constant attention from responsible adults whose only job is watching. Follow the venue's rules, use trained lifeguards or instructors where available, and get advice from qualified professionals.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do we need to take attendance at small activities?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. A simple list at the start and end makes sure nobody is missed, and it helps a great deal in an emergency. Keep the list private, share it only with those who need it, and store it securely.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if a participant has health needs?\"\r\n    a: \"Ask families and carers about support needs in advance, listen to what they say, and involve health professionals where appropriate. Do not give medicine or medical care unless you are trained and authorised.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Violence against children\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children\"\r\n  - title: \"Physical activity\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nTo keep children and vulnerable adults safe during activities, plan ahead, keep supervision open and visible, have at least two responsible adults present, take extra care around water and outings, and record who attends. This guide walks through these steps. It is general good practice, not legal or medical advice, so seek qualified input for your own situation.\r\n\r\n## Why do activities need a safety plan?\r\n\r\nActivities are where people meet, learn and enjoy themselves. They are also where accidents and misunderstandings can happen, especially when groups include children, older people or people with disabilities who may need extra support.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization recommends regular physical activity for people of all ages and abilities, including an average of 60 minutes a day for children and adolescents. Being active is good for people. Safety planning simply makes sure that it happens in a way that protects them.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: Know who is coming and what support they need\r\n\r\nBefore the day, ask families and carers what each person needs. Keep questions simple:\r\n\r\n- Does the person need help with movement, communication or eating?\r\n- Are there things that upset or frighten them?\r\n- Are there health needs the team should know about?\r\n- Who should we contact in an emergency?\r\n\r\nListen to the person as well as their family. Never make sharing this information a condition of taking part, and keep it private.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Set roles and ratios\r\n\r\nDecide who leads, who watches, and who is the welfare contact. Make sure there are enough helpers for the size of the group and the level of need. If the activity is more demanding, or the group needs more support, add more helpers.\r\n\r\n## Step 3: Have at least two adults present\r\n\r\nA widely used good practice is that at least two responsible adults are present whenever children or people who need support are taking part. This helps in several ways:\r\n\r\n1. It reduces the chance of any adult being alone and unobserved with a child.\r\n2. It protects helpers from false or mistaken accusations.\r\n3. One adult can help in an emergency while the other stays with the group.\r\n4. It makes it easier to notice concerns.\r\n\r\nIf personal care such as toileting is needed, agree in advance how it will be handled respectfully, with the family's knowledge and in line with the organisation's rules.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Choose spaces where activity can be seen, such as open rooms or areas with clear sight lines. Avoid closed rooms with nobody else nearby.\r\n\r\n## Step 4: Take extra care with water\r\n\r\nWater is one of the highest-risk settings, even in shallow areas. A person can be in difficulty quickly and quietly.\r\n\r\n- Assign helpers whose only job is watching the water.\r\n- Keep groups small and stay within arm's reach of those who need it.\r\n- Follow the venue's rules and use trained lifeguards or instructors where they are available.\r\n- Never leave anyone in or near water without an adult present.\r\n- Ask families about swimming ability and comfort, and never pressure anyone to enter the water.\r\n- Take advice from qualified professionals for any therapy in water.\r\n\r\n## Step 5: Plan outings carefully\r\n\r\nOutings add travel, crowds and unfamiliar places.\r\n\r\n1. Visit or research the place first and check access, toilets and quiet areas.\r\n2. Tell families the destination, timings and contact details in advance.\r\n3. Agree a meeting point in case someone becomes separated.\r\n4. Count the group at every stop, including when leaving and boarding transport.\r\n5. Carry a charged phone, a list of contacts and a basic first-aid kit.\r\n\r\n## Step 6: Record attendance\r\n\r\nWrite down who arrives and who leaves. Use a simple list, checked at the start, during any change of location, and at the end. Note who collected each child or person, so you know they left with someone they were expected to leave with.\r\n\r\nKeep the list private, share it only with those who need it, and keep it secure. Our article on [protecting families' personal information online](/blog/article-protecting-families-personal-information-online) has more on handling records.\r\n\r\n## Step 7: Know what to do if something goes wrong\r\n\r\n- Make the person safe first, and call emergency services if needed.\r\n- Tell the welfare contact and the person's family as soon as it is appropriate.\r\n- Write down what happened, factually and privately.\r\n- Follow the organisation's process for concerns, described in [how to raise a safeguarding concern safely](/blog/article-how-to-raise-a-safeguarding-concern-safely).\r\n\r\n## A note on dignity during safety routines\r\n\r\nSafety measures should never feel like punishment. Explain to participants, in words they can follow, why helpers stay close, why a list is taken and why everyone counts at each stop. Offer choices where you can, and speak to the person rather than about them. Some people, including autistic people and people living with dementia, may find changes of place or routine unsettling, so a calm, predictable approach helps. Ask families what reassures the person and use it.\r\n\r\n## Step 8: Review afterwards\r\n\r\nSpend a few minutes with the team after each activity. Ask what worked, what felt risky and what to change. Keep improving.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania runs public activities such as life-skills sessions, hydrotherapy and swimming, classroom learning, exercise and community time. Careful supervision is part of doing these responsibly. To learn more, see our [programs](/programs) or [contact us](/contact). For the wider picture, read [safeguarding basics for community organisations](/blog/article-safeguarding-basics-for-community-organisations).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/keeping-older-people-mentally-stimulated-and-connected.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Keeping Older People Mentally Active and Socially Connected\"\r\nslug: keeping-older-people-mentally-stimulated-and-connected\r\ncategory: \"Healthy ageing\"\r\nexcerpt: \"Why purpose, conversation and belonging matter as much as physical health in older age, and how families and communities can offer them with respect.\"\r\ndescription: \"Why purpose, conversation and belonging matter as much as physical health in older age, and how families and communities can offer them with respect.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [mental-health, social-connection, older-people, community]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/keeping-older-people-mentally-stimulated-and-connected.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/keeping-older-people-mentally-stimulated-and-connected-thumb.webp\r\nicon: heart-pulse\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Mental stimulation and social connection are part of health in older age, not extras.\"\r\n  - \"Older people are contributors with knowledge and experience, not only people who receive care.\"\r\n  - \"Loneliness and isolation can affect mood, so noticing changes and reaching out matters.\"\r\n  - \"The best activities are ones the person chooses and finds meaningful, adapted to their abilities.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Does staying mentally active protect against dementia?\"\r\n    a: \"Nobody can promise that. Mental and social activity supports wellbeing and enjoyment, and it is a good habit at any age. If you are worried about memory changes, ask a doctor or nurse for a qualified assessment.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if an older person does not want to join activities?\"\r\n    a: \"Respect the choice and gently explore why. The person may be tired, worried about mobility or feel the activity is not meant for them. Offer options, invite them to try once, and follow their interests rather than pressing.\"\r\n  - q: \"How can I tell if an older relative is feeling low?\"\r\n    a: \"Signs can include withdrawing, losing interest, sleeping or eating differently, or seeming hopeless. These can have many causes. Talk kindly with the person and encourage a check with a doctor or nurse, who can assess mood and physical health.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Mental health of older adults\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-of-older-adults\"\r\n  - title: \"Decade of Healthy Ageing\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/initiatives/decade-of-healthy-ageing\"\r\n  - title: \"Ageing\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/en/global-issues/ageing\"\r\n---\r\nAn older person's health is not only a matter of the body. A mind that is engaged and a life that includes other people are part of ageing well. Our view at Eagle's Wings is that older people should be treated as valued members of their community, with a continuing role, not as people to be managed.\r\n\r\n## Why does the mind need exercise too?\r\n\r\nWe often ask an older relative about blood pressure, appetite and walking. We less often ask, \"What are you interested in these days?\" or \"Who have you talked with this week?\"\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization (WHO) treats the mental health of older adults as an important part of healthy ageing. Life in later years can bring real changes: retirement, bereavement, reduced mobility, health problems and, sometimes, less contact with others. Meaningful activity and good relationships are among the things that help people stay well through those changes.\r\n\r\nThe United Nations and the WHO have named 2021–2030 the [Decade of Healthy Ageing](https://www.who.int/initiatives/decade-of-healthy-ageing). The message is that communities should help people stay able, active and included as they grow older.\r\n\r\n## What is a perspective worth challenging?\r\n\r\nA common assumption is that older people mostly need rest and quiet. Rest is important, but too much can leave a person feeling unneeded. A more respectful view is that older people continue to learn, teach, decide and belong.\r\n\r\nIn many Tanzanian families, elders hold knowledge of family history, farming, cooking, songs, proverbs and conflict resolution. Asking for that knowledge is a way of saying, \"You still matter to us.\" This is a value, not a programme, and any family or neighbourhood can practise it.\r\n\r\n## What does mental stimulation really mean?\r\n\r\nIt does not need to be a test or a puzzle. Mental stimulation is anything that engages attention, memory, imagination or conversation. Ideas include:\r\n\r\n- Storytelling, where a grandparent shares memories and young people ask questions.\r\n- Reading aloud, or listening to the radio and talking about it.\r\n- Singing or listening to familiar songs.\r\n- Games such as cards, board games or word games.\r\n- Practical tasks, such as sorting vegetables, folding laundry or tending plants.\r\n- Learning something new at any pace, such as a craft or a simple skill.\r\n\r\nThe best choice is the one that fits the person. A task that is too hard causes frustration, and one that is too easy can feel patronising. Adapt to the person's abilities and interests, and let them lead.\r\n\r\n## Why does social connection matter?\r\n\r\nPeople are social by nature. Regular conversation, shared meals and community gatherings support mood, give structure to the week and provide a chance to notice changes in health. Loneliness and isolation can weigh heavily on wellbeing, and they can affect people who live surrounded by family as well as those who live alone. What counts is the quality of contact, feeling heard and included.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** A short, regular visit or call is often more helpful than an occasional long one. Predictable contact gives something to look forward to.\r\n\r\n## How can families and communities help?\r\n\r\nA few small actions make a difference:\r\n\r\n1. **Ask before you assume.** Find out what the person enjoys and what they would like to keep doing.\r\n2. **Include, do not just care for.** Invite older people to family decisions, celebrations and community events.\r\n3. **Make it possible.** Think about transport, seating, lighting, hearing and toilets so that gatherings are accessible.\r\n4. **Connect generations.** Encourage children and young people to spend time with elders and learn from them.\r\n5. **Notice changes.** If an older person seems withdrawn, unusually forgetful or low in mood, encourage a check-up with a qualified health worker.\r\n\r\nSome older people, including those living with dementia, need extra support to take part. Our checklist on [supporting a person living with dementia](/blog/article-supporting-a-person-living-with-dementia-routines-calm) covers routines and communication.\r\n\r\n## What about mood and mental health?\r\n\r\nFeeling sad at times is human. Persistent low mood, worry or withdrawal is different, and it deserves attention. It should not be dismissed as \"just old age.\" A doctor or nurse can assess the person's mental and physical health and suggest support. If someone talks about harming themselves, seek urgent help from a health professional or emergency service.\r\n\r\n## How does this connect to food and movement?\r\n\r\nMind, body and mood are linked. A walk with a neighbour combines movement and company. A shared meal combines nutrition and conversation. See our guides on [gentle movement at home](/blog/article-gentle-movement-at-home-for-older-adults) and [affordable nutrition habits](/blog/article-simple-affordable-nutrition-habits-for-older-people).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania includes mental stimulation guidance in its elderly care support, alongside nutrition and physical exercise. We believe in inclusion, dignity and the value of every older person. Read about our [elderly care program](/programs/program-elderly-care) or [contact us](/contact) to talk.\r\n\r\nThis article is general education, not medical advice.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/making-community-events-sensory-friendly.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Making Community Events Sensory-Friendly, Step by Step\"\r\nslug: making-community-events-sensory-friendly\r\ncategory: Events\r\nexcerpt: \"A step-by-step guide to reducing noise, crowding and surprise at community events so autistic people and others with sensory needs can take part comfortably.\"\r\ndescription: \"A step-by-step guide to reducing noise, crowding and surprise at community events so autistic people and others with sensory needs can take part comfortably.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [sensory, autism, events, how-to]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/making-community-events-sensory-friendly.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/making-community-events-sensory-friendly-thumb.webp\r\nicon: calendar-days\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Sensory-friendly planning reduces overload from noise, light, crowds and surprise.\"\r\n  - \"Predictability helps: share the plan, the timings and the layout in advance.\"\r\n  - \"A quiet space and permission to take breaks are among the most helpful changes.\"\r\n  - \"Sensory needs differ from person to person, so ask and stay flexible.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Who benefits from a sensory-friendly event?\"\r\n    a: \"Many autistic people benefit, and so do people with ADHD, some older people, people living with dementia, and anyone easily overwhelmed by noise or crowds. Sensory needs vary widely, so flexible options help more than one fixed setup.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does sensory-friendly mean silent and dull?\"\r\n    a: \"No. It means thoughtful choices: lower volume, fewer surprises, calm spaces and warnings before loud moments. Activities can still be lively, joyful and creative, with options for people who need a gentler pace.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do we need special equipment?\"\r\n    a: \"Usually not. Simple things work: a quiet corner, clear signs, a visual timetable, seating and permission to leave and return. Some people bring their own comfort items or ear protection, which should be welcomed.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.cdc.gov/autism/index.html\"\r\n---\r\nTo make an event sensory-friendly, reduce sudden or overwhelming sound, light and crowding, share a clear plan in advance, and provide a quiet place where people can take a break. These steps help autistic people and others who find busy environments tiring. This guide gives general planning ideas. It is not clinical advice, and every person's needs are different.\r\n\r\n## What do we mean by \"sensory-friendly\"?\r\n\r\nMany people experience the world through senses that can feel louder, brighter or more intense than others expect. This is common among autistic people. The World Health Organization notes that [about 1 in 100 children worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism, and that it is a wide spectrum where support needs vary greatly. Both \"autistic person\" and \"person with autism\" are used, and it is polite to ask which someone prefers.\r\n\r\nSensory-friendly does not mean the same thing for everyone. One person may find music painful, another may be happiest near a window with fresh air. The aim is to offer choices and reduce avoidable stress.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: Ask and learn before you plan\r\n\r\nStart by asking. Talk to autistic people, parents, carers and support workers about what helps. Invite comments in your invitation, but do not force anyone to explain personal details.\r\n\r\nListen without judging. Accept that advice may differ, and plan options rather than one fixed solution.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Choose the venue and layout carefully\r\n\r\nThe place shapes how the day feels.\r\n\r\n1. Visit the venue at the same time of day as your event, and notice the noise, echo, smells and light.\r\n2. Choose spaces with natural light and airflow where possible, and avoid harsh flickering lights.\r\n3. Keep walkways wide to avoid crowding and bumping.\r\n4. Place loud activities, such as music or games, away from the quiet area.\r\n5. Keep entrances calm, with a helper ready to greet and guide people.\r\n\r\n## Step 3: Make the day predictable\r\n\r\nUncertainty can cause stress. Predictability gives people a sense of control.\r\n\r\n- Share the programme in advance, with times and a simple map of the venue.\r\n- Use a visual timetable with pictures and short words, displayed at the entrance.\r\n- Explain what will happen at the start, such as speeches, food or music.\r\n- Warn people before loud or sudden moments, such as clapping, drums or announcements.\r\n- Keep the schedule steady and flag changes gently if they happen.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** A short \"what to expect\" sheet, sent before the event, helps first-time visitors. Include photographs of the venue only if you have permission and they show no people.\r\n\r\n## Step 4: Create a quiet space\r\n\r\nA quiet space is often the most valuable change. It should be a calm area where anyone can rest without being questioned.\r\n\r\n- Choose a spot away from the noise, with dim or soft light.\r\n- Provide seating, cushions or mats, and keep it uncluttered.\r\n- Mark it with a clear sign and let people know they can use it at any time.\r\n- Do not use it as a place of punishment or isolation. It is a choice, not a consequence.\r\n- Make sure a helper checks in from a respectful distance, especially if children are alone there.\r\n\r\n## Step 5: Brief helpers to be calm and flexible\r\n\r\nHelpers set the tone. Brief them before the event.\r\n\r\n- Speak calmly, use short sentences and give people time to respond.\r\n- Avoid touching people or their belongings without permission.\r\n- Understand that a person covering their ears, leaving the room or repeating actions may be managing stress, not misbehaving.\r\n- Offer choices rather than orders, such as \"Would you like to sit here or near the window?\"\r\n- Know where the quiet space, the welfare contact and the exits are.\r\n\r\nFor more on preparing helpers, see [responsible volunteering in community work](/blog/article-responsible-volunteering-in-community-work).\r\n\r\n## Step 6: Adapt activities and food\r\n\r\n- Offer activities that people can join at their own pace, with the option to watch first.\r\n- Reduce background music or turn it low, and avoid sudden loud sounds.\r\n- Provide simple, plain food and drink options, and label them clearly.\r\n- Allow people to bring familiar objects, headphones or comfort items.\r\n- Keep the programme short enough to avoid fatigue, with breaks built in.\r\n\r\n## Step 7: Review with the people who came\r\n\r\nAfterwards, ask kindly what helped and what did not. Invite honest replies and accept that you will not get everything right the first time. Write down changes for next time and keep improving. Guidance on asking respectfully is in [collecting event feedback without exploiting stories](/blog/article-collecting-event-feedback-without-exploiting-stories).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania promotes neuro-diversity awareness, including autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability. Sensory-aware planning is one everyday way to put that awareness into practice. Learn more about our [neuro-diversity awareness program](/programs/program-neurodiversity).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/making-therapy-education-guidance-easier-to-understand.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Making Therapy and Education Guidance Easier to Understand\"\r\nslug: making-therapy-education-guidance-easier-to-understand\r\ncategory: Family guidance\r\nexcerpt: \"Why therapy and education advice often feels confusing to families, and practical ways to turn it into clear, doable steps for everyday life at home.\"\r\ndescription: \"Why therapy and education advice often feels confusing to families, and practical ways to turn it into clear, doable steps for everyday life at home.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [therapy, education, parenting, guidance, families]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/making-therapy-education-guidance-easier-to-understand.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/making-therapy-education-guidance-easier-to-understand-thumb.webp\r\nicon: book-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Guidance is only useful when a family can understand it and use it in daily life.\"\r\n  - \"Ask for plain words, a demonstration and a short written plan for every recommendation.\"\r\n  - \"Small, regular practice at home usually helps more than long, rare sessions.\"\r\n  - \"Therapy and education advice should always come from qualified professionals who know your child.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Why does professional advice sometimes feel hard to follow?\"\r\n    a: \"Advice is often given quickly, in technical words, during an emotional appointment. Families may leave with many ideas but no clear plan. Asking the professional to slow down, explain in simple words and write down the key steps can make a big difference.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is it acceptable to ask a therapist or teacher to repeat something?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Good professionals expect questions. Asking for repeats, examples or a demonstration shows you care about doing the plan correctly. It is far better to ask than to guess and practise something in a way that was not intended.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can I use general advice instead of a personal plan?\"\r\n    a: \"General information helps you understand the topic, but it cannot replace a plan made for your child by a qualified professional. Every child has different strengths, needs and health considerations, so personal guidance should come first.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Children with disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nGuidance about therapy and education helps a child only when the family can understand it and use it at home. Clear words, simple steps and steady practice matter more than complicated advice. This article explains why guidance often feels confusing and how families and professionals can make it easier to follow.\r\n\r\n## Why does therapy and education advice feel so confusing?\r\n\r\nMany families leave an appointment or school meeting with a head full of new words and a heart full of feelings. That is normal. When a child has a new diagnosis, or a long wait has finally ended, it is hard to take in everything at once.\r\n\r\nCommon reasons guidance feels hard to use:\r\n\r\n- **Technical language.** Professionals use terms that are everyday to them but new to you.\r\n- **Too much at once.** Ten suggestions in one visit can feel like none at all.\r\n- **No written plan.** Memory fades quickly after an emotional conversation.\r\n- **Advice that does not fit the home.** A plan that needs special equipment or a quiet room may not suit a busy family.\r\n- **Different voices.** The therapist, teacher and relatives may each say something different.\r\n\r\nNone of this is a failure on the family's part. It is a communication gap, and it can be closed.\r\n\r\n## What good guidance looks like\r\n\r\nClear guidance has a few things in common. It is short, specific and shown, not only told.\r\n\r\n| Hard to use | Easier to use |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| \"Work on his communication.\" | \"Each morning, hold up two cups and wait for him to point to the one he wants.\" |\r\n| \"Encourage more independence.\" | \"Let her carry her own plate to the sink after meals.\" |\r\n| \"Follow the routine consistently.\" | \"Keep the same three steps before homework each day.\" |\r\n\r\nNotice that the easier versions name one action, one moment in the day and one thing to look for. They also suit ordinary household life.\r\n\r\n### Ask for the plan in three forms\r\n\r\nWhen you meet a therapist, teacher or counsellor, you can ask for:\r\n\r\n1. **Words.** \"Can you explain that in simple language?\"\r\n2. **A demonstration.** \"Can you show me how to do it with my child?\"\r\n3. **A note.** \"Could you write the main steps down, or may I write them while you speak?\"\r\n\r\n## How can families make guidance easier to use at home?\r\n\r\nOnce you have a plan from a qualified professional, a few habits help it stick.\r\n\r\n### Start with one thing\r\n\r\nChoose the single step that feels most doable this week. Add another only when the first feels natural. A small routine that happens every day is worth more than an ambitious plan that is dropped after a few days.\r\n\r\n### Attach it to something that already happens\r\n\r\nLink practice to an existing moment, such as after breakfast, before bath time or when the family sits together in the evening. This removes the need to remember and reduces the pressure of \"finding time\".\r\n\r\n### Keep it playful and respectful\r\n\r\nChildren learn best when they feel safe and valued. Practice can be part of play, songs, drawing, helping in the kitchen or time outside. If your child is tired, upset or unwell, pausing is fine. Talk to the therapist about how to handle those days.\r\n\r\n### Note what you see\r\n\r\nA simple notebook helps. Write a few words about what went well, what was hard and any questions. Bring it to the next appointment. Professionals can adjust the plan when they hear real details from home.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Before you leave any appointment, say the plan back in your own words: \"So, this week I will try this, at this time, and watch for this.\" If you got it wrong, the professional can gently correct it on the spot.\r\n\r\n## Working together at home, school and therapy\r\n\r\nA child spends time in different places, and each place can support the same goals. When the family, teacher and therapist share simple, consistent messages, the child does not have to work out several sets of rules.\r\n\r\nHelpful ways to connect these places:\r\n\r\n- Share the same short list of goals with everyone who supports the child.\r\n- Ask therapists to explain how their suggestions can be adapted for the classroom, where appropriate.\r\n- Tell teachers what works at home, because you know your child best.\r\n- Include the child in the conversation in a way that suits their age and understanding.\r\n\r\nThe United Nations [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) is built on ideas such as dignity, participation and respect for difference. In everyday terms, that means a child and family should be treated as partners, not passive receivers of instructions.\r\n\r\n## What guidance cannot do\r\n\r\nWritten guides, including this one, are general and educational. They cannot diagnose, prescribe treatment or replace a qualified professional who knows your child. If you are worried about your child's health, development or behaviour, please speak to a doctor, therapist or other qualified professional. Never change or stop any medication without medical advice.\r\n\r\nRemember that families are not expected to become therapists overnight. Your role is to love, observe and practise gently. The professionals' role is to guide and adjust the plan.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, includes education and therapy guidance as one of its five program areas, covering education about neurological conditions, therapy exercises and specialised parenting. Our approach is to explain things in plain language, so that families feel confident and included. You can read more on the [Education and Therapy Guidance program page](/programs/program-education-therapy). If you want to build routines that support what you learn, our article on [supporting families with consistent care routines](/blog/article-supporting-families-with-consistent-care-routines) may help. For questions, visit our [contact page](/contact).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/measuring-outcomes-responsibly-small-honest-numbers.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Measuring Outcomes Responsibly: Why Small, Clear Numbers Win\"\r\nslug: measuring-outcomes-responsibly-small-honest-numbers\r\ncategory: Transparency\r\nexcerpt: \"Why small, well-explained numbers are more trustworthy than big vague ones, and how reach, learning, practice and experience give a fuller picture.\"\r\ndescription: \"Why small, well-explained numbers are more trustworthy than big vague ones, and how reach, learning, practice and experience give a fuller picture.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [impact, measurement, outcomes, accountability]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/measuring-outcomes-responsibly-small-honest-numbers.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/measuring-outcomes-responsibly-small-honest-numbers-thumb.webp\r\nicon: file-check-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"A number is only useful when it says what was counted, by whom, and over what period.\"\r\n  - \"Reach, learning, practice and experience together tell a fuller story than any one figure.\"\r\n  - \"Small, clearly explained numbers build more trust than large, vague ones.\"\r\n  - \"Responsible measurement protects the dignity and privacy of the people involved.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Why not just report how many people were helped?\"\r\n    a: \"A headcount alone says little. It does not show what people received, whether it was useful or how they felt about it. It can also encourage counting people twice or stretching the meaning of help. It works best next to other measures.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can feelings and experiences be measured honestly?\"\r\n    a: \"Partly. Listening to people, asking open questions and recording what they say can show real experience. It is not exact, and it should not be dressed up as if it were. Say what was asked, who answered and what the limits are.\"\r\n  - q: \"What does responsible measurement mean for people with disabilities?\"\r\n    a: \"It means asking with consent, protecting privacy, using respectful language and not reducing a person to a score. People and families should be able to say no to being counted or quoted without losing any support.\"\r\n  - q: \"How can a supporter tell sound numbers from inflated ones?\"\r\n    a: \"Look for a clear description of what was counted, the time period, the method and the limits. Be cautious of round, impressive totals with no explanation, or figures that quietly compare unlike things.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"World report on disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564182\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n---\r\nMeasuring outcomes responsibly means counting only what you can explain, saying what your numbers do not show, and never letting a figure replace the person behind it. A small number that is clearly described is worth more than a large number nobody can check.\r\n\r\nThis article explains that idea, and how the Foundation plans to think about it.\r\n\r\n## Why do big, vague numbers cause problems?\r\n\r\nA large total looks impressive. But a total with no explanation invites more questions than it answers. Was one person counted once or many times? Did \"helped\" mean a conversation, a workshop or long-term support? Over what period? Compared with what?\r\n\r\nWhen these answers are missing, readers cannot tell good work from good marketing. That is unfair to honest organisations too, because careful groups look weaker next to inflated claims.\r\n\r\nThere is a second risk. When a number becomes the goal, people may start to chase the number instead of the need. Work can drift toward what is easy to count and away from what matters.\r\n\r\n## What counts as a good number?\r\n\r\nA good number is one that a stranger could question and still trust. It usually has four qualities.\r\n\r\n1. **A clear definition.** What exactly was counted?\r\n2. **A source.** Who counted it, and how?\r\n3. **A period.** From when to when?\r\n4. **A limit.** What does this number not tell us?\r\n\r\nGlobal figures need the same care. The World Health Organization's World report on disability is useful background for why this work matters. It describes the world. It is not the result of any one organisation, and it should never be presented as one.\r\n\r\n## The four things the Foundation says it will report on\r\n\r\nThe Foundation's [Impact page](/impact) names four areas. Together they aim to give a rounded picture, because no single measure can.\r\n\r\n### Reach\r\n\r\nReach is about who the work touches, in general terms: which programs, which groups, roughly how widely. It answers the question \"who was this for?\" Reach is the easiest thing to count, which is why it should never stand alone. Being reached is not the same as being helped.\r\n\r\n### Learning\r\n\r\nLearning is what the organisation discovered, including mistakes. What worked, what did not, and what will change. It shows whether an organisation is paying attention. A report with no learning is often a report with no reflection.\r\n\r\n### Practice\r\n\r\nPractice is about how the work is done: the quality of sessions, the training of people who lead them, the safeguarding habits and the care taken over consent. Good practice matters because good intentions are not enough when working with children, people with disabilities and older people.\r\n\r\n### Experience\r\n\r\nExperience is what people themselves say about the work: families, caregivers and participants, in their own words and with their permission. It is the hardest area to measure and the most important to listen to. It should be reported with humility, because it is a set of voices, not a score.\r\n\r\n## How do the four fit together?\r\n\r\nHere is a simple way to see why they need each other.\r\n\r\n| Measure | The question it answers | What it cannot tell you alone |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| Reach | Who was involved? | Whether it was useful |\r\n| Learning | What did we find out? | Whether people felt respected |\r\n| Practice | Was the work done well and safely? | How many people took part |\r\n| Experience | What did people tell us? | Whether it was the same for everyone |\r\n\r\nRead one column and you may be misled. Read all four and you get a fair, if modest, picture.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Being honest does not mean having nothing to say. It means saying what you know, what you do not, and how you found out.\r\n\r\n## What does responsible measurement look like in practice?\r\n\r\nResponsible measurement is as much about behaviour as arithmetic. A few habits help:\r\n\r\n- **Ask with consent.** People and families should be told why they are being asked, and be free to say no without losing support.\r\n- **Protect privacy.** Report in general terms. Avoid details that could identify a child, a family or an older person.\r\n- **Respect dignity.** Use person-respectful language and describe abilities and support, not deficits.\r\n- **Avoid comparing unlike things.** A change in one group does not prove the same change in another.\r\n- **Do not claim more than you know.** Correlation is not proof. Say \"we observed\" rather than \"we caused\".\r\n- **Correct mistakes openly.** If a figure was wrong, say so and fix it.\r\n\r\nThese habits reflect the spirit of the UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), which is built on dignity, autonomy and participation. People are not data points. They are partners in the work.\r\n\r\n## What should you expect from a young organisation?\r\n\r\nThe Foundation was founded in 2025. A young organisation should expect to have little to show at first, and should say so. What you can fairly ask is how it plans to measure its work, what it will do when results are mixed, and whether it will share what it learns.\r\n\r\nBe cautious of any group that reports large results very early, without explaining how they were counted. Patience is part of trust on both sides.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nThe Foundation does not currently publish numeric results. Its [Impact page](/impact) explains the four areas above, and reporting will be shared on this website as it becomes available. You may also like our piece on [reading a non-profit report with good questions](/blog/article-how-to-read-a-nonprofit-report-as-a-donor). If you have a question about how we measure our work, please [contact us](/contact).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/mission-and-vision-in-plain-words.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Our Mission and Vision, Explained in Plain Words\"\r\nslug: mission-and-vision-in-plain-words\r\ncategory: Foundation\r\nexcerpt: \"What Eagle's Wings means by its mission to empower, encourage and educate, and its vision of full inclusion, explained simply and without jargon.\"\r\ndescription: \"What Eagle's Wings means by its mission to empower, encourage and educate, and its vision of full inclusion, explained simply and without jargon.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [mission, vision, inclusion, foundation]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: child\r\nthumb: child\r\nicon: building-2\r\nfeatured: true\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Our mission is to empower, encourage and educate less privileged people, people with special needs and older people by helping, caring for and supporting their needs.\"\r\n  - \"Our vision is full inclusive awareness: people with disabilities are valued equally and get comprehensive support to reach their full potential.\"\r\n  - \"The mission describes what we do. The vision describes the world we hope for.\"\r\n  - \"Both are promises about values and approach, not claims about results.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is the difference between a mission and a vision?\"\r\n    a: \"A mission says what an organisation does and for whom, today. A vision describes the future it hopes to help create. Our mission is about empowering, encouraging and educating. Our vision is about full inclusion and equal value.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who is the Foundation's work for?\"\r\n    a: \"It is for people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, including those living with Alzheimer's and dementia. It also supports their families and caregivers, who are a vital part of daily care.\"\r\n  - q: \"Why does the mission say 'less privileged'?\"\r\n    a: \"It reminds us that support is often hardest to reach for people with fewer resources. The phrase describes circumstances, not worth. Everyone we support is valued equally.\"\r\n  - q: \"Where can I read the full statement?\"\r\n    a: \"The full mission, vision and values are on the Mission, Vision and Values page of this website. This article only explains them in everyday language.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Ageing and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ageing-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nOur mission is to empower, encourage and educate less privileged people, people with special needs and older people by helping, caring for and supporting their needs. Our vision is a community where people with disabilities are valued equally and get the support they need to reach their full potential. This article explains both statements in everyday words.\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania is a non-profit founded in 2025 and based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam. We put our purpose in writing so that anyone can hold us to it.\r\n\r\n## What is the difference between a mission and a vision?\r\n\r\nThink of a journey. The mission is what we do on the road each day. The vision is the place we hope to reach.\r\n\r\n| | Mission | Vision |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Question it answers | What do we do, and for whom? | What kind of world do we hope for? |\r\n| Time frame | Now and every day | The future we work towards |\r\n| Ours in short | Empower, encourage and educate | Full inclusion and equal value |\r\n\r\nBoth matter. A mission without a vision can become a list of tasks. A vision without a mission is only a wish.\r\n\r\n## The mission, word by word\r\n\r\n### Empower\r\n\r\nTo empower is to help a person gain confidence, skills and choice over their own life. It is different from doing things for someone. In practice, this can be as simple as patiently teaching a household task, or explaining a therapy exercise so a family feels able to try it.\r\n\r\n### Encourage\r\n\r\nEncouragement means noticing what a person can do and building on it. It avoids pity. A child, a young person or an older person who is encouraged is more likely to try again.\r\n\r\n### Educate\r\n\r\nEducation runs in two directions. We share clear information with families, caregivers and communities. We also learn from them. The information we offer is general and educational. It never replaces advice from a doctor, therapist, nurse or social worker.\r\n\r\n### Helping, caring for and supporting needs\r\n\r\nThese three phrases describe practical support. They are about listening to what a person actually needs, rather than deciding on their behalf.\r\n\r\n## Who is \"less privileged, special needs and older\"?\r\n\r\nThe mission names three groups of people: those who are less privileged, people with special needs, and older people. Our work focuses on people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, including those living with Alzheimer's and dementia. We also support their families and caregivers.\r\n\r\nWe use the phrase \"special needs\" because it appears in our founding statement. In our daily language we prefer to say \"person with a disability\", \"autistic person\" or \"person with autism\" (both are widely used), and \"person living with dementia\". We describe support needs, not weaknesses.\r\n\r\n## The vision in plain words\r\n\r\nOur vision is full inclusive awareness. That means people are aware, in the everyday sense, that people with disabilities belong in community life and are valued equally. It also means they receive comprehensive support, meaning help that looks at the whole person and family rather than one part of life.\r\n\r\nThe phrase \"reach their full potential\" is easy to misread. We do not use it to mean \"become like everyone else\". We mean that each person should have the chance to grow in their own way, at their own pace.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** The vision matches an idea found in the UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008. It is built on dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination, participation and inclusion, accessibility and respect for difference.\r\n\r\n## How the vision becomes four practical pillars\r\n\r\nOur vision rests on four pillars: community inclusion; rights and dignity; empowerment and independence; and early support. Each turns a big idea into something a person can recognise in daily life. We describe them one by one in [our article on the four pillars](/blog/article-four-pillars-of-our-vision-in-daily-practice).\r\n\r\n## Why we write these promises down\r\n\r\nThe WHO estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, around 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Behind that number are individual people and families, many of whom feel unseen. Naming our purpose openly keeps us honest. If our actions drift from our words, people can and should point it out.\r\n\r\nIt is also why we talk about values and approach. We do not claim results we cannot prove. We explain that approach in [how Eagle's Wings approaches responsible program growth](/blog/article-responsible-program-growth).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nOur mission is what we try to do each day, and our vision is what we hope our community can become. You can read both in full on the [mission, vision and values](/about#mission) page, and see who we are on the [About page](/about).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/neurodiversity-myths-and-facts.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Neuro-diversity Myths and Facts: Your Questions Answered\"\r\nslug: neurodiversity-myths-and-facts\r\ncategory: Neuro-diversity\r\nexcerpt: \"Clear, respectful answers to common myths about autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability, including what WHO says about how common autism is.\"\r\ndescription: \"Clear, respectful answers to common myths about autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability, including what WHO says about how common autism is.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [myths, autism, adhd, awareness, faq]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/neurodiversity-myths-and-facts.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/neurodiversity-myths-and-facts-thumb.webp\r\nicon: brain\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Autism is not caused by bad parenting, and neuro-diversity is not caused by curses or blame.\"\r\n  - \"About 1 in 100 children worldwide has autism, according to WHO, so it is far from rare.\"\r\n  - \"People with disabilities can learn, work, form relationships and contribute to their communities with the right support.\"\r\n  - \"Accurate information reduces stigma and helps families seek support earlier.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How common is autism?\"\r\n    a: \"The World Health Organization estimates that about 1 in 100 children worldwide has autism. It is a wide spectrum, and support needs vary greatly from person to person. Autism exists in every country and community.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can a person grow out of autism or ADHD?\"\r\n    a: \"Autism is lifelong, though people learn and develop throughout life and support can make daily life easier. ADHD often continues into adulthood, although how it shows can change. A qualified professional can explain what to expect for an individual.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is it rude to ask if someone is autistic?\"\r\n    a: \"It is best not to ask strangers about diagnoses. Instead, be kind and patient. If a person or family chooses to share, listen with respect and ask what helps. Privacy is part of dignity.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"ADHD\"\r\n    publisher: \"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.cdc.gov/adhd/index.html\"\r\n---\r\nMost myths about autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability come from fear and lack of information. The facts are simpler and kinder: these are natural differences in human development, they are common, and they are not anyone's fault. Here are answers to questions families and communities often ask.\r\n\r\n## Is autism rare?\r\n\r\n**Myth:** Autism is rare, so I will never meet an autistic person.\r\n\r\n**Fact:** The World Health Organization estimates that [about 1 in 100 children worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism. It is a wide spectrum, and support needs vary greatly. If you go to school, work, worship or shop, you have almost certainly met autistic people, perhaps without knowing it.\r\n\r\nMany are also adults. Autism does not end at childhood.\r\n\r\n## Is it caused by bad parenting or a curse?\r\n\r\n**Myth:** A child is autistic, has ADHD or has Down syndrome because of something the mother did, or because of witchcraft or a curse.\r\n\r\n**Fact:** No. Parenting style does not cause autism or ADHD. Down syndrome is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21, which happens by chance. Blame hurts families and stops them from seeking help. Mothers, fathers and caregivers deserve support, not judgement.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** If you hear blame or fear spoken about a child, you can gently say, \"This is a difference, not a punishment, and the family needs our support.\"\r\n\r\n## Are people with these conditions unable to learn or work?\r\n\r\n**Myth:** A person with a disability cannot learn, work or live a full life.\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Ability varies widely, and many people learn, work and build independence with the right support and opportunities. Some need help with daily tasks, and others need very little. The WHO estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, around 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. They are a huge part of humanity and of every workforce, school and community.\r\n\r\nAssuming a person cannot do something is often the biggest barrier of all.\r\n\r\n## Can ADHD be fixed by discipline?\r\n\r\n**Myth:** A child with ADHD is simply naughty or lazy and needs stricter discipline.\r\n\r\n**Fact:** ADHD is a difference in how the brain manages attention, activity and impulses. Harsh punishment does not cure it and can harm a child's confidence. Structure, clear routines, movement breaks and encouragement help many children. Decisions about treatment, including any medication, should be made with a doctor.\r\n\r\n## Is there a cure for autism or Down syndrome?\r\n\r\n**Myth:** With the right treatment or prayer, autism or Down syndrome will go away.\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Autism and Down syndrome are lifelong. This is not a reason for despair. Therapy, education, health care and inclusion can help a person develop skills and enjoy life. Be careful of anyone who promises a miracle cure or asks for large payments. Speak to a qualified health professional before trying any treatment.\r\n\r\n## Do autistic people not have feelings?\r\n\r\n**Myth:** Autistic people do not care about others or do not feel emotions.\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Autistic people have feelings, friendships and love, just like everyone else. They may express them differently, for example by sharing a favourite interest, helping with a task or being loyal and honest. Different expression is not missing emotion.\r\n\r\n## Should people with disabilities be kept at home?\r\n\r\n**Myth:** It is better to protect a child or older person with a disability by keeping them at home.\r\n\r\n**Fact:** Isolation harms wellbeing and limits opportunity. The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006, is built on dignity, participation and inclusion. Safe, supported participation in school, play, worship and community life helps people belong. Protection and inclusion can go together with planning and supervision.\r\n\r\n## Myths and facts at a glance\r\n\r\n| Myth | Fact |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Autism is rare | About 1 in 100 children has autism, per WHO |\r\n| Bad parenting causes it | Parenting does not cause autism or ADHD |\r\n| People with disabilities cannot contribute | With support, many learn, work and contribute |\r\n| ADHD is just bad behaviour | It is a difference in attention and impulse control |\r\n| A cure will make it disappear | These are lifelong; support improves quality of life |\r\n| Autistic people lack feelings | They feel deeply and may express it differently |\r\n\r\n## How can I help change these myths?\r\n\r\n1. Share accurate information with family and friends.\r\n2. Use respectful words, and correct harmful ones kindly.\r\n3. Invite people with disabilities to take part, and listen to them.\r\n4. Support families instead of judging them.\r\n5. Ask qualified professionals when you are unsure.\r\n\r\nThis article is general education and not medical advice.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania works to replace myths with understanding through Neuro-diversity awareness on autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability. Learn more on the [Neuro-diversity program page](/programs/program-neurodiversity), browse our [frequently asked questions](/faq), or read about [how everyday inclusion grows from awareness](/blog/article-community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/open-data-and-iati-aid-transparency-explained.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Open Data and the IATI Standard: Questions Answered Simply\"\r\nslug: open-data-and-iati-aid-transparency-explained\r\ncategory: Transparency\r\nexcerpt: \"Plain answers about open data and the IATI aid-transparency standard, and what supporters can reasonably expect from a small non-profit today.\"\r\ndescription: \"Plain answers about open data and the IATI aid-transparency standard, and what supporters can reasonably expect from a small non-profit today.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [open data, iati, transparency, donors]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/open-data-and-iati-aid-transparency-explained.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/open-data-and-iati-aid-transparency-explained-thumb.webp\r\nicon: file-check-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Open data is information shared in a form that others can find, read and reuse.\"\r\n  - \"IATI is a shared standard that helps organisations publish aid information in a consistent way.\"\r\n  - \"Open data has limits: privacy and dignity come before publishing.\"\r\n  - \"Donors can expect general, plain updates, not promises about specific dates or amounts.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Do small non-profits have to use IATI?\"\r\n    a: \"Not necessarily. IATI is a voluntary standard, and many small organisations start with plain, clear reporting on their own websites. What matters first is honesty and clarity. A shared standard can be a later step as capacity grows.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does open data mean publishing personal information?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Responsible open data covers organisational information such as activities and finances. Details about children, families or older people should stay private unless there is clear, informed consent. Privacy comes before publishing.\"\r\n  - q: \"How will donors be kept informed?\"\r\n    a: \"Through reports and updates shared on this website, and through answers to questions sent to the Foundation. This is a general commitment to communicate honestly. It is not a promise about particular dates, amounts or results.\"\r\n  - q: \"Where can I learn more about IATI?\"\r\n    a: \"The official IATI website explains the standard, who uses it and how it works. It is written for many audiences, and its introductory pages are a good place to start.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n  - title: \"UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nOpen data means sharing information in a form that other people can find, read and reuse. IATI, the International Aid Transparency Initiative, is a shared standard that helps organisations publish information about aid in a consistent way. This page answers the common questions in plain language.\r\n\r\n## What is open data, in simple words?\r\n\r\nThink of a notice board in a village. Anyone can walk up and read it. Open data is the same idea for information: it is published where people can reach it, in a form they can use, without needing special permission.\r\n\r\nOpen does not mean careless. Some information should never be public, such as personal details about children or families. Open data is about being clear on the things an organisation should explain, like what it does and how it uses funds.\r\n\r\n## What is IATI?\r\n\r\nIATI stands for the International Aid Transparency Initiative. According to its website, it is a global effort to make information about aid and development easier to see, compare and use. It offers a common standard, which is a shared way of describing things.\r\n\r\nWhy does a shared way matter? If every organisation describes its work in a different style, comparing them is hard. A common format makes information easier to read for donors, researchers, communities and other organisations.\r\n\r\nYou can read the details on the [official IATI website](https://iatistandard.org/en/).\r\n\r\n## Who is it for?\r\n\r\nThe standard exists for several groups:\r\n\r\n- **Donors and supporters**, who want to understand where support goes.\r\n- **Communities**, who have a right to know about work that affects them.\r\n- **Partners and researchers**, who want to learn from many organisations at once.\r\n- **The organisations themselves**, who benefit from being clear and consistent.\r\n\r\n## Does a small non-profit need to publish this way?\r\n\r\nNo. IATI is a voluntary standard, and it was not built only for large agencies. A young, small organisation can start with something simpler: a clear website, plain-language reports and open answers to questions. Consistent honesty is the foundation. A shared standard is one way to build on it as an organisation grows.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Publishing in a technical format does not make an organisation honest. Clear, plain, truthful reporting matters more than any format.\r\n\r\n## What can and cannot be shared?\r\n\r\nResponsible transparency has limits. The table below shows the general idea.\r\n\r\n| Usually suitable to share | Usually kept private |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| What programs exist and their purpose | Names and photos of children without consent |\r\n| General information on how funds are used | Health or family details about individuals |\r\n| What was learned, including difficulties | Home addresses and private contact details |\r\n| How to raise a concern or ask a question | Anything a person asked not to be shared |\r\n\r\nThis balance reflects the dignity and privacy that the UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) puts at the centre. Our article on [why consent matters in non-profit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) goes deeper.\r\n\r\n## Why does open data matter to the people served?\r\n\r\nTransparency is not only for donors. Families and communities have an interest in knowing how an organisation works, what it promises and how they can raise a concern. Open information helps them ask questions with confidence, and helps organisations stay answerable to the people they exist to serve.\r\n\r\n## Does open data prove an organisation is good?\r\n\r\nNo. Data can be open and still be incomplete, out of date or badly explained. It is a tool for asking better questions, not a certificate of quality.\r\n\r\nThat is why numbers should be read with care. Our explainer on [measuring outcomes responsibly](/blog/article-measuring-outcomes-responsibly-small-honest-numbers) describes what a good number looks like.\r\n\r\n## How can donors expect to be kept informed?\r\n\r\nSupporters should be able to understand what an organisation is doing without having to chase it. In general terms, a donor can reasonably expect:\r\n\r\n1. **Plain-language reports** that explain the work, the learning and the use of funds.\r\n2. **Stated limits**, including what is not yet known.\r\n3. **A way to ask questions**, and a respectful, timely answer where possible.\r\n4. **Corrections**, if something published turns out to be wrong.\r\n5. **Respect for privacy**, so that people are never exposed to make a report more moving.\r\n\r\nThese are general commitments about how to communicate. They are not promises about particular dates, amounts or results.\r\n\r\n## How can you check an organisation's openness yourself?\r\n\r\nYou do not need technical skills. Try these simple checks:\r\n\r\n- Can you find a report or update without searching for long?\r\n- Does it explain what was done, in ordinary words?\r\n- Does it say what was hard or unfinished?\r\n- Is there a clear contact route?\r\n- Does it treat people with dignity?\r\n\r\nIf the answers are mostly yes, that is a good sign. If not, ask a polite question and see how it responds.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nThe Foundation was founded in 2025 and does not currently publish numeric results. It intends to share reporting openly, with care for privacy and consent, on this website as it becomes available. If you have a question about how we share information, please [contact us](/contact).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/partner-due-diligence-checklist-both-sides.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"A Partner Due-Diligence Checklist for Both Sides\"\r\nslug: partner-due-diligence-checklist-both-sides\r\ncategory: Partnerships\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist that companies and non-profits can both use to check purpose, safeguarding, transparency and fit before agreeing to work together.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist that companies and non-profits can both use to check purpose, safeguarding, transparency and fit before agreeing to work together.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [partnerships, checklist, safeguarding, transparency]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: stockHandshake\r\nthumb: stockHandshakeThumb\r\nicon: handshake\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Due diligence is a two-way process: companies check non-profits, and non-profits check companies.\"\r\n  - \"Key areas are purpose, safeguarding, transparency, capacity and fit.\"\r\n  - \"Ask for clear answers, and accept that some things take time to show.\"\r\n  - \"A checklist supports good judgment. It does not replace conversation or professional advice.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is due diligence in a partnership?\"\r\n    a: \"It is the careful checking that both sides do before agreeing to work together. It covers purpose, reputation, safeguards, finances and capacity. The aim is to build trust and avoid surprises, not to create obstacles.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should a non-profit also check a company?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. A non-profit is responsible for the people it serves, so it is right to ask a prospective partner about their values, expectations and how they will use its name and stories.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do we need a lawyer?\"\r\n    a: \"For binding agreements, it is wise to seek qualified legal advice. This checklist is general educational guidance only and does not give legal advice.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Violence against children\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children\"\r\n---\r\nBefore two organisations agree to work together, both should check that their purpose, values, safeguards and capacity fit. This is called due diligence, and it works best when it goes in both directions. The checklist below gives companies and non-profits a shared set of questions to ask.\r\n\r\n## Why should both sides check?\r\n\r\nCompanies want to be sure their support is used well. Non-profits want to be sure a partner will treat the people they serve with respect. Each side carries responsibility, so each side has a right to ask questions.\r\n\r\nGood due diligence is not a sign of distrust. It shows care, and it usually makes the relationship stronger. Ask in a friendly way, explain why you are asking, and be ready to answer the same questions yourself.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 1: Purpose and values\r\n\r\n- Do we both understand what the partnership is meant to achieve?\r\n- Are our values compatible, especially on dignity and the rights of people with disabilities?\r\n- Does the plan reflect the principles of the [UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), such as dignity, participation and non-discrimination?\r\n- Is the aim to help people, and not mainly to gain publicity?\r\n\r\n## Checklist 2: Safeguarding and consent\r\n\r\nPeople who depend on others for care can be at greater risk of harm. The World Health Organization's information on [violence against children](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children) is a reminder of why protective steps matter.\r\n\r\n- Is there a written safeguarding approach, and do all involved understand it?\r\n- Will anyone who meets children, older people or people with disabilities follow it?\r\n- How will consent be handled for photos, stories and personal information?\r\n- Is there a clear, safe way to raise a concern?\r\n\r\nOur article on [preparing community events with safeguarding in mind](/blog/article-preparing-community-events-safeguarding-ready) offers related ideas.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 3: Transparency and records\r\n\r\n- Can each side explain, in plain language, how resources are received and used?\r\n- Are records kept, and can relevant information be shared on request?\r\n- Does reporting state its limits and unknowns, not only successes?\r\n- Are there conflicts of interest that should be disclosed?\r\n\r\nThe [IATI Standard](https://iatistandard.org/en/) is an example of how the wider sector approaches openness about resources and activities. A small organisation does not need complex systems, but it should be able to explain what it does clearly.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 4: Capacity and fit\r\n\r\n- Does each side have the time, people and skills to do what it promises?\r\n- Is the timeline realistic, and can the work continue after the first burst of energy?\r\n- Will the partnership avoid overloading families, caregivers or staff?\r\n- Is the offered contribution actually wanted and useful?\r\n\r\n## Checklist 5: Communication and branding\r\n\r\n- Who speaks for each organisation, and how are decisions made?\r\n- How will each side's name, logo and words be used, and who approves this?\r\n- Will people with disabilities and their families be portrayed with dignity?\r\n- How often will we check in, and how will disagreements be handled?\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** A partner that welcomes these questions is usually a good sign. Be cautious if questions are avoided or if someone insists on speed.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 6: Exit and review\r\n\r\n- Can either side pause or end the partnership respectfully?\r\n- Is there a point where we review how things are going?\r\n- What happens to shared materials, data and commitments if the relationship ends?\r\n- Is there a plan so that people who rely on the work are not left suddenly without support?\r\n\r\n## How to use this checklist\r\n\r\nTreat it as a conversation guide. Go through it together, note where answers are not yet clear, and decide who will find out more. Some answers will only become clear over time.\r\n\r\nIf a question touches contracts, taxes, employment or other legal matters, ask a qualified professional. This checklist is general and educational, and does not replace legal or financial advice.\r\n\r\nFor an overview of the different forms partnership can take, see [ways organisations can partner](/blog/article-ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us). To understand what usually happens next, read [how a partnership starts and what an agreement covers](/blog/article-how-a-partnership-starts-and-what-an-agreement-covers).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nWe welcome questions from prospective partners, and we ask our own. You can [contact us](/contact) to start a conversation. We would rather move slowly and get it right than promise too much too soon.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/partnership-faq-for-companies-and-ngos.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Partnership Questions From Companies and NGOs, Answered\"\r\nslug: partnership-faq-for-companies-and-ngos\r\ncategory: Partnerships\r\nexcerpt: \"Plain answers to common questions companies and other NGOs ask when considering a partnership with a disability and ageing non-profit.\"\r\ndescription: \"Plain answers to common questions companies and other NGOs ask when considering a partnership with a disability and ageing non-profit.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [partnerships, faq, ngo, companies]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/partnership-faq-for-companies-and-ngos.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/partnership-faq-for-companies-and-ngos-thumb.webp\r\nicon: handshake\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Partnership can take many forms, and it need not involve money.\"\r\n  - \"Good partners ask about needs first and commit to steady, respectful support.\"\r\n  - \"People's dignity, consent and safety come before publicity.\"\r\n  - \"The best next step is a conversation through the Contact page.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Who can partner with us?\"\r\n    a: \"Companies, other NGOs, community groups, professionals and institutions can all explore partnership. What matters most is shared respect for the dignity of people with disabilities and older people.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do we have to give money?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Contributions can include skills, materials, time or expertise. The key is that the offer matches a real need and can be kept up.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can we use your name in our marketing?\"\r\n    a: \"Only with agreement. Use of any organisation's name, logo or images should be discussed and approved in advance, and people served must never be shown without informed consent.\"\r\n  - q: \"How do we begin?\"\r\n    a: \"Use the Contact page to introduce yourself. A first conversation about needs and fit is the usual starting point.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Ageing and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ageing-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nCompanies and NGOs considering a partnership often ask the same questions: who can partner, what can we give, and how do we start? The short answer is that many kinds of organisation can help, in many ways, provided the work is respectful and clearly agreed. Below are plain answers to the questions we expect most often.\r\n\r\n## Who can be a partner?\r\n\r\nBusinesses of any size, other non-profits, community groups, schools, professionals and institutions can all be partners. You do not need to be large or well known.\r\n\r\nWhat matters is shared purpose. The World Health Organization estimates that [about 16% of the world's population experience significant disability](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health), and older people and caregivers also need support. There is room for many kinds of contribution.\r\n\r\n## What can we offer?\r\n\r\nCommon forms of contribution include:\r\n\r\n- **Goods or services** that meet a described need.\r\n- **Professional skills** such as design, accounting or training.\r\n- **Funding** for an activity or for running costs.\r\n- **Time**, for example through staff volunteering.\r\n\r\nWe describe these in our explainer on [ways organisations can partner](/blog/article-ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us). Many partnerships combine more than one.\r\n\r\n## Do we need to give money?\r\n\r\nNo. Money is helpful, but it is not the only way to contribute. A steady offer of skills or time can be just as valuable. The important thing is to ask what is needed and to offer only what you can keep doing.\r\n\r\n## What should we expect to be asked?\r\n\r\nA responsible non-profit will ask about your purpose, your values, how you handle safeguarding, and what you hope to gain. Expect questions about how you would use its name and any photos or stories.\r\n\r\nThese questions are a sign of care. They also work in your favour, because you can ask the same of us. Our [partner due-diligence checklist](/blog/article-partner-due-diligence-checklist-both-sides) lists questions for both sides.\r\n\r\n## Can we visit, take photos or share stories?\r\n\r\nVisits, photos and stories involve real people, often children, older people and people who rely on others for care. Their dignity and privacy come first.\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) is built on respect for dignity, autonomy and participation. In practice this means informed, freely given consent, and a willingness to accept that some things will not be shared. Our article on [why consent matters in nonprofit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explains more.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Partners should never expect access to people as a right. Visits and images are agreed carefully and in advance.\r\n\r\n## Is a one-day project welcome?\r\n\r\nSometimes, if it responds to a real request and is well prepared. But steady, patient support usually helps more than a single event, and it is easier on families and caregivers. Our perspective piece on [doing CSR well](/blog/article-csr-done-well-beyond-drop-in-projects) looks at this in more detail.\r\n\r\n## How does a partnership start?\r\n\r\nUsually with a simple conversation. You introduce your organisation, listen to what is needed, check that you fit together, and then agree a modest first step. Later, the agreement is written down. We describe this in general terms in [how a partnership starts and what an agreement covers](/blog/article-how-a-partnership-starts-and-what-an-agreement-covers).\r\n\r\nThat article is general guidance. It is not a statement of legal terms, and binding agreements should be reviewed by a qualified legal professional.\r\n\r\n## Will we see results?\r\n\r\nWe prefer honesty over big promises. We will not claim results we cannot support, and we ask partners to do the same. Good reporting tells you what was agreed, what was done and what was learned, including what did not work.\r\n\r\n## What about the people we serve, who are they?\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation works to improve the lives of people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and supports their families and caregivers. Support for older people includes those living with dementia. The WHO notes that [more than 55 million people worldwide live with dementia](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia), which shows why family and caregiver support matters. You can learn more about our [programs](/programs).\r\n\r\n## Who do we contact?\r\n\r\nUse the [Contact page](/contact) to reach us. You can also find the Foundation on Instagram at @Eaglewings.tz.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nWe are a young non-profit based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, and we hope to work with organisations that share our respect for people with disabilities and older people. If you have a question that is not covered here, please [contact us](/contact) or look at our general [FAQ](/faq).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/planning-safe-swimming-exercise-day-young-people.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Planning a Safe Swimming or Exercise Day for Young People\"\r\nslug: planning-safe-swimming-exercise-day-young-people\r\ncategory: Events\r\nexcerpt: \"Common questions about planning a safe swimming or exercise day for young people: supervision, medical information, consent and emergency planning.\"\r\ndescription: \"Common questions about planning a safe swimming or exercise day for young people: supervision, medical information, consent and emergency planning.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [swimming, exercise, safety, young people]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: galleryExtra2\r\nthumb: galleryExtra2\r\nicon: calendar-days\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Water and exercise activities need close, constant supervision by capable adults.\"\r\n  - \"Gather medical information and written consent from parents or guardians before the day.\"\r\n  - \"Have an emergency plan that everyone knows, with a person trained in first aid and rescue.\"\r\n  - \"Seek qualified professional advice for anyone with specific health or safety needs.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How much exercise should young people do?\"\r\n    a: \"The World Health Organization recommends that children and adolescents average about 60 minutes of physical activity a day. Individual needs vary, especially for young people with disabilities or health conditions, so ask a health professional for advice.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who should supervise a swimming activity?\"\r\n    a: \"Supervisors should be capable adults who are alert, present and focused on the water. They should not be distracted by other tasks. Get advice from qualified lifeguards or instructors about how many supervisors are appropriate for your group and location.\"\r\n  - q: \"What medical information should we collect?\"\r\n    a: \"Ask parents or guardians about conditions, allergies, mobility needs, and anything that affects safety in water or exercise. Keep it private and share it only with people who need it. A doctor can advise on whether an activity is suitable.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is consent from young people needed as well as parents?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes, where possible. Written permission from a parent or guardian is important, and the young person should also understand the activity and be free to say they do not want to take part or want to stop.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Physical activity\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n---\r\nA safe swimming or exercise day depends on four things: close supervision, up-to-date medical information, informed consent and a rehearsed emergency plan. This guide answers common questions in general terms. It is not medical or lifesaving advice. Always involve qualified professionals, such as a doctor, therapist, lifeguard or instructor, when you plan activities in or near water.\r\n\r\n## Why are swimming and exercise days worth planning carefully?\r\n\r\nPhysical activity supports health, confidence and enjoyment. The World Health Organization recommends that children and adolescents average about 60 minutes of physical activity a day, and it stresses that people living with disability can benefit too, with adaptations. Water and movement can also be a happy way to spend time with others.\r\n\r\nAt the same time, water is a risk for anyone, and exercise can be tiring or unsafe for some people. Good planning lets young people enjoy the day, and helps parents and carers feel confident about the arrangements.\r\n\r\n## What supervision is needed?\r\n\r\nSupervision is the most important safeguard. Water activities need constant, close attention.\r\n\r\n- Supervisors should be capable adults who are alert and focused on the participants.\r\n- They should not be looking after food, photos or other tasks at the same time.\r\n- Take advice from a qualified lifeguard or instructor about how many supervisors your group needs.\r\n- Make sure someone always knows who is in the water and who has left.\r\n- Give helpers clear zones and roles, and confirm handovers when they change.\r\n\r\nYoung people should never be left alone in or near water, even in shallow areas.\r\n\r\n## What medical information should we gather?\r\n\r\nAsk parents or guardians to share relevant information well before the day, using a simple form. Useful questions include:\r\n\r\n1. Are there health conditions, allergies or medicines that affect safety?\r\n2. Does the young person have mobility or communication needs?\r\n3. Are there fears, triggers or sensory needs around water or crowds?\r\n4. Who should we call in an emergency, and is that person reachable?\r\n5. Has a doctor or therapist advised anything about swimming or exercise?\r\n\r\nKeep the information private and share it only with those who need it. Never advise anyone to start, stop or change medicine. That is a decision for a doctor.\r\n\r\n## How do we handle consent?\r\n\r\nConsent has two parts. Parents or guardians should give written permission after they understand the plan. Young people should also be told what will happen, in plain words, and be free to say no, rest or stop at any time.\r\n\r\nNever pressure or tease a young person who is nervous about water. Invite them to watch, join for a short time or take part in a different way. Photos and videos need separate permission, and our article on [why consent matters in nonprofit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explains why.\r\n\r\n## What should the emergency plan include?\r\n\r\nWrite a short plan and make sure every helper has read it.\r\n\r\n- A named person who is trained in first aid, and a clear first-aid kit.\r\n- A trained person or lifeguard for water activities, where possible.\r\n- A working phone, a list of emergency numbers and directions to the nearest medical help.\r\n- A way to account for everyone, such as a register at the start, during and at the end.\r\n- A clear signal for gathering the group quickly.\r\n- A plan for informing parents or guardians if there is an incident.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Practise the plan with your helpers before the day. A plan that has only been written down is much harder to follow in a real emergency.\r\n\r\n## What else should we prepare for?\r\n\r\n### The environment\r\n\r\nCheck the pool or water area beforehand for hazards, depth, slippery surfaces and access for people who use mobility aids. Choose sensible timing to avoid extreme heat, and arrange shade and drinking water.\r\n\r\n### Adapting to different needs\r\n\r\nPeople move differently. Offer choices, such as gentle games, floating, walking in the water with support, or seated exercise. Rest is part of the activity. Ask a physiotherapist or therapist for guidance if someone has specific needs. Our articles on [making events sensory-friendly](/blog/article-making-community-events-sensory-friendly) and [accessibility for community events](/blog/article-accessibility-universal-design-checklist-community-events) give further ideas.\r\n\r\n### Dignity and privacy\r\n\r\nProvide private changing spaces, and respect modesty and comfort. Helpers should follow safeguarding practice, use respectful language and never leave a young person alone with an adult without appropriate oversight.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania includes hydrotherapy, swimming and exercise among its public activities, always with the principle that supervision and professional guidance come first. To understand how movement and learning connect, explore our [education and therapy guidance program](/programs/program-education-therapy).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/preparing-community-events-safeguarding-ready.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Preparing Community Events That Are Clear and Safeguarding-Ready\"\r\nslug: preparing-community-events-safeguarding-ready\r\ncategory: Events\r\nexcerpt: \"A plain-language guide to planning community events with clear roles, welcoming information and basic safeguarding, so everyone can take part safely.\"\r\ndescription: \"A plain-language guide to planning community events with clear roles, welcoming information and basic safeguarding, so everyone can take part safely.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [events, safeguarding, planning, inclusion]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/preparing-community-events-safeguarding-ready.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/preparing-community-events-safeguarding-ready-thumb.webp\r\nicon: calendar-days\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"A safeguarding-ready event is one where roles, information and responses to problems are agreed before anyone arrives.\"\r\n  - \"Clear communication in advance helps families, carers and volunteers know what to expect.\"\r\n  - \"Every event needs named people with responsibility for welfare, first aid and emergencies.\"\r\n  - \"Photos, personal details and consent need a plan, not an afterthought.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What does safeguarding mean at a community event?\"\r\n    a: \"Safeguarding means taking reasonable steps to protect people from harm, especially children, older people and people with disabilities. At an event, that includes clear supervision, safe spaces, trained helpers, respectful photo practice and a known way to raise concerns.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do small events need a plan too?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. A small gathering still involves people who may need support. A short written plan covering roles, emergency steps, consent and who to call takes little time and prevents confusion when something unexpected happens.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who should be responsible for welfare on the day?\"\r\n    a: \"Name one or two people as the welfare contact. Everyone should know who they are and how to reach them. They should be free from other tasks, so they can respond calmly if someone is worried or unwell.\"\r\n  - q: \"How should we handle photos and personal information?\"\r\n    a: \"Ask for clear, informed permission before taking or sharing photos, and let people say no without pressure. Collect only the personal details you really need, keep them private and share them only with those who must know.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Violence against children\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children\"\r\n---\r\nA community event is clear and safeguarding-ready when three things are settled before the day: who does what, what people can expect, and what happens if something goes wrong. This guide explains those ideas in plain language. It is general planning guidance, not legal advice, and it does not describe any specific event.\r\n\r\n## Why do clarity and safeguarding belong together?\r\n\r\nConfusion is one of the biggest risks at any gathering. When nobody knows who is in charge, who is watching the children, or where to go if someone feels unwell, small problems grow quickly.\r\n\r\nSafeguarding is the habit of protecting people from harm. It matters most for children, older people and people with disabilities, who may depend on others for support. The World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, around 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. That means almost any community gathering will include people with a range of support needs, whether or not anyone mentions them.\r\n\r\nClear planning is how that care becomes practical. It also reflects the principles of the UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html): dignity, participation and inclusion.\r\n\r\n## What should be decided before the event?\r\n\r\nStart with a short written plan. It does not need to be long. It needs to be shared with everyone who is helping.\r\n\r\n### Purpose and audience\r\n\r\nWrite one or two sentences on why the event is happening and who it is for. This keeps the programme focused and helps you judge whether the venue, timing and helpers suit the people you expect.\r\n\r\n### Roles and responsibilities\r\n\r\nGive every helper a clear role. A simple structure works well:\r\n\r\n- **Lead organiser:** makes final decisions and is the main contact.\r\n- **Welfare contact:** looks after anyone who is worried, distressed or unwell.\r\n- **Registration helpers:** welcome people and record only necessary details.\r\n- **First-aid contact:** a person who is trained, or a link to a trained professional.\r\n- **Floating helpers:** support people, guide them around the space and keep walkways clear.\r\n\r\n### Limits on what you can offer\r\n\r\nBe honest about what the event can and cannot provide. If you do not have medical staff, say so. If a space is not step-free, say so. Accurate information lets families choose for themselves.\r\n\r\n## How do we communicate clearly with families and carers?\r\n\r\nPeople take part more confidently when they know what to expect. Share information early, in plain words, and offer more than one way to reach you, such as a phone call as well as a message.\r\n\r\nInclude these points in your invitation:\r\n\r\n1. What the event is and who is welcome.\r\n2. Where it is, how to get there and how the venue is laid out.\r\n3. The order of the day, in simple steps.\r\n4. How to ask for support, for example a quiet space, seating or help with mobility.\r\n5. Who to contact before and during the event.\r\n\r\nUse short sentences and avoid jargon. If many people speak Kiswahili at home, consider offering the key information in both Kiswahili and English.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Ask people to tell you about support needs in advance, but never make it a condition of attending. Some people will only mention their needs when they arrive, and that is fine.\r\n\r\n## What does safeguarding look like on the day?\r\n\r\nGood safeguarding is mostly ordinary good practice, done consistently.\r\n\r\n### Supervision and safe spaces\r\n\r\nChildren and people who need support should never be left without a responsible adult. Agree how helpers will share responsibility for areas such as entrances, toilets, food and quiet spaces. Keep exits clear and make sure helpers know where they are.\r\n\r\n### Helper conduct\r\n\r\nVolunteers and staff should understand that they must treat everyone with respect, avoid being alone and unobserved with a child, and speak up if they see something worrying. Brief them before the event and give them the welfare contact's details. Our article on [responsible volunteering in community work](/blog/article-responsible-volunteering-in-community-work) covers this in more depth.\r\n\r\n### Photos and personal information\r\n\r\nAsk permission before taking or sharing photos, and make it easy to say no. For children or people who may not be able to give informed consent, seek permission from a parent, guardian or trusted representative. Why this matters is explored in [why consent matters in nonprofit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling).\r\n\r\nCollect only the information you need, store it privately and delete it when it is no longer required.\r\n\r\n## What if something goes wrong?\r\n\r\nPlan for problems so that nobody has to invent a response under stress.\r\n\r\n- Decide who calls emergency services and who stays with the person who needs help.\r\n- Keep a phone charged and a list of key contacts in one place.\r\n- Agree how helpers report a concern, and who they report it to.\r\n- Write down what happened after any incident, factually and privately.\r\n- Take every concern seriously and never dismiss a child or adult who speaks up.\r\n\r\nIf there is a concern about a child's safety, it should be passed to the appropriate local authorities or child protection services. Seek qualified advice about the correct process where you live.\r\n\r\n## Checking the plan afterwards\r\n\r\nAfter the event, gather the helpers for a short review. Ask what worked, what felt confusing, and what should change. Write down two or three improvements and keep them for next time. The aim is steady progress, not perfection.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania works with people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and their families and caregivers. Clear, respectful and safe gatherings are part of how community inclusion becomes real. You can learn how to help responsibly on our [volunteers page](/volunteers).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/preparing-for-a-therapy-or-assessment-appointment.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Preparing for a Therapy or Assessment Appointment: A Checklist\"\r\nslug: preparing-for-a-therapy-or-assessment-appointment\r\ncategory: Family guidance\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist of what to bring, what to note and which questions to ask, so you feel prepared and heard at your child's therapy or assessment visit.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist of what to bring, what to note and which questions to ask, so you feel prepared and heard at your child's therapy or assessment visit.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [assessment, therapy, checklist, parenting, appointments]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: /photos/blog/preparing-for-a-therapy-or-assessment-appointment.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/preparing-for-a-therapy-or-assessment-appointment-thumb.webp\r\nicon: book-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"A little preparation helps you explain your child's strengths and needs clearly.\"\r\n  - \"Write down your questions and observations before the visit, because stress makes memory unreliable.\"\r\n  - \"Prepare your child gently, in simple words and at a pace that suits them.\"\r\n  - \"Always ask for next steps in writing, and follow qualified professional advice.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What should I bring to an assessment?\"\r\n    a: \"Bring any earlier reports, health or school records you have, a list of your questions, notes about your child's daily life, and something comforting for your child. If you are unsure what the service needs, ask when you book.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should my child know about the appointment in advance?\"\r\n    a: \"Usually yes, in simple and honest words that suit their age and understanding. Explaining who they will meet and what will happen can reduce worry. Some children need a picture routine or a short practice at home first.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if I forget to ask something important?\"\r\n    a: \"It happens to everyone. Ask how you can follow up, such as by phone or a later visit. Keeping a running list of questions between appointments makes this less likely in future.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Children with disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n---\r\nA short checklist can turn a stressful appointment into a useful conversation. Before you go, gather your records, write down what you have noticed, list your questions and prepare your child gently. This article gives a step-by-step checklist you can adapt for a therapy visit or an assessment.\r\n\r\n## Why does preparation help?\r\n\r\nAppointments can feel rushed and emotional. You may be worried, tired or travelling a long way through Dar es Salaam traffic. In that state it is easy to forget the very thing you meant to say.\r\n\r\nPreparation does not mean being perfect. It means giving the professional a clear picture of your child, and giving yourself a calm way to take part. You know your child better than anyone, and your observations are valuable.\r\n\r\n## The checklist before the day\r\n\r\n### Gather what you already have\r\n\r\n- Earlier reports from doctors, therapists or teachers, if any exist\r\n- Health records and a list of any medicines your child takes (bring the packaging or the doctor's note, and never change medicines without medical advice)\r\n- School reports or teacher comments\r\n- Your child's name, date of birth and any identification the service asks for\r\n- Contact details for other people involved in your child's care\r\n\r\n### Write down what you have noticed\r\n\r\nSpend a few days jotting simple notes. Focus on what you actually see and hear, not on labels.\r\n\r\n- What is your child good at, and what do they enjoy?\r\n- What do they find hard, and when does it happen?\r\n- How do they communicate what they want or need?\r\n- How do they sleep, eat and move through the day?\r\n- What helps when they are upset or overwhelmed?\r\n- What worries you most right now?\r\n\r\nStarting with strengths is important. An assessment is not only about difficulties. It should describe the whole child.\r\n\r\n### Write your questions\r\n\r\nQuestions are easy to forget under pressure. Write them on paper and put the most important ones first. Some ideas are below.\r\n\r\n## Questions worth asking\r\n\r\n**About the visit itself**\r\n\r\n- What will happen today, and how long will it take?\r\n- Can I stay with my child throughout?\r\n- Will I receive a written summary?\r\n\r\n**About what you hear**\r\n\r\n- Can you explain that in simple words?\r\n- What are my child's strengths, as you see them?\r\n- What does this mean for daily life at home and at school?\r\n\r\n**About next steps**\r\n\r\n- What should we try first, and how will I know if it is helping?\r\n- Can you show me how to do the exercise or activity?\r\n- When should we come back, and who should I contact with questions in between?\r\n- Are there other professionals we should see?\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Bring a second adult if you can. One person can listen and ask questions while the other writes notes. If that is not possible, ask permission to write during the conversation.\r\n\r\n## Preparing your child\r\n\r\nHow you prepare your child depends on their age, communication and needs. A few gentle ideas:\r\n\r\n1. **Explain in simple, honest words.** For example: \"We are going to meet someone who helps children learn and play. You will not be in trouble.\"\r\n2. **Describe the steps.** Tell them where you will go, who they may meet and what may happen.\r\n3. **Use pictures or a simple schedule** if your child understands routines better that way.\r\n4. **Pack comfort items.** A favourite toy, a snack that is allowed, a drink of water and a quiet activity can help.\r\n5. **Plan the day around rest.** Try not to schedule other demanding activities right before or after.\r\n\r\nIf your child finds new places difficult, ask whether a short, calm visit beforehand is possible.\r\n\r\n## During the appointment\r\n\r\n- Ask the professional to slow down whenever needed.\r\n- Say if something does not match what you see at home. Your insight matters.\r\n- Speak about your child with respect, as they may be listening.\r\n- Ask for any recommendation to be written down.\r\n- Before you leave, repeat the plan in your own words to check you understood.\r\n\r\nIf you feel overwhelmed, it is fine to say so and ask for a short pause.\r\n\r\n## After the appointment\r\n\r\nTake a few minutes while it is fresh.\r\n\r\n- Write down the main points and the next steps.\r\n- Choose one small action to begin this week.\r\n- Share the plan, in simple form, with other adults who care for your child, such as grandparents or a teacher.\r\n- Give your child a kind word or a small treat that suits them. Attending is hard work for them too.\r\n- Look after yourself. It is common to feel relief, sadness, worry or all of them together. Talking to someone you trust helps.\r\n\r\n## What a checklist cannot do\r\n\r\nThis checklist is general education. It cannot diagnose or decide what your child needs. Only qualified professionals can carry out an assessment and recommend therapy. If you are concerned about your child's development or health, please seek a qualified assessment as soon as you can, since early support is one of the ideas at the heart of good care.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania helps families understand education and therapy guidance in plain language, so they feel prepared and confident. Learn more on our [Education and Therapy Guidance page](/programs/program-education-therapy). If the words you hear at appointments are new, our [plain-language glossary of common terms](/blog/article-understanding-common-terms-in-therapy-and-assessment) can help. You may also like [Making Therapy and Education Guidance Easier to Understand](/blog/article-making-therapy-education-guidance-easier-to-understand).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/protecting-families-personal-information-online.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Protecting Families' Personal Information Online\"\r\nslug: protecting-families-personal-information-online\r\ncategory: Safeguarding\r\nexcerpt: \"A plain checklist for families, carers and volunteers on keeping personal details, photos and messages about children and older people safe online.\"\r\ndescription: \"A plain checklist for families, carers and volunteers on keeping personal details, photos and messages about children and older people safe online.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [privacy, online-safety, consent, photos]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: /photos/blog/protecting-families-personal-information-online.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/protecting-families-personal-information-online-thumb.webp\r\nicon: shield-check\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Share less, share with fewer people and share only when there is a clear reason.\"\r\n  - \"Photos can reveal more than you expect, including faces, places, uniforms and routines.\"\r\n  - \"Ask permission before posting about another person, and respect a no.\"\r\n  - \"You can ask an organisation what it holds about you and how it uses it.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Is it safe to post photos of children online?\"\r\n    a: \"It carries risks, because images can be copied and shared beyond your control. Think about who can see the photo, what it reveals and whether the child would be comfortable with it later. Always ask permission before posting other people's children.\"\r\n  - q: \"What details should never be posted?\"\r\n    a: \"Avoid home addresses, phone numbers, school names, daily routes, medical details and anything that shows when a person is alone. Together, these details can help the wrong person find or target someone.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should volunteers use personal phones for photos?\"\r\n    a: \"It is safer to follow the organisation's rules. Many organisations ask helpers to avoid taking photos on personal devices, or to move them to an approved place straight away and then delete them from the personal phone.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can I ask for a photo to be removed?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. You can ask the organisation or the person who posted it to take it down. A responsible organisation should remove it where it can, explain what it has done, and not make you justify your request.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Violence against children\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nTo protect a family's personal information online, share only what is needed, keep private details out of public posts, ask before sharing anyone else's photo, and use strong privacy settings. This checklist puts those habits in plain language. It is general guidance, not legal advice, and it does not describe the law in any particular country.\r\n\r\n## Why does personal information need protection?\r\n\r\nFamilies of children, older people and people with disabilities often share details to get help: names, health needs, routines and photos. That is sensible, but the same details can be misused if they reach the wrong people.\r\n\r\nPeople with disabilities and their carers can face extra risks if private details become public. A person's dignity and safety are part of their rights, and the UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) emphasises respect and autonomy. Privacy is one way of respecting them.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 1: Before you post anything\r\n\r\n- [ ] Do I have a clear reason for sharing this?\r\n- [ ] Is it about me, or about someone else? If it is about someone else, have I asked?\r\n- [ ] Would this person be comfortable with it in a year, or in ten years?\r\n- [ ] Have I read it as a stranger would, looking for clues about where the person lives, studies or spends time?\r\n- [ ] Am I sure that nothing in the background gives away a location?\r\n\r\nIf the answer to any of these is no or unsure, wait.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 2: Details to keep out of public posts\r\n\r\nAvoid sharing these openly:\r\n\r\n- home address, street names or landmarks near home\r\n- phone numbers and email addresses\r\n- names of schools, classes or regular meeting places\r\n- medical or diagnosis details\r\n- daily routines, travel routes and times when someone is alone\r\n- identity documents, or anything that shows them\r\n\r\nEven when each piece looks harmless, several small pieces together can give a clear picture.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 3: Photos and videos\r\n\r\nPhotos carry a lot of information. A face, a uniform, a street sign or a familiar building can identify a person or place.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Ask for permission before taking a photo of another person.\r\n- [ ] For a child, or an adult who may not be able to consent alone, ask a parent, guardian or trusted representative, and also listen to the person.\r\n- [ ] Explain where the photo will appear and who may see it.\r\n- [ ] Respect a no, and never take a photo of someone who is upset or in a private moment such as personal care.\r\n- [ ] Do not add names, or details that could identify someone, unless they have clearly agreed.\r\n- [ ] Remember that people can change their minds, and be ready to delete.\r\n\r\nOur article on [why consent matters in nonprofit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explains this in more depth.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Before posting a group photo, ask yourself if everyone in it has agreed. If you cannot be sure, choose another photo.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 4: Accounts and devices\r\n\r\n- [ ] Use a strong, different password for each account, and do not share it.\r\n- [ ] Review the privacy settings on social media, so posts are visible only to people you know.\r\n- [ ] Be careful with public group chats, which can include people you do not know.\r\n- [ ] Keep phones locked with a code or fingerprint.\r\n- [ ] Be cautious about links and messages from unknown senders, especially those asking for personal details or money.\r\n- [ ] Log out of shared or borrowed devices.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 5: When you give information to an organisation\r\n\r\n- [ ] Ask why they need it and how they will use it.\r\n- [ ] Give only what is necessary.\r\n- [ ] Ask who will see it and how it is stored.\r\n- [ ] Ask how you can correct it, or ask for it to be removed.\r\n- [ ] Read the privacy information. You can read ours on the [privacy page](/privacy).\r\n\r\nA responsible organisation should collect only what it needs, keep it secure, and share it only with those who must know.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 6: For volunteers and helpers\r\n\r\n- [ ] Follow the organisation's rules on photos and messages.\r\n- [ ] Do not share names, photos or stories about the people you support on your own accounts.\r\n- [ ] Do not contact children privately online outside the organisation's approved channels.\r\n- [ ] Report any concern about how information has been used.\r\n\r\nRead more about the wider role of helpers in [safeguarding basics for community organisations](/blog/article-safeguarding-basics-for-community-organisations).\r\n\r\n## What if something has already been shared?\r\n\r\nDo not panic. Ask the person who posted it to remove it, explain why, and keep a record of your request. If the person is a child, or someone may be at risk, tell the organisation's leadership and, where needed, the relevant local authorities. See [how to raise a safeguarding concern safely](/blog/article-how-to-raise-a-safeguarding-concern-safely).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania works with people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and their families and caregivers. Respecting families' privacy is part of respecting their dignity. Please see our [privacy page](/privacy) for how information is handled, or [contact us](/contact) if you have questions or want to raise a concern about your information.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/respectful-language-about-disability-guide.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"A Practical Guide to Respectful Language About Disability\"\r\nslug: respectful-language-about-disability-guide\r\ncategory: Inclusion\r\nexcerpt: \"Simple do-and-say-instead guidance for talking and writing about disability with dignity, including person-first and identity-first language and what to ask.\"\r\ndescription: \"Simple do-and-say-instead guidance for talking and writing about disability with dignity, including person-first and identity-first language and what to ask.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [language, disability, respect, communication]\r\ntemplate: how-to\r\nimage: /photos/blog/respectful-language-about-disability-guide.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/respectful-language-about-disability-guide-thumb.webp\r\nicon: megaphone\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Words shape how people are seen. Choose words that describe a person, not a problem.\"\r\n  - \"Both person-first and identity-first language are used; when unsure, ask the person.\"\r\n  - \"Avoid pity, miracle and deficit framing, and avoid words such as handicapped or victim.\"\r\n  - \"Speak to the person, not over their head to a caregiver.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Should I say person with autism or autistic person?\"\r\n    a: \"Both are used. Some people prefer person-first language, and many autistic people prefer identity-first language because autism is part of who they are. If you can, ask the person what they prefer and use their choice.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if I use the wrong word by mistake?\"\r\n    a: \"It happens to everyone. Apologise briefly, correct yourself and move on. Being willing to learn matters more than being perfect. Avoid long explanations that turn attention onto your own discomfort.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is it wrong to say someone is inspiring?\"\r\n    a: \"It depends on why. Praise for ordinary daily tasks can sound like low expectations. Recognise real achievements the same way you would for anyone, by naming the specific thing the person did well.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nRespectful language about disability starts with one idea: describe the person first, and describe support needs only when they are relevant. The steps below give you simple habits and word swaps you can use at home, at school, in worship and at work.\r\n\r\n## Why do words matter so much?\r\n\r\nWords carry attitudes. A phrase that sounds harmless to one person can quietly tell another that they are a burden, a patient or a problem to be solved. Over time, these small signals affect how people are treated and how they see themselves.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, around 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. That means most of us talk with, work with or live with someone who does. Respectful language is not a niche skill. It is basic good manners for everyone.\r\n\r\n## Step one: put the person first\r\n\r\nStart by naming the person, then mention disability only if it helps the conversation.\r\n\r\n- Say \"a person with a disability\" rather than \"a disabled case\" or \"the disabled\".\r\n- Say \"a child who uses a wheelchair\" rather than \"a wheelchair child\".\r\n- Say \"a person living with dementia\" rather than \"a dementia sufferer\".\r\n\r\nPerson-first language reminds listeners that the person is more than a diagnosis. It is the standard in many organisations, including those that work with the United Nations.\r\n\r\n## Step two: know that identity-first language is also valid\r\n\r\nSome people prefer to lead with the identity word. Many autistic people, and many Deaf people, say they prefer \"autistic person\" or \"Deaf person\", because they see it as part of who they are, not something separate to be politely mentioned second.\r\n\r\nBoth styles are respectful. The best approach is simple: ask, \"How do you like to describe yourself?\" Then follow their lead. When you must write about a group and cannot ask, our own articles use both forms and explain that both are common.\r\n\r\n## Step three: swap loaded words for plain ones\r\n\r\nThe table below shows common swaps. It is a guide, not a rulebook, and language keeps changing.\r\n\r\n| Instead of | Try |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Suffers from, afflicted with | Has, lives with |\r\n| Victim of, stricken by | Person with, person who has |\r\n| Handicapped, crippled | Person with a disability |\r\n| Special case, special child | Child with additional support needs, or simply the child's name |\r\n| Normal versus abnormal | Typical versus non-typical, or describe the difference directly |\r\n| Confined to a wheelchair | Uses a wheelchair |\r\n| Overcame her disability | Achieved her goal |\r\n| Mentally retarded | Person with an intellectual disability |\r\n\r\nAvoid words used as insults, such as calling something \"crazy\" or \"lame\" in jokes. They spread stigma even when nobody is targeted.\r\n\r\n## Step four: describe abilities and support, not deficits\r\n\r\nCompare two sentences. \"He cannot speak and depends on others for everything\" describes gaps. \"He communicates with gestures and pictures and likes support with meals\" describes the person and what helps.\r\n\r\nWhen you write or talk about someone:\r\n\r\n1. Say what the person can do, enjoys and chooses.\r\n2. Say what support helps them do it.\r\n3. Only mention diagnosis when it matters for the conversation, and never guess at one.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Before you describe someone, ask yourself, \"Would they be comfortable hearing this said about them?\" If not, rephrase.\r\n\r\n## Step five: speak to the person, not past them\r\n\r\nA common mistake is speaking to the caregiver or interpreter instead of the person. Look at the person, greet them, and address questions to them. If they need more time or a different way to reply, allow it.\r\n\r\nDo not touch a wheelchair, cane or communication device without permission. These are part of a person's personal space. Do not assume someone needs help, either. Offer, and accept a \"no thank you\" calmly.\r\n\r\n## Step six: avoid pity and miracle stories\r\n\r\nTwo framings do quiet harm. Pity says a person's life is sad. Miracle framing says any ordinary achievement is extraordinary. Both treat disability as the main story.\r\n\r\nInstead, treat people like anyone else: congratulate real accomplishments, acknowledge real difficulties and stay curious. Ask about interests, not only about the disability.\r\n\r\n## Step seven: keep learning and forgive yourself\r\n\r\nLanguage shifts, and different people have different preferences. If someone corrects you, say thanks and adjust. If you are unsure, ask politely and privately, and do not expect one person to speak for everyone.\r\n\r\nThis guide is general education, not legal or clinical advice. For questions about a specific person's diagnosis, needs or care, speak with qualified professionals such as doctors, therapists and social workers.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nAt Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania we try to write and speak in ways that respect the dignity and rights of the people we exist to serve. Our [neuro-diversity awareness program](/programs/program-neurodiversity) explains autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability in plain terms.\r\n\r\nTo go further, read [our perspective on everyday inclusion](/blog/article-community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion) or see how you can [get involved](/get-involved) respectfully.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/responsible-partnerships-with-the-foundation.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"What a Responsible Partnership With the Foundation Can Look Like\"\r\nslug: responsible-partnerships-with-the-foundation\r\ncategory: Partnerships\r\nexcerpt: \"A values-based view of responsible partnership: shared purpose, dignity first, open reporting and respect for the people the work serves.\"\r\ndescription: \"A values-based view of responsible partnership: shared purpose, dignity first, open reporting and respect for the people the work serves.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [partnerships, dignity, accountability, inclusion]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/responsible-partnerships-with-the-foundation.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/responsible-partnerships-with-the-foundation-thumb.webp\r\nicon: handshake\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"A responsible partnership starts from shared purpose and respect for the people served, not from what is easiest to give.\"\r\n  - \"Dignity, consent and safeguarding come before publicity, branding or speed.\"\r\n  - \"Plain, open reporting builds more trust than impressive claims.\"\r\n  - \"Good partnerships are steady and long-term, and both sides are free to say no.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What makes a partnership responsible?\"\r\n    a: \"A responsible partnership is built on shared purpose, clear roles, respect for the dignity of the people served, and honest communication. Both sides can raise concerns, and either side can decline something that does not fit its values or capacity.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should a partner expect photos or stories of beneficiaries?\"\r\n    a: \"Not as a right. People with disabilities and their families decide whether they are shown, and consent must be informed and freely given. A good partner accepts that some work is simply not shared publicly.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does every partnership involve money?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Partnerships can include skills, materials, time or expertise as well as funding. What matters is that the contribution fits real needs and that both sides understand what is being offered.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n---\r\nA responsible partnership is one where both sides put the dignity and wellbeing of people with disabilities first, agree openly on what each will do, and report honestly on what happens. It is less about the size of a gift and more about the quality of the relationship. This article sets out the values we believe such a partnership rests on.\r\n\r\n## Why does the way we partner matter?\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [1.3 billion people, about 16% of the world's population](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health), experience significant disability. Many of them, along with their families and caregivers, need steady support rather than one-off gestures.\r\n\r\nWhen organisations work together, they can reach further than either could alone. But partnership also carries risk. A well-meant project can create dependence, put pressure on families or use people's stories in ways they never chose. The way we partner decides whether the outcome is respectful or harmful.\r\n\r\n## Start with shared purpose\r\n\r\nEvery good partnership begins with a simple question: what are we both trying to achieve? For Eagle's Wings Foundation, the purpose is to empower, encourage and educate people with special needs, less privileged people and older people by helping, caring for and supporting their needs.\r\n\r\nA partner does not need to share every detail of that work. But they should share its spirit. If a proposal mainly serves a partner's image, and only loosely serves the people involved, it is worth pausing and talking again.\r\n\r\n## Put dignity and rights first\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) rests on dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination, participation and respect for difference. These ideas can guide everyday decisions in a partnership.\r\n\r\n- **Dignity:** people are described by their abilities and support needs, never with pity.\r\n- **Autonomy:** people and families are asked, not assumed for.\r\n- **Participation:** people with disabilities and caregivers help shape what is offered.\r\n- **Respect:** activities are planned so that no one is put on display.\r\n\r\nIn practice, this means partners agree that a visit, a photo or a story is never more important than someone's comfort and consent.\r\n\r\n## What does responsible support look like?\r\n\r\nSupport is most useful when it fits real needs and can be sustained. Some questions help test this:\r\n\r\n1. Does this contribution answer a need that has actually been described, rather than one we assumed?\r\n2. Can it continue, or will it stop when our attention moves elsewhere?\r\n3. Does it build the skills of local people, or does it replace them?\r\n4. Does it avoid creating extra work or pressure for families and caregivers?\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Support does not have to be money. Skills, materials, expertise and time can all help, as long as they are matched to real needs and agreed in advance. See our explainer on [ways organisations can partner](/blog/article-ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us).\r\n\r\n## Be honest in reporting\r\n\r\nTrust grows when organisations tell the truth about what they have done, including what did not work. Plain reporting is better than polished claims. It should name what was agreed, what was delivered, and what remains uncertain.\r\n\r\nAid transparency efforts such as the [IATI Standard](https://iatistandard.org/en/) show that openness about resources and activities is increasingly expected across the sector. Even small organisations can follow the same principle by keeping clear records and sharing them.\r\n\r\nWe hold ourselves to the same rule on this website: no invented numbers, no invented stories. A partner should expect us to say \"we do not know yet\" when that is the honest answer.\r\n\r\n## Protect people first: consent and safeguarding\r\n\r\nChildren, older people and people who rely on others for care can be vulnerable to being overlooked or exposed. A responsible partnership therefore treats consent and safeguarding as ground rules, not extras.\r\n\r\n- Anyone visiting or volunteering agrees to follow safeguarding expectations.\r\n- Photos and stories are shared only with informed, freely given consent.\r\n- Activities such as swimming or exercise are supervised and guided by suitably qualified people.\r\n- Concerns can be raised by anyone, without fear of blame.\r\n\r\nOur related article on [why consent matters in nonprofit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explains this in more detail.\r\n\r\n## Keep it steady, and keep it free\r\n\r\nThe best partnerships are patient. They give time for trust to grow, for plans to adjust, and for lessons to be learned. They also leave room for either side to say no, to change course or to end the relationship respectfully.\r\n\r\nNobody should feel trapped by an agreement, and nobody should feel pressured into accepting something that does not serve the people at the centre of the work.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nWe are a young non-profit in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, and we would rather grow carefully than quickly. If your organisation shares this approach, we would be glad to talk. Please [contact us](/contact) to begin a conversation. Nothing is promised in advance. We start by listening.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/responsible-program-growth.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"How Eagle’s Wings Approaches Responsible Program Growth\"\r\nslug: responsible-program-growth\r\ncategory: Foundation\r\nexcerpt: \"Why Eagle's Wings chooses to grow its programs carefully: focus first, listen to families, protect quality, and stay honest about what we can offer.\"\r\ndescription: \"Why Eagle's Wings chooses to grow its programs carefully: focus first, listen to families, protect quality, and stay honest about what we can offer.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [growth, quality, accountability, nonprofit]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/responsible-program-growth.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/responsible-program-growth-thumb.webp\r\nicon: building-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Responsible growth means growing only as fast as quality, safety and honesty can keep up.\"\r\n  - \"Eagle's Wings is a young foundation, founded in 2025, and treats focus as a strength.\"\r\n  - \"Listening to people with disabilities, older people and caregivers comes before adding anything new.\"\r\n  - \"We describe values and commitments, not results we cannot prove.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What does responsible program growth mean?\"\r\n    a: \"It means adding people, places or activities only when the existing work stays safe, respectful and well run. Growth is not the goal by itself. Better support for the people we serve is the goal, and sometimes that means waiting.\"\r\n  - q: \"Why does a young foundation grow slowly?\"\r\n    a: \"Trust is built through consistent, careful work. A foundation founded in 2025 is still learning its community. Moving carefully lets us listen, correct mistakes early and avoid promising more than we can deliver.\"\r\n  - q: \"How does Eagle's Wings decide what to add next?\"\r\n    a: \"We start from the needs of people with disabilities, older people and their caregivers, then ask whether we have the skills, supervision and resources to do it well. If not, we wait or look for guidance.\"\r\n  - q: \"Will growth change the Foundation's values?\"\r\n    a: \"It should not. Our mission and vision are the fixed point. Any new activity has to fit them, including dignity, inclusion, consent and honest communication.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n---\r\nResponsible program growth means growing only as fast as our quality, safety and honesty can keep up. For Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, a non-profit founded in 2025 in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, this is a working principle, not a slogan. It shapes what we say yes to, and what we choose to wait on.\r\n\r\nThis article is a statement of viewpoint. It describes how we think about growth. It does not report results, and it does not announce anything new.\r\n\r\n## Why bigger is not automatically better\r\n\r\nMany organisations feel pressure to show growth. More participants, more locations, more activities. But the WHO estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, roughly 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. That scale is a reminder of how much need exists. It is not a reason to spread thin.\r\n\r\nA programme that is stretched too far can hurt the very people it wants to help. A child in a life-skills session needs a calm, prepared adult. An older person who joins an exercise session needs supervision. A caregiver who comes to learn needs clear, accurate guidance. If growth outpaces preparation, these basics slip first.\r\n\r\n## Starting focused on purpose\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings works across five program areas: neuro-diversity awareness, elderly care support, education and therapy guidance, life skills programs, and caregiver training. We describe how they connect in [how our five program areas fit together](/blog/article-how-five-program-areas-fit-together).\r\n\r\nWe are a young foundation. That gives us a chance to build habits early: to write things down honestly, to ask before assuming, and to keep each activity small enough to do properly. We see this focus as a strength.\r\n\r\n## Five questions we ask before adding anything\r\n\r\nBefore taking on a new activity, we think a responsible organisation should be able to answer these questions:\r\n\r\n1. **Is there a real need?** Have people with disabilities, older people or caregivers told us this would help them?\r\n2. **Does it fit our mission?** Does it help to empower, encourage and educate, and to care for and support people's needs?\r\n3. **Do we have the people and skills?** Are trained, patient adults available, or do we need guidance from qualified professionals first?\r\n4. **Is it safe?** Water, exercise and outings need supervision and clear boundaries.\r\n5. **Can we explain it honestly?** If we cannot describe it plainly, without exaggeration, we are not ready to offer it.\r\n\r\nIf the answer to any question is \"not yet\", the answer to growth is also \"not yet\".\r\n\r\n## Listening comes before expanding\r\n\r\nThe most reliable guide to good support is the people who receive it. That includes autistic people and people with other disabilities, older people, families and caregivers. Good listening is slow. It means asking open questions, accepting that we may be wrong, and changing course when someone tells us something is not working.\r\n\r\nIt also means respecting privacy and consent. Our public activities include life-skills sessions, hydrotherapy and swimming, classroom learning, exercise and community time. When we share images or stories from such activities, the person's permission and dignity come first. [Why consent matters in non-profit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explains this in more detail.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** \"Responsible\" does not mean \"timid\". It means that every step up is matched by a step up in preparation, supervision and honesty.\r\n\r\n## Honest communication as a growth rule\r\n\r\nA common temptation is to promise more than can be delivered. We try to avoid it. We do not claim numbers we cannot prove. We do not publish invented stories. We say clearly that our articles are educational and are not medical, legal or clinical advice. Families should always speak with a doctor, therapist, nurse or social worker about diagnosis, treatment or medication.\r\n\r\nClear reporting is part of this. As programs grow, so should the habit of explaining what was done and what was learned. Open standards such as the International Aid Transparency Initiative show one way the wider sector thinks about sharing information plainly. Our related article on [transparent reporting as programs grow](/blog/article-transparent-reporting-as-programs-grow) looks at this idea further.\r\n\r\n## Growth that includes people, not only activities\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination, participation and inclusion. We take those ideas as a test for our own growth. Are we making more room for people to take part, or only adding to a list of activities?\r\n\r\nReal growth, to us, looks like more people feeling valued, more families feeling less alone, and more of the community understanding inclusion. It is measured in relationships and understanding as much as in activities.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nWe would rather do a few things carefully than many things badly. We will keep listening, keep our promises small and honest, and keep our values as the fixed point while the work develops. You can read the full statement of our purpose on our [mission, vision and values](/about#mission) page, and learn more about who we are on the [About page](/about).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/responsible-volunteering-in-community-work.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"What Responsible Volunteering Looks Like in Community Work\"\r\nslug: responsible-volunteering-in-community-work\r\ncategory: \"Volunteering\"\r\nexcerpt: \"Good intentions are not enough. A values-based look at what responsible volunteering means when you support children, older people and families.\"\r\ndescription: \"Good intentions are not enough. A values-based look at what responsible volunteering means when you support children, older people and families.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [volunteering, dignity, community, responsibility]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/responsible-volunteering-in-community-work.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/responsible-volunteering-in-community-work-thumb.webp\r\nicon: hand-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Responsible volunteering puts the dignity and needs of the people supported ahead of the volunteer's wish to feel useful.\"\r\n  - \"Reliability, humility and respect for professionals matter more than big gestures.\"\r\n  - \"Volunteers support the work. They do not replace trained therapists, nurses or social workers.\"\r\n  - \"Asking questions and accepting guidance is a sign of strength, not weakness.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What does responsible volunteering mean?\"\r\n    a: \"It means giving your time in ways that respect the people you support, follow the organisation's guidance, stay within your skills and do no harm. It focuses on what the community needs, not on what feels rewarding to the volunteer.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can a volunteer replace a trained professional?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Volunteers add helping hands, company and practical support. Diagnosis, therapy, medical care and legal or social-work decisions belong to qualified professionals, and a good volunteer knows where that line sits.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is it better to help a little and often, or a lot once?\"\r\n    a: \"For people who rely on familiar faces and routines, steady and predictable help is usually more valuable than a single burst of effort. Be honest about what you can keep up, and offer only that.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"United Nations Volunteers\"\r\n    publisher: \"UN Volunteers\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unv.org/\"\r\n---\r\nResponsible volunteering means giving your time in a way that serves the people you support, not your own need to feel helpful. It is reliable, respectful and honest about its limits. In community work with children, older people and families, that approach protects dignity and builds trust.\r\n\r\nThis is a values-based view from the Eagle's Wings Editorial Team. It is not a story about a particular volunteer. It is a description of the habits we believe make volunteering safe and worthwhile.\r\n\r\n## Why does \"good intentions\" fall short?\r\n\r\nAlmost every volunteer starts with a kind wish. Kindness is a good beginning, but it can go wrong when it ignores what people actually want and need.\r\n\r\nA person with a disability is not a project. An older person living with dementia is not an audience for your generosity. Each person has their own history, preferences and rights. The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination, participation and inclusion. Responsible volunteering puts those ideas into everyday behaviour.\r\n\r\n## Values that guide responsible volunteers\r\n\r\n### Dignity first\r\n\r\nSpeak to the person, not about them. Ask before you help. Offer choices instead of taking over. Help that removes someone's say in their own day is not really help.\r\n\r\n### Reliability\r\n\r\nPeople plan around you. A family may arrange their morning because a volunteer said they would come. If you cannot keep a commitment, say so early and clearly. Small, steady contributions are worth more than dramatic ones that fade.\r\n\r\n### Humility\r\n\r\nYou are joining work that others have shaped. Listen before you suggest changes. Expect to learn from staff, families and the people you support, who know their own needs far better than a newcomer does.\r\n\r\n### Honesty about limits\r\n\r\nKnow what you are trained for and what you are not. If a situation feels beyond your skill, stop and ask a coordinator. Guessing is not brave. It can be unsafe.\r\n\r\n## What volunteers do and do not do\r\n\r\nThe table below is a simple guide. Your organisation's own rules always come first.\r\n\r\n| Volunteers can usually support | Volunteers should leave to trained people |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Company, conversation and shared activities | Diagnosis or medical judgement |\r\n| Practical help such as set-up, tidying and carrying | Medication decisions of any kind |\r\n| Encouraging routines and joining group sessions | Therapy planning and clinical treatment |\r\n| Sharing skills the organisation has asked for | Legal, safeguarding or social-work decisions |\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** If someone tells you something that worries you, do not investigate on your own or promise to keep it secret. Pass it to the responsible coordinator straight away.\r\n\r\n## Why does responsible volunteering matter for this community?\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [1.3 billion people, about 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Families and caregivers often carry much of the daily support. Community volunteers can lighten that load, but only when their help fits with the care plans, routines and wishes of the family and the professionals involved.\r\n\r\nUnplanned or careless help can cause confusion, break a helpful routine or leave someone feeling like an object of pity. Careful help does the opposite. It makes a person feel included and respected.\r\n\r\n## Habits you can practise from day one\r\n\r\n- Arrive on time and ready to listen.\r\n- Ask what the person prefers and accept the answer.\r\n- Use respectful language. Say \"person with a disability\" or \"person living with dementia\" rather than words that define someone by a condition.\r\n- Respect privacy. Do not share names, photos or personal details without clear permission from the organisation and the person or their family.\r\n- Follow supervision instructions, especially around water, exercise and outings.\r\n- Reflect afterwards. Ask yourself what went well and what you would do differently.\r\n\r\n## Volunteering is a relationship, not a favour\r\n\r\nThe strongest volunteering feels like a partnership. The volunteer brings time and willingness. The organisation brings structure, training and safeguards. The people supported bring their own knowledge of their lives. Take away any one of these and the arrangement weakens.\r\n\r\nThe UN Volunteers programme describes volunteering as something that benefits both the community and the volunteer. That is a healthy way to think about it, provided the community's needs are named first.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation works to empower, encourage and educate people with special needs and older people, and to support their families and caregivers. We see volunteers as valued helpers who work within clear guidance and alongside qualified people.\r\n\r\nIf this way of giving time appeals to you, read more about the [volunteer opportunities](/volunteers) or visit the [Get Involved](/get-involved) page. You can also explore our [programs](/programs) to see the kind of work that responsible volunteering supports.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/safeguarding-basics-for-community-organisations.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Safeguarding Basics for Community Organisations\"\r\nslug: safeguarding-basics-for-community-organisations\r\ncategory: Safeguarding\r\nexcerpt: \"A plain-language explainer of what safeguarding means, who it protects and the everyday habits that help community organisations keep people safe.\"\r\ndescription: \"A plain-language explainer of what safeguarding means, who it protects and the everyday habits that help community organisations keep people safe.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [safeguarding, children, community, protection]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/safeguarding-basics-for-community-organisations.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/safeguarding-basics-for-community-organisations-thumb.webp\r\nicon: shield-check\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Safeguarding means taking reasonable steps to protect people from harm and to respond well when a concern arises.\"\r\n  - \"It matters most for children, older people and people with disabilities, who may depend on others for support.\"\r\n  - \"Good safeguarding is mostly everyday habits: clear roles, open supervision, respectful behaviour and a known way to raise concerns.\"\r\n  - \"Safeguarding is a shared responsibility, and it does not replace the work of qualified professionals and local authorities.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is the difference between safeguarding and child protection?\"\r\n    a: \"Safeguarding is the wider effort to prevent harm and promote safety for everyone at risk. Child protection is the response when a child is being harmed or is at risk of harm. Both need clear, known steps.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does a small group need safeguarding rules?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Any group that works with children, older people or people with disabilities can have safeguarding duties. A short written set of rules, shared with everyone, is much better than none.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who is responsible for safeguarding?\"\r\n    a: \"Everyone involved shares it, but leadership is accountable. Leaders should name a person people can go to with worries, make sure helpers know what to do, and check that the practices are followed.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is safeguarding only about abuse?\"\r\n    a: \"It covers abuse and neglect, but also accidents, unsafe activities, bullying, exploitation and misuse of personal information. In short, it is about the overall safety, wellbeing and dignity of everyone involved in your activities.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Child maltreatment\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/child-maltreatment\"\r\n  - title: \"Violence against children\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nSafeguarding means taking reasonable steps to protect people from harm and knowing what to do if something goes wrong. It applies to every community organisation that works with children, older people or people with disabilities. This explainer covers the basic ideas in plain language. It is general guidance, not legal advice.\r\n\r\n## What does safeguarding actually mean?\r\n\r\nSafeguarding has two halves. The first is prevention: setting up activities, spaces and habits so harm is less likely. The second is response: acting quickly and properly when someone is worried or hurt.\r\n\r\nThink of it as a culture, not a form. An organisation that safeguards well is one where people feel able to speak up, where adults behave openly, and where problems are dealt with rather than hidden.\r\n\r\n## Who does safeguarding protect?\r\n\r\nSafeguarding is for everyone, but it focuses on people who may find it harder to protect themselves or to speak up.\r\n\r\n- **Children,** who rely on adults for care and decisions.\r\n- **Older people,** including people living with dementia, who may depend on others for daily support.\r\n- **People with disabilities,** who may face barriers to being heard or believed.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, around 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Many community groups will therefore meet people who need extra care, whether or not it is mentioned.\r\n\r\n## Why is it a community responsibility?\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization explains that child maltreatment and violence against children are serious global problems that can harm health and wellbeing, sometimes for a lifetime. Harm often happens where a child should be safest, and by people they know. That is a reason for every organisation to have clear practices, not a reason for suspicion of every adult.\r\n\r\nPrevention works best when a whole community pays attention: families, volunteers, staff and leaders. Nobody should feel that safeguarding is someone else's job.\r\n\r\n## What are the core building blocks?\r\n\r\n### A named person to go to\r\n\r\nChoose one or two people whom others can approach with a worry. Make their names and contact details known to helpers and families.\r\n\r\n### Clear expectations of behaviour\r\n\r\nWrite a short code of conduct. Helpers should treat everyone with respect, avoid being alone and unobserved with a child, and never use their position to gain personal favours or access.\r\n\r\n### Open supervision\r\n\r\nChildren and people who need support should be with responsible adults, in visible places, with enough helpers for the group. Our guide to [keeping children and vulnerable adults safe during activities](/blog/article-keeping-children-and-vulnerable-adults-safe-during-activities) explains this in practical steps.\r\n\r\n### Careful recruitment and briefing\r\n\r\nKnow who is helping you. Ask newcomers about their experience, speak to people who know them where appropriate, and brief every helper before they start.\r\n\r\n### Respect for privacy\r\n\r\nCollect only the personal details you need, keep them secure and share them only with people who must know. See our article on [protecting families' personal information online](/blog/article-protecting-families-personal-information-online).\r\n\r\n### A way to raise concerns\r\n\r\nPeople must know how to report a worry and be confident it will be taken seriously. Read more in [how to raise a safeguarding concern safely](/blog/article-how-to-raise-a-safeguarding-concern-safely).\r\n\r\n## What are common warning signs?\r\n\r\nNo single sign proves that something is wrong, and many have harmless explanations. Still, it is wise to notice changes such as:\r\n\r\n- a person who becomes unusually withdrawn, fearful or upset\r\n- unexplained injuries or a person who avoids certain people or places\r\n- a helper who seeks time alone with a child or gives special gifts or attention\r\n- someone who is regularly left unsupervised or without basic care\r\n\r\nIf you notice something, do not investigate on your own or confront anyone. Write down what you saw and heard, factually, and pass it to the person responsible for welfare.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Your role is to notice and report. Deciding what has happened belongs to leadership, qualified professionals and the relevant local authorities.\r\n\r\n## How do small organisations get started?\r\n\r\nYou do not need a long policy to begin. Start with a single page that answers four questions.\r\n\r\n1. Who is our welfare contact, and how do people reach them?\r\n2. How do we expect helpers to behave around children and people who need support?\r\n3. What do we do if a person is hurt, or if someone raises a worry?\r\n4. How do we handle photos and personal details?\r\n\r\nShare the page with every helper before they begin, go through it together, and review it now and then. If you are unsure whether your rules are enough, seek qualified advice, for example from a social worker or an experienced child-protection professional.\r\n\r\n## Common myths\r\n\r\n**\"We know everyone here, so we don't need rules.\"** Rules protect people because trust alone is not enough, and they protect helpers from misunderstandings too.\r\n\r\n**\"Safeguarding is only for large organisations.\"** Small groups can follow simple written practices just as easily.\r\n\r\n**\"Speaking up will cause trouble.\"** A worry raised early is far easier to deal with than one that is ignored.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania works with people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and their families and caregivers. Keeping people safe and treated with dignity is part of doing that work responsibly. You can read how we handle personal information on our [privacy page](/privacy) or [contact us](/contact) with questions.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/sensory-friendly-home-and-community-checklist.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"A Sensory-Friendly Home and Community Checklist\"\r\nslug: sensory-friendly-home-and-community-checklist\r\ncategory: Neuro-diversity\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist to reduce sensory overload at home, school and community events, so autistic children and others can feel calm and take part.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist to reduce sensory overload at home, school and community events, so autistic children and others can feel calm and take part.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [sensory, autism, checklist, accessibility]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: stockHeadphones\r\nthumb: stockHeadphonesThumb\r\nicon: brain\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Sensory overload happens when sound, light, touch, smell or crowds feel too intense for a person to cope with.\"\r\n  - \"Small changes, such as a quiet corner or a warning before noise, can help a lot.\"\r\n  - \"Every person's sensory needs are different, so observe and ask.\"\r\n  - \"Making spaces sensory-friendly helps many people, not only neurodivergent children.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What does sensory-friendly mean?\"\r\n    a: \"It means a place or event that has been adjusted to reduce things that overwhelm the senses, such as loud noise, harsh light, strong smells or crowding, and that offers quiet space and clear information. It makes taking part easier for many people.\"\r\n  - q: \"How do I know what overwhelms my child?\"\r\n    a: \"Watch for patterns. Note when your child covers ears, refuses clothing, avoids foods or becomes upset, and what was happening at the time. Ask your child in their own way. A therapist can also help identify sensory needs.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do sensory-friendly changes cost a lot?\"\r\n    a: \"Not necessarily. Many helpful changes are free or low-cost, such as turning down music, giving a warning, choosing a quiet corner, or letting a child wear comfortable clothes. Simple planning goes a long way.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Centre for Excellence in Universal Design\"\r\n    publisher: \"National Disability Authority, Ireland\"\r\n    url: \"https://universaldesign.ie/\"\r\n---\r\nA sensory-friendly space is one where sound, light, touch, smell and crowds are managed so nobody is overwhelmed. Use the checklists below at home, at school and at community gatherings. You do not need to do everything. Start with one or two changes and notice what helps.\r\n\r\n## What is sensory overload?\r\n\r\nOur senses take in a lot of information all the time. Many autistic people, and some people with ADHD or other differences, experience sensory input more strongly, or sometimes less strongly, than others. A hand dryer may feel painful. A clothing label may feel unbearable. A busy market may feel like too much all at once.\r\n\r\nWhen this happens, a person may cover their ears, cry, run away, freeze or become very upset. This is a natural response, not bad behaviour. The goal is to reduce triggers and give people ways to recover.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Sensory needs differ a great deal. One child may dislike noise but love strong hugs. Another may seek movement and dislike sitting still. Observe the individual.\r\n\r\n## Home checklist\r\n\r\n### Sound\r\n\r\n- [ ] Turn off the TV or radio when nobody is watching or listening.\r\n- [ ] Use soft furnishings, such as rugs or curtains, to reduce echo.\r\n- [ ] Warn your child before loud sounds, such as a blender or a knock at the door.\r\n- [ ] Keep a quiet place where the child can go to rest.\r\n\r\n### Light and space\r\n\r\n- [ ] Use natural light where you can, and avoid flickering bulbs.\r\n- [ ] Keep the child's play or study area tidy and simple.\r\n- [ ] Put up a picture routine chart so the day feels predictable.\r\n\r\n### Touch, clothing and food\r\n\r\n- [ ] Let your child choose soft, comfortable clothes. Cut out itchy labels if needed.\r\n- [ ] Introduce new foods slowly, without pressure or shame.\r\n- [ ] Offer a safe, familiar food alongside new ones.\r\n- [ ] Respect a child's dislike of certain textures, and talk with a health professional if eating is a worry.\r\n\r\n### Calm and comfort\r\n\r\n- [ ] Create a calm corner with cushions, a blanket and favourite items.\r\n- [ ] Offer movement breaks, such as walking, stretching or carrying light items.\r\n- [ ] Keep bedtime and mealtime routines steady.\r\n\r\n## School and learning checklist\r\n\r\n- [ ] Seat the child away from noisy doors, windows or fans if this helps.\r\n- [ ] Give instructions in short steps, with pictures where possible.\r\n- [ ] Allow movement breaks and quiet breaks.\r\n- [ ] Warn about changes, such as a visitor, a fire drill or a new timetable.\r\n- [ ] Let the child use tools that help, such as headphones or a fidget item, if agreed with the teacher.\r\n- [ ] Teach classmates that everyone senses the world differently.\r\n\r\n## Community and event checklist\r\n\r\nWhether you are planning a church gathering, family celebration, school event or community day, ask yourself:\r\n\r\n- [ ] Is there a quiet space people can retreat to?\r\n- [ ] Can music and speakers be kept to a comfortable level?\r\n- [ ] Can we share the plan for the day in advance, with simple pictures or words?\r\n- [ ] Are there clear signs and a calm way to find toilets and exits?\r\n- [ ] Can people arrive early, or leave early, without judgement?\r\n- [ ] Have we thought about strong smells, flashing lights and crowded queues?\r\n- [ ] Are volunteers and hosts ready to respond with patience and kindness?\r\n\r\n## When you are out and about\r\n\r\nEven with good planning, some places are hard. A few small habits help.\r\n\r\n1. **Visit at quieter times,** when the market or clinic is less crowded.\r\n2. **Prepare your child** with a short, simple description of where you are going.\r\n3. **Bring comfort items,** such as a favourite toy, snack or water.\r\n4. **Agree a signal** your child can use when they need a break.\r\n5. **Leave without guilt** if it becomes too much. Try again another day.\r\n\r\n## Sensory-friendly is good design for everyone\r\n\r\nMany changes that help autistic children also help older people, people with hearing loss, people who are tired or anxious, and people living with dementia. This idea is part of *universal design*, which means designing spaces that as many people as possible can use. The Centre for Excellence in Universal Design offers helpful information on the idea.\r\n\r\n## A note on safety and professional help\r\n\r\nThis checklist is general guidance. If sensory difficulties cause serious distress, affect eating, sleep or safety, speak to a doctor or qualified therapist. They can offer advice suited to your child.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nAt Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, we believe inclusion begins with everyday details: a quieter corner, a clear plan, a patient welcome. Our Neuro-diversity awareness work helps families and communities understand these needs. Read more on our [Neuro-diversity program page](/programs/program-neurodiversity), explore [community awareness and everyday inclusion](/blog/community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion), or [get in touch](/contact) to ask how you can help.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/sharing-care-tasks-across-a-family-checklist.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"A Checklist for Sharing Care Tasks Across a Family\"\r\nslug: sharing-care-tasks-across-a-family-checklist\r\ncategory: Caregiving\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist to help relatives divide care tasks fairly, talk openly, and keep the person at the centre of every decision.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist to help relatives divide care tasks fairly, talk openly, and keep the person at the centre of every decision.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [caregiving, family, teamwork, checklist]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: family\r\nthumb: stockGrandmotherBibiThumb\r\nimageCredit: \"Card photo: “Grandmother Bibi” by ssilberman, CC BY 2.0|https://www.flickr.com/photos/26016052@N00/27588343934\"\r\nicon: users-round\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Care is lighter and more sustainable when tasks are named, divided and reviewed together.\"\r\n  - \"Start with a family conversation, and include the person receiving care wherever possible.\"\r\n  - \"Share different kinds of help, including money, time, transport, paperwork and emotional support.\"\r\n  - \"Review the plan regularly, because needs and family circumstances change.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What if one relative does all the work?\"\r\n    a: \"Call a calm family meeting and list every task on paper. Seeing the whole workload often helps relatives understand. Then ask each person to choose tasks they can do reliably, even if small.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if relatives live far away?\"\r\n    a: \"Distance does not rule out helping. Faraway relatives can handle phone calls, paperwork, reminders, research or contributing to costs, and can give the main caregiver time to rest by phone or by visiting.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should the person receiving care be included?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes, wherever possible. A person with a disability or an older person has a right to be heard about their own care. Ask what they prefer, and explain the plan in words they understand.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the family disagrees?\"\r\n    a: \"Return to the person's needs and wishes. Write down the points of agreement first. If disagreements continue, a respected elder, social worker or counsellor may help the family talk.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia\"\r\n---\r\nSharing care across a family works best when every task is named, every relative knows their part, and the plan is reviewed often. Use this checklist as a starting point for a family conversation. Adapt it to your own home, because every family is different.\r\n\r\n## Before you begin: three ground rules\r\n\r\nSharing care can bring out old tensions. These rules help.\r\n\r\n- **Keep the person at the centre.** The plan is about their wellbeing, dignity and wishes.\r\n- **Assume goodwill.** Most relatives want to help, but they may not know how.\r\n- **Aim for fair, not identical.** People have different jobs, health, money and time.\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on dignity, autonomy and participation. In practice, that means the person is a partner in the plan and not just its subject.\r\n\r\n## Checklist part one: prepare the conversation\r\n\r\n- [ ] Choose a calm time, not in the middle of a crisis.\r\n- [ ] Invite everyone who helps or wishes to help, including relatives who live elsewhere by phone.\r\n- [ ] Ask the person receiving care what matters to them, and how they would like to be supported.\r\n- [ ] Agree that everyone speaks and everyone listens.\r\n- [ ] Decide who will write things down.\r\n\r\n## Checklist part two: list every task\r\n\r\nMany caregivers do far more than others realise. Writing the tasks down makes the work visible.\r\n\r\n### Daily personal care\r\n\r\n- [ ] Washing, dressing and grooming\r\n- [ ] Meals and drinks, including preparing and feeding if needed\r\n- [ ] Toileting support\r\n- [ ] Reminders for medicine that a doctor or nurse has prescribed\r\n\r\n### Movement, rest and activity\r\n\r\n- [ ] Gentle exercise or walks that a professional has said are suitable\r\n- [ ] Supervised activities, especially anything involving water\r\n- [ ] Time for hobbies, learning or talking\r\n- [ ] Quiet rest\r\n\r\n### Household and practical tasks\r\n\r\n- [ ] Shopping, cooking and cleaning\r\n- [ ] Laundry\r\n- [ ] Transport to appointments or school\r\n- [ ] Paperwork and keeping health records\r\n\r\n### Emotional and social support\r\n\r\n- [ ] Companionship and conversation\r\n- [ ] Visiting or phoning regularly\r\n- [ ] Taking the person to community or faith gatherings, if they wish\r\n\r\n## Checklist part three: match tasks to people\r\n\r\nUse a simple table like this on a sheet of paper.\r\n\r\n| Task | Who | How often | Backup |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| Morning routine | | | |\r\n| Meals | | | |\r\n| Appointments and transport | | | |\r\n| Paperwork | | | |\r\n| Evening visit or call | | | |\r\n| Rest time for the main caregiver | | | |\r\n\r\nSome help does not involve hands-on care. A relative who lives far away might handle phone calls, research or a contribution to costs. Another might take over for a few hours so the main caregiver can rest.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Ask each person, \"What can you do reliably?\" A small task done every week is worth more than a large promise that is soon forgotten.\r\n\r\n## Checklist part four: agree how you will work together\r\n\r\n- [ ] Decide how relatives will share news, such as a family message group or a notebook in the home.\r\n- [ ] Keep a shared record. Our article on [keeping a simple daily observation log](/blog/article-keeping-a-simple-daily-observation-log) explains how.\r\n- [ ] Agree that everyone uses the same calm approach, so the person is not confused by different rules.\r\n- [ ] Decide who speaks to doctors, therapists or teachers, and who else attends.\r\n- [ ] Agree how to handle emergencies, and keep important phone numbers in one place.\r\n\r\n## Checklist part five: protect the caregiver\r\n\r\n- [ ] Make sure the main caregiver has regular time off.\r\n- [ ] Ask honestly how they are coping.\r\n- [ ] Notice signs of exhaustion, low mood or illness, and encourage them to seek help.\r\n- [ ] Say thank you. Recognition matters.\r\n\r\nRead more in [why caregivers need support too](/blog/article-why-caregivers-need-support-too).\r\n\r\n## Checklist part six: review the plan\r\n\r\nNeeds change. A child grows, an older person's health shifts, a relative moves for work.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Choose a date to review the plan.\r\n- [ ] Ask what is working and what is not.\r\n- [ ] Change one thing at a time.\r\n- [ ] Bring in professional advice when the person's needs change. Our [FAQ on seeking professional guidance](/blog/article-when-and-how-to-seek-professional-guidance-faq) can help you decide when.\r\n\r\nThis checklist is general guidance. It does not replace advice from doctors, nurses, therapists or social workers, who can advise on the needs of the individual.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania supports people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, together with their families and caregivers. Our caregiver training program area focuses on practical, respectful skills that families can share. Visit the [caregiver training program page](/programs/program-caregiver-training) to learn more, or [contact us](/contact).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/simple-affordable-nutrition-habits-for-older-people.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Simple, Affordable Nutrition Habits for Older People\"\r\nslug: simple-affordable-nutrition-habits-for-older-people\r\ncategory: \"Healthy ageing\"\r\nexcerpt: \"Answers to common questions about eating well in older age, using WHO healthy diet guidance and everyday, locally available foods.\"\r\ndescription: \"Answers to common questions about eating well in older age, using WHO healthy diet guidance and everyday, locally available foods.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [nutrition, older-adults, healthy-diet, family-meals]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/simple-affordable-nutrition-habits-for-older-people.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/simple-affordable-nutrition-habits-for-older-people-thumb.webp\r\nicon: heart-pulse\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"A healthy diet is varied and built mostly on vegetables, fruit, legumes, whole grains and nuts, as described by the WHO.\"\r\n  - \"Regular small meals, enough safe water and shared mealtimes help when appetite is low.\"\r\n  - \"Local, seasonal foods are often the most affordable and the most familiar.\"\r\n  - \"A doctor, nurse or dietitian should guide anyone with a medical condition, chewing or swallowing difficulty, or unexplained weight change.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What does a healthy diet look like for an older person?\"\r\n    a: \"The WHO describes a varied diet with vegetables, fruit, legumes, nuts and whole grains, moderate animal foods, and less salt, sugar and unhealthy fat. Older people need the same broad pattern, adjusted for appetite, teeth and health conditions.\"\r\n  - q: \"How can we eat well on a small budget?\"\r\n    a: \"Choose seasonal produce, buy legumes and grains in usable amounts, cook at home, and reduce waste by using leftovers. Simple home-cooked meals are often more nourishing and cheaper than processed snacks.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if an older person has little appetite?\"\r\n    a: \"Offer small, frequent meals, favourite foods and shared mealtimes. If poor appetite lasts, or the person loses weight without trying, ask a doctor or nurse to check for causes such as dental pain or illness.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should an older person take supplements?\"\r\n    a: \"Only a health professional can advise on supplements, because needs and risks vary and some products interact with medicines. Food should come first, and any supplement should be discussed with a doctor or pharmacist.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Healthy diet\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet\"\r\n  - title: \"Ageing and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ageing-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Oral health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/oral-health\"\r\n---\r\nGood nutrition in older age does not need special or costly foods. It needs variety, regular meals, enough water and a pleasant place to eat. Below are answers to the questions families ask most often, based on World Health Organization (WHO) healthy diet guidance.\r\n\r\n## What does the WHO say a healthy diet includes?\r\n\r\nThe WHO describes a healthy diet as one that protects against malnutrition in all its forms and against long-term conditions. In practice, that means eating a variety of foods:\r\n\r\n- Vegetables and fruit, ideally fresh and in season.\r\n- Legumes such as beans, lentils and peas.\r\n- Nuts and whole grains, such as unrefined maize, millet or brown rice.\r\n- Moderate amounts of fish, eggs, milk or meat.\r\n\r\nIt also means limiting salt, free sugars and unhealthy fats, and drinking safe water. This is general guidance for the whole population, and it suits most older people well.\r\n\r\n## Are older people's food needs different?\r\n\r\nThe broad pattern is the same, but circumstances often change with age. Appetite may fall. Teeth or gums may hurt. Some medicines change taste or cause a dry mouth. Cooking or shopping may become tiring. Thirst may be less obvious.\r\n\r\nThese are reasons to adapt how food is prepared and served, not to give up on variety. If you notice a sudden change in eating, or weight loss the person did not intend, mention it to a doctor or nurse.\r\n\r\n## How can we eat well without spending much?\r\n\r\nAffordable eating is mostly about planning and simplicity.\r\n\r\n### Cook with what is in season\r\n\r\nSeasonal vegetables and fruit tend to be fresher and cheaper. Buying from a local market or growing a few plants, even in containers, adds variety.\r\n\r\n### Lean on legumes and whole grains\r\n\r\nBeans, lentils and grains are filling, flexible and good value. They combine well in stews and porridges.\r\n\r\n### Waste less\r\n\r\nUse leftovers in the next meal. Add vegetables to sauces and soups. Store food safely so it does not spoil.\r\n\r\n### Limit costly extras\r\n\r\nSweet drinks and packaged snacks can be expensive and offer little nourishment. Keep them for occasional treats.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Cook a larger pot of a nourishing dish, keep a portion safely for the next day, and serve it warm with a fresh vegetable or fruit on the side.\r\n\r\n## What can help when appetite is low?\r\n\r\nTry a gentle, patient approach.\r\n\r\n1. Offer small meals and snacks several times a day rather than three big plates.\r\n2. Serve favourite, familiar dishes, and make food look and smell inviting.\r\n3. Add flavour with herbs, lemon or spices rather than extra salt.\r\n4. Sit and eat together, since company often helps people eat more.\r\n5. Keep a drink within reach and offer water regularly.\r\n\r\nNever force-feed. If the person coughs or chokes when eating, or food seems to get stuck, see a health professional promptly, because swallowing difficulty needs proper assessment.\r\n\r\n## How do teeth and gums affect eating?\r\n\r\nPain, loose teeth or poorly fitting dentures can make chewing hard and lead people to avoid nutritious foods. The WHO highlights oral health as part of overall health. Softer preparations, such as mashed beans, soft-cooked vegetables and soups, can help in the meantime. A dentist or oral health worker can treat the underlying problem.\r\n\r\n## How much should a person drink?\r\n\r\nThere is no single right amount for everyone, so ask a health professional if the person has a heart, kidney or other condition. As a general habit, offer safe drinking water regularly through the day, and include soups and fruit that contain water. Watch for signs that someone is not drinking enough, such as unusual tiredness, confusion or very dark urine, and seek advice.\r\n\r\n## How does mealtime affect mood?\r\n\r\nMeals are social moments. Eating with family or neighbours gives structure to the day and a chance to talk. For older people who live alone, an invitation to share a meal can matter as much as the food itself. See our related guide on [keeping older people mentally stimulated and socially connected](/blog/article-keeping-older-people-mentally-stimulated-and-connected).\r\n\r\nNutrition also works alongside movement. Our article on [gentle movement at home for older adults](/blog/article-gentle-movement-at-home-for-older-adults) shows how the two habits support each other.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nNutrition is one of the areas covered by Eagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania in its elderly care support, together with physical exercise and mental stimulation guidance. We aim to give practical, respectful information that families and caregivers can use at home. Learn more about our [elderly care program](/programs/program-elderly-care) or [contact us](/contact).\r\n\r\nThis article is general education and not a substitute for advice from a doctor, nurse or dietitian.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/skilled-and-professional-volunteering-what-it-can-and-cannot-do.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Skilled Volunteering: What Professionals Can and Cannot Offer\"\r\nslug: skilled-and-professional-volunteering-what-it-can-and-cannot-do\r\ncategory: \"Volunteering\"\r\nexcerpt: \"How teachers, communicators and health-related professionals can volunteer their skills well, and where the limits of volunteering must stay clear.\"\r\ndescription: \"How teachers, communicators and health-related professionals can volunteer their skills well, and where the limits of volunteering must stay clear.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [skilled-volunteering, professionals, teaching, communications]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/skilled-and-professional-volunteering-what-it-can-and-cannot-do.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/skilled-and-professional-volunteering-what-it-can-and-cannot-do-thumb.webp\r\nicon: hand-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Skilled volunteering means offering a professional skill in a way the organisation has agreed to.\"\r\n  - \"A skill helps only when it fits the organisation's needs, methods and safeguards.\"\r\n  - \"Volunteering is not a substitute for a professional's formal role, registration or a proper assessment.\"\r\n  - \"Communications skills are valuable but must protect privacy and dignity.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is skilled volunteering?\"\r\n    a: \"It is volunteering in which you share a specific professional or practical skill, such as teaching, writing, design or organisation, to support an organisation's work. It is agreed in advance and fits the organisation's needs.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can a therapist or nurse volunteer to treat people?\"\r\n    a: \"Clinical treatment is a formal responsibility with professional rules, registration and accountability. Anyone with health training should talk with the organisation about what is appropriate and never assume that volunteering replaces those requirements.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is a skilled volunteer better than a general volunteer?\"\r\n    a: \"Not better, just different. Skilled help meets particular needs, while general volunteers provide company, practical help and time. Both matter. What counts most is fit, reliability and respect.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"United Nations Volunteers\"\r\n    publisher: \"UN Volunteers\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unv.org/\"\r\n  - title: \"World report on disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564182\"\r\n  - title: \"Assistive technology\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/assistive-technology\"\r\n---\r\nSkilled volunteering means giving a professional or practical skill, such as teaching, writing or organising, to support an organisation's work. It can be very valuable when it matches real needs and works within clear limits. It becomes a problem when a volunteer treats a skill as permission to act outside the rules of the organisation or their own profession.\r\n\r\n## What counts as skilled volunteering?\r\n\r\nAny ability you have built through study, work or experience can be offered. Common examples include:\r\n\r\n- Teaching and tutoring\r\n- Writing, translation and communications\r\n- Photography and design, with proper permissions\r\n- Accounting and administration\r\n- Planning and organising events\r\n- Health-related knowledge, shared within agreed limits\r\n\r\nThe UN Volunteers programme recognises that volunteers bring diverse skills to community development. The key words are diverse and agreed. A good match starts with the organisation, not the volunteer, deciding what is needed.\r\n\r\n## How can teaching skills help?\r\n\r\nTeachers understand how to explain ideas, hold attention and adapt tasks. In a community setting, those abilities can support classroom-style sessions, reading and writing practice or creative activities.\r\n\r\nTwo cautions apply. First, learning support for children and adults with disabilities often follows an approach set by trained professionals and families. Volunteers should follow that plan rather than introducing their own methods. Second, be ready to slow down, repeat and adapt. What works in one classroom may not suit a person who learns differently.\r\n\r\n## How can communications skills help?\r\n\r\nClear writing, design and social-media skills help a small organisation explain its work and reach supporters. This is a real gift because many non-profits have limited time for communications.\r\n\r\nIt also carries responsibility. Communications volunteers should:\r\n\r\n- Ask the organisation what may be shared and how.\r\n- Never use a person's name, image or story without proper consent.\r\n- Avoid pity-based or \"miracle\" framing that treats people with disabilities as objects of inspiration.\r\n- Keep every claim accurate. If a number or outcome cannot be verified, do not use it.\r\n\r\nEthical storytelling protects dignity and keeps trust with families and the public.\r\n\r\n## What about therapy-adjacent skills?\r\n\r\nSome volunteers have training in health, rehabilitation or counselling. Their knowledge is valuable, but this is the area where limits matter most.\r\n\r\nDiagnosis, treatment, therapy planning and medication decisions are formal responsibilities. They involve professional registration, accountability and knowledge of the individual person. A volunteer visit does not replace that structure, and it should never be treated as a shortcut.\r\n\r\nIf you have health-related training, the respectful path is to:\r\n\r\n1. Tell the organisation exactly what your qualifications are.\r\n2. Ask what kind of contribution, if any, is appropriate.\r\n3. Follow the organisation's supervision and safeguarding arrangements.\r\n4. Refer any person's individual needs to the qualified professionals already involved.\r\n\r\nYou might, for example, help with general education for caregivers or organise materials, if the organisation agrees. You should not assess, treat or advise on medication informally.\r\n\r\n## Skilled versus general volunteering\r\n\r\n| | Skilled volunteering | General volunteering |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Focus | A particular professional skill | Time, company and practical help |\r\n| Agreement | Scope defined in advance | Tasks assigned as needed |\r\n| Main risk | Working beyond agreed limits | Overcommitting time |\r\n| Main value | Fills specific gaps | Builds community and routine |\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Both kinds of volunteering matter. A dependable volunteer who simply turns up, listens and helps set up can be just as important as one with a professional title.\r\n\r\n## Why fit and respect matter\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization's report on disability explains that people with disabilities often face barriers in education, health services and daily life. Skilled volunteers can help reduce some of these barriers, for example through learning support or clear information. But they can only do so if their work fits with the wider support already around a person.\r\n\r\nThe WHO also describes assistive technology as an important area of support. Anyone with knowledge in this field should remember that choosing and fitting devices is a specialised task best guided by qualified providers.\r\n\r\n## Tips for skilled volunteers\r\n\r\n- Start with listening. Ask what is needed before you offer solutions.\r\n- Share knowledge in a way others can continue without you.\r\n- Be open to being taught by staff, families and the people you support.\r\n- Put agreements in writing where the organisation asks for it.\r\n- Keep your professional boundaries and confidentiality.\r\n- Say no to tasks outside your competence.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation works across neuro-diversity awareness, elderly care support, education and therapy guidance, life skills and caregiver training. We welcome people who want to offer time and skills within clear, safe limits.\r\n\r\nRead about our [programs](/programs), see the [volunteers page](/volunteers) or go to [Get Involved](/get-involved) to begin a conversation about how your skills could fit.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/supporting-a-person-living-with-dementia-routines-calm.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Supporting a Person Living With Dementia: A Daily Checklist\"\r\nslug: supporting-a-person-living-with-dementia-routines-calm\r\ncategory: \"Healthy ageing\"\r\nexcerpt: \"A practical checklist for families and caregivers on routines, clear communication and calm surroundings that help a person living with dementia feel safe.\"\r\ndescription: \"A practical checklist for families and caregivers on routines, clear communication and calm surroundings that help a person living with dementia feel safe.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [dementia, caregiving, routines, communication]\r\ntemplate: checklist\r\nimage: /photos/blog/supporting-a-person-living-with-dementia-routines-calm.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/supporting-a-person-living-with-dementia-routines-calm-thumb.webp\r\nicon: heart-pulse\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"More than 55 million people worldwide live with dementia, according to the WHO, so families everywhere are learning how to support it.\"\r\n  - \"Predictable routines, simple communication and a calm environment can reduce confusion and stress.\"\r\n  - \"Focus on the person's remaining abilities, preferences and dignity, not only on what has changed.\"\r\n  - \"Seek a qualified assessment for memory changes and ask a health professional about medicines or sudden changes.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Is dementia the same as normal ageing?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Some slowing of memory can come with age, but dementia affects thinking, daily tasks and behaviour more seriously. If you notice changes, ask a doctor or nurse for a qualified assessment rather than assuming it is age.\"\r\n  - q: \"How should I respond if the person repeats questions?\"\r\n    a: \"Answer calmly each time, as though it is the first. The person is not doing it on purpose. A short, warm reply, a gentle change of activity or a written note can help. Arguing or reminding them they already asked tends to cause distress.\"\r\n  - q: \"Where can families find more information?\"\r\n    a: \"The WHO dementia fact sheet, NHS dementia pages and the Alzheimer's Society About dementia page explain the condition in plain language. A local doctor, nurse or social worker can also point to support.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia\"\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/dementia/\"\r\n  - title: \"About dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"Alzheimer's Society (UK)\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.alzheimers.org.uk/about-dementia\"\r\n---\r\nA person living with dementia can feel safer and calmer when days follow a familiar pattern, people speak clearly and kindly, and the surroundings are quiet and uncluttered. This checklist collects practical ideas for families and caregivers. It is general information and does not replace advice from a doctor, nurse or social worker.\r\n\r\n## What is dementia, briefly?\r\n\r\nDementia is a term for a group of conditions that affect memory, thinking, mood and the ability to manage daily tasks. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), [more than 55 million people worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia) live with dementia, and Alzheimer's disease is the most common form, making up 60–70% of cases.\r\n\r\nEach person is different. Two people with the same diagnosis may have very different abilities and needs. Getting a qualified assessment matters, because some causes of memory change can be treated. Only a health professional can diagnose dementia or advise on medicines.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 1: Build a gentle daily routine\r\n\r\nRoutine gives structure when memory is unreliable.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Wake, eat, wash and rest at similar times each day.\r\n- [ ] Keep the order of familiar tasks the same, such as tea, a wash, then a walk.\r\n- [ ] Plan the busier tasks for the time of day when the person is usually most alert.\r\n- [ ] Leave room for rest, since tiredness can increase confusion.\r\n- [ ] Include something enjoyable every day, such as music, a chat or time outside.\r\n- [ ] Introduce changes slowly, and explain them simply.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 2: Communicate clearly and kindly\r\n\r\nHow we speak often matters more than what we say.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Approach from the front, make eye contact and say the person's name.\r\n- [ ] Use short sentences and one idea at a time.\r\n- [ ] Ask simple questions, ideally with two choices, such as \"Tea or juice?\"\r\n- [ ] Give time to answer without rushing or finishing sentences.\r\n- [ ] Use gestures, pictures or objects to support your words.\r\n- [ ] Avoid arguing or correcting. Respond to the feeling behind the words.\r\n- [ ] Speak to the person, not about them in front of them.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** If the person becomes upset, try to name the feeling gently (\"You seem worried\") and offer comfort, before trying to explain or fix the facts.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 3: Create a calm, safe environment\r\n\r\nA quiet setting reduces overload.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Reduce background noise, such as a radio and television playing together.\r\n- [ ] Keep rooms well lit, and avoid shadows and glare.\r\n- [ ] Keep floors clear of loose items, rugs and trip hazards.\r\n- [ ] Put familiar objects and photographs where the person can see them.\r\n- [ ] Label or mark important places, such as the toilet, with clear signs or pictures.\r\n- [ ] Keep medicines, sharp tools and cleaning products stored safely.\r\n- [ ] Consider safety around doors and gates if the person may wander, and never leave them alone in an unsafe situation.\r\n\r\n## Checklist 4: Protect dignity and independence\r\n\r\nDementia changes many things, but a person remains a person.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Let the person do what they safely can, even if it is slower.\r\n- [ ] Offer choices about clothes, food and activities.\r\n- [ ] Help with personal care privately and respectfully.\r\n- [ ] Keep involving them in family life and conversation.\r\n- [ ] Draw on life history, such as work, songs, food and faith, to spark connection.\r\n\r\nSome of these ideas overlap with keeping the mind active. See our article on [keeping older people mentally stimulated and socially connected](/blog/article-keeping-older-people-mentally-stimulated-and-connected).\r\n\r\n## Checklist 5: Look after the caregiver\r\n\r\nCaring for a person living with dementia is demanding, and caregivers need support too.\r\n\r\n- [ ] Share tasks among family members and neighbours.\r\n- [ ] Take regular breaks, even short ones.\r\n- [ ] Talk with others who understand, such as a community or faith group.\r\n- [ ] Ask a health professional or social worker about local support.\r\n- [ ] Be kind to yourself. You will have hard days, and that is normal.\r\n\r\n## When should you seek professional help?\r\n\r\nContact a doctor or nurse if the person shows sudden confusion, a rapid change in behaviour, new pain, a fall or refusal to eat or drink. Sudden change can be caused by an infection or other treatable problem. Always ask a professional before changing any medicine.\r\n\r\nFor an overview of ageing well, see [healthy ageing: nutrition, movement and meaningful activity](/blog/article-healthy-ageing-nutrition-movement-meaningful-activity).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania supports older people, including those living with Alzheimer's and dementia, and their families and caregivers. Our approach centres on dignity, practical guidance and shared understanding. See our [elderly care program](/programs/program-elderly-care) for more, or [contact us](/contact) to ask how we can help.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/supporting-brothers-and-sisters-of-a-child-with-special-needs.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Supporting Brothers and Sisters of a Child with Special Needs\"\r\nslug: supporting-brothers-and-sisters-of-a-child-with-special-needs\r\ncategory: Family guidance\r\nexcerpt: \"A values-based look at how families can make room for siblings' feelings, questions and needs while caring for a child with a disability.\"\r\ndescription: \"A values-based look at how families can make room for siblings' feelings, questions and needs while caring for a child with a disability.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [siblings, family, inclusion, parenting, wellbeing]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/supporting-brothers-and-sisters-of-a-child-with-special-needs.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/supporting-brothers-and-sisters-of-a-child-with-special-needs-thumb.webp\r\nicon: book-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Brothers and sisters have their own feelings, needs and worth, and deserve attention in their own right.\"\r\n  - \"Honest, age-appropriate explanations help siblings understand and feel included.\"\r\n  - \"Siblings should be children first, not automatic carers.\"\r\n  - \"Mixed feelings are normal, and talking about them openly protects wellbeing.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Is it normal for a sibling to feel jealous or upset?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Many children feel proud, loving, confused, embarrassed or left out, sometimes all in one day. These feelings are not wrong. What helps most is listening without judgement and giving them time and attention of their own.\"\r\n  - q: \"How much should I tell a brother or sister about the disability?\"\r\n    a: \"Use simple, honest words that suit their age, and add more as they grow. Encourage questions. Children often imagine worse things when they are told nothing, so clear and kind explanations usually help.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should siblings help with caring duties?\"\r\n    a: \"Some helping can be a natural part of family life, but it should not become a heavy or constant responsibility. Siblings need time to play, study and rest. If you are unsure about balance, talk to a counsellor or social worker.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Children with disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nBrothers and sisters of a child with special needs are children too, with their own hopes, worries and need for attention. We believe a family is strongest when every child is seen, heard and valued equally. This article shares a perspective on how families can support siblings with honesty, fairness and warmth.\r\n\r\n## Why do siblings deserve their own attention?\r\n\r\nWhen one child needs extra care, therapy visits or close supervision, family time and energy are stretched. That is understandable. Yet a brother or sister may quietly notice they are asked to be patient, to wait or to understand more than most children their age.\r\n\r\nThis is not a criticism of any parent or caregiver. Most families do their best with limited time. But to say it clearly: every child in the family has equal value. The rights ideas behind the UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), such as dignity, respect for difference and inclusion, begin at home, in how we treat each other every day.\r\n\r\n## What might a sibling be feeling?\r\n\r\nEvery child is different, but many siblings experience a mix of feelings at once.\r\n\r\n- **Love and pride.** Many siblings are fiercely protective and proud of their brother or sister.\r\n- **Confusion.** They may not understand why their sibling behaves or learns differently.\r\n- **Frustration.** Shared toys, noise or routines can feel hard.\r\n- **Feeling left out.** Time and attention may seem to go elsewhere.\r\n- **Worry.** They may fear for their sibling's health or their parents' tiredness.\r\n- **Embarrassment.** Especially among friends or in public, and often followed by guilt.\r\n\r\nNone of these feelings is wrong. Naming them helps children know they are normal.\r\n\r\n## Talking honestly, at the right level\r\n\r\nChildren fill silence with their own ideas, which are sometimes scarier than the truth. Simple, honest explanations are kinder.\r\n\r\n### Use plain words\r\n\r\nFor a younger child, you might say: \"Your brother's brain works in a different way, so some things are harder for him and some things are easy. He still loves to play with you.\" Older children can be told more as they ask.\r\n\r\n### Invite questions, and it is fine not to know\r\n\r\nYou do not need every answer. \"That is a good question. Let us ask the therapist together\" shows children that curiosity is welcome.\r\n\r\n### Speak respectfully about the disability\r\n\r\nThe words families use become the words children use. Focus on what the child can do and what support helps them. Avoid pity or phrases that suggest the child is a burden.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Families use different words for describing disability, such as \"person with a disability\" or, for autism, \"autistic person\" or \"person with autism\". Listening to the words your family member prefers is a respectful place to start.\r\n\r\n## Protecting a sibling's childhood\r\n\r\n### Time of their own\r\n\r\nEven a short, regular moment matters: a walk, a game, helping with dinner or a chat before sleep. The message is, \"You matter to me for who you are, not for how you help.\"\r\n\r\n### Helping, but not as a full-time carer\r\n\r\nSmall tasks can build love and responsibility. But brothers and sisters should not become the main carer. Children need time to play, learn, rest and make friends.\r\n\r\n### Their own interests and achievements\r\n\r\nCelebrate school, sport, art and friendships. It is easy for good news to be overlooked during demanding weeks. Let siblings share it with pride.\r\n\r\n## Helping siblings become friends and allies\r\n\r\nSiblings often build unique bonds. You can nurture these by:\r\n\r\n1. Finding shared activities that both children enjoy, such as music, drawing or games outside.\r\n2. Teaching a sibling simple ways to communicate that work for their brother or sister, if a therapist has suggested them.\r\n3. Showing that differences are valued, not hidden.\r\n4. Letting siblings share their views when family decisions are made, in ways that suit their age.\r\n5. Welcoming their friends into the home so they do not have to choose between worlds.\r\n\r\n## When is extra support needed?\r\n\r\nWatch for signs that a brother or sister might be struggling: withdrawing, unusual anger, trouble sleeping, changes at school, headaches or stomach aches with no clear cause, or seeming very anxious. These signs do not always mean something serious, but they are worth a gentle conversation. If they continue, consider speaking to a doctor, teacher, counsellor or social worker.\r\n\r\nExtended family and community can also help. In many Tanzanian families, aunts, uncles, grandparents and neighbours are part of daily life. A trusted relative can offer one-to-one time to a sibling and give parents a moment to breathe.\r\n\r\nThis article offers a general perspective. It is not counselling or clinical advice.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania works to support families, not only individual children, through education and therapy guidance and specialised parenting support. Every family member counts. Read more on the [Education and Therapy Guidance page](/programs/program-education-therapy). You may also like our reflections on [understanding neuro-diversity beyond labels](/blog/article-understanding-neurodiversity-beyond-labels) and our [community awareness](/blog/article-community-awareness-to-everyday-inclusion) article.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/supporting-families-with-consistent-care-routines.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Supporting Families With Consistent Care Routines\"\r\nslug: supporting-families-with-consistent-care-routines\r\ncategory: Caregiving\r\nexcerpt: \"Why steady daily routines help people with disabilities and older people feel safe, and how families can build routines that are calm, flexible and shared.\"\r\ndescription: \"Why steady daily routines help people with disabilities and older people feel safe, and how families can build routines that are calm, flexible and shared.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [caregiving, routines, family, wellbeing]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/supporting-families-with-consistent-care-routines.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/supporting-families-with-consistent-care-routines-thumb.webp\r\nicon: users-round\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"A predictable routine helps many people feel safe, because they know what comes next.\"\r\n  - \"Good routines are simple, written or pictured, and flexible enough to change on hard days.\"\r\n  - \"Routines work best when the person is involved and when the tasks are shared across the family.\"\r\n  - \"A routine supports care and does not replace advice from doctors, therapists or nurses.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Why do routines matter so much in caregiving?\"\r\n    a: \"Routines make the day predictable. When a person knows what usually happens next, there is often less worry and fewer difficult moments. They also help caregivers plan energy, avoid forgetting important tasks and share work more fairly.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the person resists the routine?\"\r\n    a: \"Resistance is a message. It may mean the routine is too fast, too crowded or not meaningful to the person. Try changing one small thing at a time, ask the person what would help, and share your observations with a qualified professional.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should a routine be strict?\"\r\n    a: \"No. A routine should be a steady framework, not a rulebook. Illness, tiredness, visitors and special days will change things. A calm return to the usual pattern matters more than perfect timing.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can routines help people living with dementia?\"\r\n    a: \"Many families find familiar patterns comforting for older people living with dementia. Routines cannot change the condition, but they can make days feel less confusing. A doctor or nurse can advise on the needs of the individual.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n---\r\nA consistent care routine gives a person a predictable shape to the day, and that predictability often brings calm, safety and confidence. It also gives caregivers a clear plan, so the work feels lighter and more shared. This article explains why routines help and how a family can build one that fits real life.\r\n\r\n## Why do routines help?\r\n\r\nMany of us rely on routines without noticing. We wake, wash, eat and rest in a familiar order. For a person with a disability, an autistic person, or an older person living with dementia, that familiar order can matter even more. Knowing what comes next reduces uncertainty, and less uncertainty can mean less stress.\r\n\r\nRoutines help the caregiver too. When the day has a rhythm, tasks such as meals, movement, rest and medicine reminders are less likely to be forgotten. Note that any medicine plan must come from a doctor or nurse. A routine only helps the family follow it.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [1.3 billion people, about 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Behind that figure are many families arranging daily life at home. Simple, shared routines are one of the most practical tools they have.\r\n\r\n## What makes a routine work?\r\n\r\nNot every routine helps. A good one usually has these qualities:\r\n\r\n- **Simple.** A few anchor points, such as waking, meals, activity, rest and bedtime, are easier to keep than a full timetable.\r\n- **Visible.** A written list, a drawing or photos on a wall help the person see the day. This is especially useful for people who understand pictures better than spoken words.\r\n- **Flexible.** The order matters more than the exact time. Hard days will come.\r\n- **Meaningful.** Include things the person enjoys and can do, not only tasks that must be done.\r\n- **Shared.** If one person carries everything, the routine will not last.\r\n\r\n## A routine is not the same as control\r\n\r\nThe goal is not to run the person's life by a clock. Dignity means the person takes part wherever possible. Ask what they prefer. Offer choices inside the routine, such as which shirt to wear or which activity comes first.\r\n\r\nFor a child, involvement might mean placing a picture on a chart. For an older person, it might mean deciding when to take a walk or which chair to sit in for the morning. Each choice keeps a sense of independence alive.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Routines support care. They do not replace advice from a doctor, therapist or nurse. If you notice sudden changes, share them with a qualified professional.\r\n\r\n## How can a family build a routine step by step?\r\n\r\n### Start with what already happens\r\n\r\nWatch a normal week. Notice when the person is most alert, when they are tired, and which parts of the day are hardest. Build around these natural patterns.\r\n\r\n### Choose a few anchors\r\n\r\nPick three or four fixed points. Meals and bedtime are common anchors. Add one movement or activity block and one quiet block.\r\n\r\n### Prepare for transitions\r\n\r\nMoving from one activity to another is often the difficult moment. A gentle warning, a familiar phrase or a visual cue can help. Give the person time. Rushing usually makes things harder.\r\n\r\n### Include movement and rest\r\n\r\nActivity and rest both belong in the day. The WHO recommends regular physical activity for adults, including adults living with disability, and an average of 60 minutes a day for children and adolescents. Any activity plan should suit the person, and water or exercise activities need supervision. Ask a health professional what is safe for the individual.\r\n\r\n### Review and adjust\r\n\r\nTry the routine for a while and notice what works. Change one thing at a time. If something keeps causing distress, that is useful information to share with a professional.\r\n\r\n## What if the routine breaks down?\r\n\r\nIt will, sometimes. A journey, an illness, a funeral or a busy market day can disrupt any plan. This does not mean the routine has failed.\r\n\r\n- Keep the anchors, such as meals and bedtime, even if the rest changes.\r\n- Tell the person in advance about changes when you can.\r\n- Return to the usual pattern without blame.\r\n\r\nExtended families are common in Tanzania, and many relatives may help in different ways. That can be a strength. Agree together on who does what, so the person receives the same calm approach from everyone. Our article on [a checklist for sharing care tasks across a family](/blog/article-sharing-care-tasks-across-a-family-checklist) may help with this step.\r\n\r\n## Looking after the caregiver inside the routine\r\n\r\nA routine should include time for the caregiver to eat, rest and see other people. If the plan has no space for the carer, it is not a sustainable plan. Read more about this in [why caregivers need support too](/blog/article-why-caregivers-need-support-too).\r\n\r\nA short written record of the day, such as sleep, meals and mood, also helps when you meet a professional. See [how to keep a simple daily observation log](/blog/article-keeping-a-simple-daily-observation-log).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania supports people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and their families and caregivers. Caregiver training is one of our five program areas. It focuses on practical, respectful skills for daily care, including routines that suit each home. Learn more on the [caregiver training program page](/programs/program-caregiver-training), or [contact us](/contact) with questions.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/transparent-reporting-as-programs-grow.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"What Transparent Reporting Should Show as Programs Grow\"\r\nslug: transparent-reporting-as-programs-grow\r\ncategory: Transparency\r\nexcerpt: \"As a non-profit grows, its reporting must grow with it. Here is what a clear report should show about activities, learning, limits and money.\"\r\ndescription: \"As a non-profit grows, its reporting must grow with it. Here is what a clear report should show about activities, learning, limits and money.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [transparency, reporting, accountability, donors]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/transparent-reporting-as-programs-grow.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/transparent-reporting-as-programs-grow-thumb.webp\r\nicon: file-check-2\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Transparent reporting is not the same as impressive reporting. It shows what was done, what was learned and what is still hard.\"\r\n  - \"As programs grow, reports must explain more, not just show larger totals.\"\r\n  - \"Good reports include limits, mistakes and changes of plan.\"\r\n  - \"Readers should be able to tell facts from hopes, and small numbers from vague claims.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is transparent reporting?\"\r\n    a: \"It means an organisation openly shares what it does, how it uses resources, what it has learned and where it fell short, in plain language. The goal is that supporters and families can understand and question the work, not only admire it.\"\r\n  - q: \"Why does reporting need to change as a non-profit grows?\"\r\n    a: \"More programs, more people and more money mean more that can go wrong and more that readers need to understand. A short note may be enough at the start. Later, readers need clear structure, consistent measures and plain explanations of change.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should a report only share good news?\"\r\n    a: \"No. A report with only good news is hard to trust. Useful reports also share what did not work, what was changed and what the organisation still does not know. Honesty about limits builds more trust than polish.\"\r\n  - q: \"How does Eagle's Wings approach reporting?\"\r\n    a: \"The Foundation is young and does not publish numeric results yet. Its Impact page names what it intends to report on: reach, learning, practice and experience. It aims to report only what it can honestly stand behind.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"IATI Standard\"\r\n    publisher: \"International Aid Transparency Initiative\"\r\n    url: \"https://iatistandard.org/en/\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nTransparent reporting shows readers what an organisation really did, what it learned and where it struggled. It is not the same as impressive reporting. As programs grow, the report should get clearer and more complete, not just bigger.\r\n\r\nThis is a values-based view from the Eagle's Wings Editorial Team. It is not a report of results. The Foundation, founded in 2025 in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, does not currently publish numeric results, and this article does not claim any.\r\n\r\n## Why does growth make reporting harder?\r\n\r\nA very small organisation can explain its work in a few sentences. Everyone who cares about it can ask questions directly. When programs multiply, that closeness fades. Supporters, families and partners can no longer see everything for themselves.\r\n\r\nAt that point the report has to do the seeing for them. It must be organised, consistent and readable by people who were not in the room. If it is not, gaps fill with assumptions, and assumptions are often more flattering than the truth.\r\n\r\n## What should a growing organisation show?\r\n\r\nA useful report answers a few plain questions. Each one below is a promise to the reader.\r\n\r\n### What did we do?\r\n\r\nDescribe the activities in ordinary words. Say who took part in general terms, where the work happened and what a session or service actually looked like. Avoid vague phrases such as \"empowered communities\" unless the report explains what that means in practice.\r\n\r\n### What did we learn?\r\n\r\nLearning is more useful than applause. A good report says what worked, what did not, and what will change. It admits when a plan had to be adjusted, and why.\r\n\r\n### What are the limits?\r\n\r\nEvery program has boundaries. A report should say what the organisation does not do, what it cannot promise, and where it depends on other professionals such as doctors, therapists and social workers.\r\n\r\n### How was money used?\r\n\r\nReaders deserve a clear description of where funds came from and how they were spent, in categories they can follow. The point is not perfection. The point is that the story adds up.\r\n\r\n## What does good measurement look like?\r\n\r\nNumbers can help, but only when they are clearly explained. A small number that is clearly explained is worth more than a large number nobody can check. A good report says what was counted, how it was counted and what the number cannot tell us.\r\n\r\nIt also keeps facts and hopes apart. \"We plan to\" is not the same as \"we did\". Readers should never have to guess which one they are reading.\r\n\r\nGlobal context can be quoted, but it must be labelled as global context. For example, the World Health Organization estimates that about 16% of the world's population experiences significant disability. That figure describes the world. It says nothing about what any single organisation has achieved.\r\n\r\n## How should mistakes and changes be handled?\r\n\r\nGrowth involves mistakes. A session may be too crowded. A plan may not fit a family's real life. A message may be worded badly.\r\n\r\nTransparent organisations say so. They explain the change and what they did about it. This does not weaken trust. It shows that someone is paying attention.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** A report that never mentions a difficulty is not proof that there were none. It is a reason to ask more questions.\r\n\r\n## How does dignity fit into reporting?\r\n\r\nReporting about people with disabilities and older people carries extra responsibility. Reports should never expose individuals for the sake of a good story. Consent matters, privacy matters, and respectful language matters.\r\n\r\nThe [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) is built on dignity, autonomy and participation. A report that treats people as material for fundraising works against those ideas. A report can be moving without being intrusive. Our related article on [why consent matters in non-profit storytelling](/blog/article-why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling) explains this in more detail.\r\n\r\n## Can open standards help?\r\n\r\nYes. Some organisations publish their information in common, shared formats so that others can compare and check it. The International Aid Transparency Initiative offers one such standard, mainly used in international aid. Even organisations that do not use it can borrow its spirit: publish clearly, publish consistently, and let people look for themselves.\r\n\r\n## What should a reader do?\r\n\r\nReaders have a role too. Ask for plain answers. Notice when a report avoids a question. Compare reports from year to year, and look for whether the organisation says what it learned. Trust is built when both sides take part.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nThe Foundation is at an early stage. It would rather say so than pretend otherwise. Its [Impact page](/impact) sets out the four things it intends to report on: reach, learning, practice and experience. It will not describe results it cannot honestly support.\r\n\r\nWhen reporting becomes available, it will be published on this website. If you have a question about how the Foundation works, please [contact us](/contact) and ask.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/un-convention-rights-persons-with-disabilities-explained.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"The UN Disability Rights Convention (CRPD) Explained Simply\"\r\nslug: un-convention-rights-persons-with-disabilities-explained\r\ncategory: Inclusion\r\nexcerpt: \"A plain-language explainer of the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities: what it is, its core principles and why it matters to families.\"\r\ndescription: \"A plain-language explainer of the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities: what it is, its core principles and why it matters to families.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [crpd, rights, disability, dignity]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/un-convention-rights-persons-with-disabilities-explained.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/un-convention-rights-persons-with-disabilities-explained-thumb.webp\r\nicon: megaphone\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"The CRPD is a United Nations human rights treaty, adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008.\"\r\n  - \"It does not create new rights; it makes clear that existing human rights apply fully to people with disabilities.\"\r\n  - \"Its principles include dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination, participation, accessibility and respect for difference.\"\r\n  - \"It shifts the focus from fixing the person to removing barriers in society.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is the CRPD?\"\r\n    a: \"The Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities is an international human rights treaty of the United Nations. It states that people with disabilities have the same human rights as everyone and sets out how societies should respect and protect them.\"\r\n  - q: \"When was it adopted?\"\r\n    a: \"The United Nations adopted the Convention in 2006, and it came into force in 2008. Countries choose whether to join, and each country has its own laws, so check with official sources for what applies where you live.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does it give special privileges?\"\r\n    a: \"No. It recognises that people with disabilities are entitled to the same rights as others, and that barriers in society often stop them from using those rights. Accessibility and support are about equal opportunity, not special treatment.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nThe [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), often called the CRPD, is a United Nations human rights treaty. It was adopted in 2006 and came into force in 2008. Its central message is simple: people with disabilities have the same human rights as everyone else, and society must remove the barriers that stand in the way.\r\n\r\n## What is the CRPD?\r\n\r\nThe CRPD is an international agreement. Countries that choose to join commit to respecting, protecting and promoting the rights it describes. It is one of several UN human rights treaties, and it was created because people with disabilities were often left out of the rights other people took for granted.\r\n\r\nThe Convention does not invent new rights. Instead, it spells out how existing human rights, such as education, health, work, family life and freedom from harm, apply to people with disabilities. This clarity helps governments, schools, employers and communities know what respect looks like in practice.\r\n\r\n## Why was it needed?\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, roughly 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Despite this, many people with disabilities have faced exclusion from schools, workplaces, health services and public life, often because of barriers and attitudes, not because of the disability itself.\r\n\r\nThe CRPD reflects a change in thinking. Older approaches treated disability mainly as a personal medical problem or a matter of charity. The Convention supports a rights-based view: disability arises when a person with an impairment meets barriers in the environment or in attitudes. The task is to reduce the barriers.\r\n\r\n## What are its core principles?\r\n\r\nThe Convention rests on a few big ideas. Here they are in everyday words.\r\n\r\n| Principle | What it means in daily life |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Dignity | Every person has worth, and no one is treated as a burden |\r\n| Autonomy | People make their own choices, with support where needed |\r\n| Non-discrimination | People are not treated worse because of disability |\r\n| Participation and inclusion | People take part fully in community life |\r\n| Respect for difference | Disability is part of human diversity |\r\n| Accessibility | Places, information and services can be used by all |\r\n\r\nThese ideas overlap. For example, real participation needs accessibility, and real autonomy needs respect for a person's own voice.\r\n\r\n## What areas of life does it cover?\r\n\r\nThe Convention covers many parts of daily life. In broad terms, it addresses:\r\n\r\n- education and learning\r\n- health and rehabilitation\r\n- work and employment\r\n- family life and relationships\r\n- access to information, buildings and transport\r\n- protection from violence, abuse and neglect\r\n- taking part in community, cultural and public life\r\n\r\nEach area follows the same principle: people with disabilities should have equal opportunities, and reasonable support and changes should be made so that they can use their rights.\r\n\r\n## What does \"nothing about us without us\" mean?\r\n\r\nThis phrase, widely used by disability rights advocates, captures how the Convention was shaped and how it should be used. Decisions that affect people with disabilities should involve them, along with their families and representative organisations.\r\n\r\nIt also applies at community level. A school, faith group or neighbourhood association that wants to include people should ask them what is needed and listen to the answers. Our [inclusion checklist](/blog/article-inclusion-checklist-schools-faith-groups-neighbourhoods) turns this idea into practical questions.\r\n\r\n## How is the CRPD used in each country?\r\n\r\nA treaty sets shared principles. Each country decides whether to join and how to put the principles into national law and practice. This means details differ from place to place. This article is general education and not legal advice. To find out which laws and services apply where you live, consult official government sources or a qualified legal professional.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** You can read the Convention itself, and helpful plain summaries, on the United Nations disability pages listed in the sources below.\r\n\r\n## Why does the CRPD matter to families?\r\n\r\nFor families and caregivers, the Convention offers language and confidence. It says that wanting your child to go to school with peers, or your older relative to be treated with dignity, is not asking for a favour. It is asking for rights.\r\n\r\nIt also supports the idea that families need support too. Caring for someone is easier when services, information and community attitudes are welcoming.\r\n\r\n## What can communities do with it?\r\n\r\nYou do not need to be a lawyer to use the Convention's spirit. Communities can ask simple questions.\r\n\r\n1. Who is missing from our activities, and why?\r\n2. What barriers, physical or attitudinal, could we reduce?\r\n3. Are people with disabilities helping to decide what we do?\r\n4. Are we speaking about people with dignity? See our [respectful language guide](/blog/article-respectful-language-about-disability-guide).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, was founded in 2025 with a vision that people with special needs and disabilities are valued equally and can reach their full potential. Rights and dignity is one of our four pillars, and the CRPD's principles are close to how we think about our work.\r\n\r\nExplore our [neuro-diversity awareness program](/programs/program-neurodiversity), read our [mission and vision](/about#mission), or see how to [get involved](/get-involved).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/understanding-common-terms-in-therapy-and-assessment.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Common Terms in Therapy and Assessment, in Plain Language\"\r\nslug: understanding-common-terms-in-therapy-and-assessment\r\ncategory: Family guidance\r\nexcerpt: \"A plain-language guide to words families often hear, such as assessment, therapy, early intervention and intellectual disability, and what they mean.\"\r\ndescription: \"A plain-language guide to words families often hear, such as assessment, therapy, early intervention and intellectual disability, and what they mean.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [glossary, assessment, early-intervention, intellectual-disability, families]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/understanding-common-terms-in-therapy-and-assessment.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/understanding-common-terms-in-therapy-and-assessment-thumb.webp\r\nicon: book-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Professional words can be explained simply, and you are always welcome to ask.\"\r\n  - \"An assessment describes strengths and needs, and therapy and support build on them.\"\r\n  - \"Early support matters, but it is never too late to seek help.\"\r\n  - \"This glossary is general education, not a diagnosis or medical advice.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Is an assessment the same as a diagnosis?\"\r\n    a: \"Not exactly. An assessment is the process of gathering information about a person's abilities and needs. A diagnosis is a conclusion a qualified professional may reach from it. Assessments also help plan support, even when no diagnosis is given.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does early intervention mean a child is behind?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Early intervention means offering support, such as therapy, play-based learning or family guidance, early in a child's life. It is about giving a child helpful tools and giving families confidence, not about labelling or blaming.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who can carry out a proper assessment?\"\r\n    a: \"Qualified professionals such as doctors, psychologists and therapists working in their areas of training. If you are worried about your child, speak to a doctor or another qualified professional about the right place to go.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Children with disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"UNICEF\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unicef.org/disabilities\"\r\n  - title: \"ADHD\"\r\n    publisher: \"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.cdc.gov/adhd/index.html\"\r\n---\r\nMany words used in therapy and education are simple ideas in technical clothing. This guide explains common terms in plain language so you can follow appointments with confidence. It is general education only, and a qualified professional should explain what any term means for your own child.\r\n\r\n## Why does the language feel so hard?\r\n\r\nProfessionals learn specialised words during training and use them daily. Families hear them for the first time, often at a stressful moment. You do not have to learn all of them. It is always fine to say, \"Please explain that in simpler words.\"\r\n\r\nThe terms below are grouped by theme. Meanings can vary slightly between services and countries, so treat them as a starting point.\r\n\r\n## Words about finding out what a person needs\r\n\r\n### What is an assessment?\r\n\r\nAn assessment is a process of gathering information about a person's abilities, needs and daily life. It may include talking with the family, observing the person, playing or working on tasks together, and reading reports. A good assessment describes strengths as well as challenges.\r\n\r\n### What is a diagnosis?\r\n\r\nA diagnosis is a name that a qualified professional may give after an assessment, such as autism or Down syndrome. It can help families find support and understand what to expect. A diagnosis does not define a person's worth or limit what they can become.\r\n\r\n### What does screening mean?\r\n\r\nScreening is a quick check to see whether a fuller assessment may be useful. It is not a diagnosis. If a screening raises questions, a qualified professional can explain the next step.\r\n\r\n## Words about support\r\n\r\n### What is therapy?\r\n\r\nTherapy means planned support to help a person build skills, manage challenges and take part in daily life. There are different kinds, for example:\r\n\r\n- **Speech and language therapy:** communication, understanding and, in some cases, feeding and swallowing.\r\n- **Occupational therapy:** everyday skills such as dressing, eating, play and handwriting.\r\n- **Physiotherapy (physical therapy):** movement, strength and balance.\r\n- **Behavioural or psychological support:** understanding feelings, behaviour and coping.\r\n\r\nWhich kind is suitable depends on the person, and a qualified professional should decide.\r\n\r\n### What is early intervention?\r\n\r\nEarly intervention means support offered early in a child's life, when they are learning and growing quickly. It can include therapy, play-based learning and guidance for the family. The goal is to help a child reach their potential and to give families tools and confidence. Support is valuable at any age, so do not feel it is too late.\r\n\r\n### What is a therapy plan or goals?\r\n\r\nA therapy plan is a set of steps agreed between the professional and the family. Goals are the small, specific things the plan aims for, such as \"asks for help using a word or gesture\". Good goals are realistic and meaningful to the family.\r\n\r\n### What is specialised parenting guidance?\r\n\r\nThis is support that helps parents and carers understand a child's needs and learn practical ways to encourage learning, communication and independence at home. It does not mean parents did anything wrong.\r\n\r\n## Words about differences in how people learn and think\r\n\r\n### What is neuro-diversity?\r\n\r\nNeuro-diversity is the idea that people's brains work in a range of ways, and that this variation is natural. The term is often used for autism, ADHD and other differences in learning and thinking. It encourages us to support people rather than try to make everyone the same.\r\n\r\n### What is autism?\r\n\r\nAutism is a lifelong difference in how a person communicates, interacts and experiences the world. It is described as a spectrum because it looks different for every person and support needs vary greatly. The World Health Organization estimates that [about 1 in 100 children worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism. Some people prefer \"autistic person\" and others \"person with autism\", so it is good to ask what someone prefers.\r\n\r\n### What is ADHD?\r\n\r\nADHD (attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder) is a condition linked to differences in attention, activity levels and impulse control. Support can include understanding, routines, school adjustments and, where advised by a doctor, other treatments.\r\n\r\n### What is intellectual disability?\r\n\r\nIntellectual disability means a person finds it harder to learn, reason and manage everyday skills, and needs support with these. It begins in childhood. People with intellectual disability have strengths, personalities and dreams like anyone else, and with the right support many take part fully in community life.\r\n\r\n### What causes Down syndrome?\r\n\r\nDown syndrome is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21. It is a lifelong condition, and people with Down syndrome learn and grow in different ways and at their own pace, and can be part of school, work and family life.\r\n\r\n## Words about school and rights\r\n\r\n### What is inclusive education?\r\n\r\nInclusive education means children with different abilities learn together, with support and adjustments so that everyone can take part. It is based on respect and belonging.\r\n\r\n### What are reasonable adjustments, or accommodations?\r\n\r\nThese are practical changes that help a person take part, such as extra time, a quiet space, pictures to explain a task or a different way of showing what they know.\r\n\r\n### What is the CRPD?\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) was adopted in 2006 and came into force in 2008. It is built on dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination, participation, inclusion, accessibility and respect for difference.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Keep a small list of new words from each appointment, with the meaning as explained by the professional. Over time you will build your own family glossary.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania offers education and therapy guidance that explains conditions, therapy exercises and parenting approaches in plain language. To learn more, visit our [Education and Therapy Guidance page](/programs/program-education-therapy). For more on differences in thinking and learning, read [understanding neuro-diversity beyond labels](/blog/article-understanding-neurodiversity-beyond-labels), or see our [FAQ](/faq).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/understanding-neurodiversity-beyond-labels.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Understanding Neuro-diversity Beyond Labels\"\r\nslug: understanding-neurodiversity-beyond-labels\r\ncategory: Neuro-diversity\r\nexcerpt: \"Neuro-diversity means brains work in many natural ways. Learn why looking past labels helps families and communities offer better support.\"\r\ndescription: \"Neuro-diversity means brains work in many natural ways. Learn why looking past labels helps families and communities offer better support.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [neurodiversity, inclusion, autism, dignity]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/understanding-neurodiversity-beyond-labels.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/understanding-neurodiversity-beyond-labels-thumb.webp\r\nicon: brain\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Neuro-diversity means people's brains naturally work in different ways. It is a description of human variety, not a problem to fix.\"\r\n  - \"A label can open the door to support, but it never tells the whole story of a person.\"\r\n  - \"Inclusion works best when we change environments and attitudes, not only the person.\"\r\n  - \"Every person deserves dignity, choice and the support they need to take part in community life.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Is neuro-diversity the same as a diagnosis?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Neuro-diversity describes the natural variety in how human brains think, learn, communicate and sense the world. A diagnosis is a separate step made by qualified professionals to help identify support needs. A person does not need a diagnosis to deserve respect.\"\r\n  - q: \"Does neuro-diversity mean support is not needed?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Many neurodivergent people need real support, and some need a lot of it. Accepting difference and providing support go together. Good support helps a person take part and live with dignity.\"\r\n  - q: \"Should I use the word autistic or person with autism?\"\r\n    a: \"Both are used. Some people prefer identity-first language such as autistic person, and others prefer person with autism. The kindest approach is to ask the person or family which they prefer and then use it.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n---\r\nNeuro-diversity is the idea that human brains naturally differ in how they learn, focus, communicate and experience the world. It asks us to see a person first, not only a label. Understanding this helps families, schools and communities offer support that fits the individual.\r\n\r\n## What does neuro-diversity actually mean?\r\n\r\nThink of a garden. It is healthier and more beautiful with many kinds of plants, and each plant needs slightly different care. People are similar. Some think in pictures, some in words, some notice tiny details, and some need more time to process a busy room.\r\n\r\nThe word *neurodivergent* is often used for people whose brains work differently from what society expects. This can include autistic people, people with ADHD, people with Down syndrome, and people with intellectual disability. *Neurotypical* describes people whose brains follow the more common pattern.\r\n\r\nNeuro-diversity does not say that every challenge disappears. It says that difference is part of being human, and that the world should make room for it.\r\n\r\n## Why labels help, and where they fall short\r\n\r\nA diagnosis or label can be useful. It can explain why a child finds noise painful. It can help a family reach therapy, school support or a community of others who understand. For many people, a name brings relief.\r\n\r\nBut a label is only a starting point. Two autistic children can be very different. One may love routines and quiet; another may be full of energy and talk constantly. The label tells us little about their favourite games, their sense of humour or their dreams.\r\n\r\nHere are some common traps to avoid:\r\n\r\n- Assuming a label describes everything about a person.\r\n- Deciding in advance what a person cannot do.\r\n- Speaking about a person as if they are not in the room.\r\n- Using a label as an insult or a joke.\r\n\r\n## The shift from \"fixing\" to \"fitting\"\r\n\r\nFor a long time, many societies asked people with disabilities to change so they could fit in. A more respectful view asks a second question: what in the environment could change?\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html) (CRPD), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on ideas such as dignity, autonomy, non-discrimination, participation, accessibility and respect for difference. In simple words, the person is not the problem. Barriers are.\r\n\r\nSome examples of barriers that can change:\r\n\r\n- A classroom with constant loud noise.\r\n- Instructions given only in long spoken sentences.\r\n- Events with no quiet space.\r\n- Attitudes that treat difference as something shameful.\r\n\r\n## Why does this matter so much?\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [about 1.3 billion people, roughly 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. Disability, in other words, is a common part of life in every country, and Tanzania is no exception. Alongside this, WHO notes that [about 1 in 100 children worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders) has autism, and that support needs vary greatly.\r\n\r\nThose figures remind us that these are not rare or distant issues. They are about our neighbours, classmates, relatives and colleagues in Dar es Salaam and in every community.\r\n\r\n### Stigma can hurt more than the condition\r\n\r\nIn many places, families face blame, silence or fear when a child develops differently. Some hide a child at home. This isolation can be more harmful than the difference itself. Children who are included, spoken to and encouraged usually have more chances to learn and grow.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Neuro-diversity is not a medical claim that all conditions are the same. It is a way of seeing people with respect. Diagnosis, therapy and any medical decisions should always involve qualified professionals.\r\n\r\n## How can we practise this in everyday life?\r\n\r\nYou do not need special training to begin. A few habits make a real difference.\r\n\r\n1. **Ask, do not assume.** Ask the person or their caregiver what helps.\r\n2. **Focus on strengths.** Notice what a person enjoys and does well, and build from there.\r\n3. **Adjust the environment.** Reduce noise, give extra time, use simple words and pictures.\r\n4. **Use respectful language.** Say \"a child with autism\" or \"an autistic child\" according to the person's preference, and avoid words like \"suffers from\".\r\n5. **Include, do not just tolerate.** Invite people to take part in games, meals, lessons and celebrations.\r\n6. **Support caregivers.** Families who feel understood are better able to support their children.\r\n\r\n## A family example (hypothetical)\r\n\r\nImagine a family whose young son covers his ears at busy markets. A neighbour might call him \"difficult\". Another neighbour might say, \"Let us walk in the quiet hour, and he can hold my hand.\" Nothing about the boy changed, but the second response opened the way for him to take part. This is only an illustration, but it shows how attitudes shape a child's day.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania, based in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, works to raise awareness about autism, ADHD, Down syndrome and intellectual disability, and to support families and caregivers. Our view is simple: every person is valued equally and deserves the chance to reach their full potential. You can read more about this work on our [Neuro-diversity program page](/programs/program-neurodiversity), explore our [mission, vision and values](/about#mission), or [contact us](/contact) with questions.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/volunteering-faq-questions-new-volunteers-ask.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Volunteering FAQ: Questions New Volunteers Often Ask\"\r\nslug: volunteering-faq-questions-new-volunteers-ask\r\ncategory: \"Volunteering\"\r\nexcerpt: \"Clear answers to common questions from people thinking about volunteering, from what to expect to how to prepare, plus where to find the interest form.\"\r\ndescription: \"Clear answers to common questions from people thinking about volunteering, from what to expect to how to prepare, plus where to find the interest form.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [volunteering, faq, getting-started, preparation]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/volunteering-faq-questions-new-volunteers-ask.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/volunteering-faq-questions-new-volunteers-ask-thumb.webp\r\nicon: hand-heart\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"To register your interest in volunteering with Eagle's Wings, use the volunteer interest form on the Get Involved page.\"\r\n  - \"General good practice is to be reliable, respectful and clear about your limits.\"\r\n  - \"Preparing questions, an open mind and a realistic schedule helps you begin well.\"\r\n  - \"Volunteers support the work and do not replace trained professionals.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How do I express interest in volunteering with Eagle's Wings?\"\r\n    a: \"Use the volunteer interest form on the Get Involved page. The Foundation can answer your questions about what happens next, so please ask through the contact details on the site rather than relying on assumptions.\"\r\n  - q: \"Do I need experience with disability or dementia?\"\r\n    a: \"Experience can help, but willingness to listen, learn and treat people with respect matters greatly. Ask the organisation what guidance and orientation it provides for people who are new.\"\r\n  - q: \"What should I bring or prepare for a first session?\"\r\n    a: \"Ask the organisation what to bring and wear. Beyond that, prepare questions, plan your travel, arrive on time and bring an open, patient attitude. Leave phones and photography for later unless you are told they are allowed.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can I volunteer if I only have limited time?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes, being honest about limited time is better than overpromising. A small commitment you can keep is more useful than a large one you cannot. Explain your availability clearly when you get in touch.\"\r\n  - q: \"Will volunteering involve caring for people on my own?\"\r\n    a: \"Ask the organisation directly. In general, tasks such as personal care, exercise or water activities need training and supervision, and responsible volunteers do not take them on informally.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"United Nations Volunteers\"\r\n    publisher: \"UN Volunteers\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unv.org/\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia\"\r\n---\r\nIf you would like to volunteer with Eagle's Wings Foundation, the place to start is the volunteer interest form on the [Get Involved](/get-involved) page. The questions below cover general good practice for new volunteers. For anything specific to the Foundation, such as roles, schedules or requirements, please ask the team directly rather than assuming.\r\n\r\n## Getting started\r\n\r\n### How do I begin?\r\n\r\nFill in the volunteer interest form on the Get Involved page. You can also read the [volunteers page](/volunteers) for an overview. If you have questions first, use the details on the [contact](/contact) page.\r\n\r\n### What information should I be ready to share?\r\n\r\nThink about why you want to volunteer, the skills you could offer and how much time you realistically have. Being honest at this stage helps everyone plan well.\r\n\r\n### Is there anything I should not assume?\r\n\r\nYes. Do not assume particular roles, schedules or entry requirements. Those are decisions for the organisation, and the team is the right source for accurate answers.\r\n\r\n## Understanding the work\r\n\r\n### Who does the Foundation support?\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation works to improve the lives of people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, including those living with Alzheimer's and dementia, and to support their families and caregivers. You can see the five [program areas](/programs) for more detail.\r\n\r\n### Why does this work matter?\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [1.3 billion people, about 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability. It also reports that [more than 55 million people worldwide](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia) live with dementia. Families and caregivers often provide much of the daily support, so extra help from the community can make a real difference when it is offered carefully.\r\n\r\n### Are volunteers replacing professionals?\r\n\r\nNo. Volunteers add time, company and practical help. Diagnosis, therapy and medical decisions stay with qualified professionals. A good volunteer understands and respects that line.\r\n\r\n## Preparing yourself\r\n\r\n### What attitude should I bring?\r\n\r\n- Patience. People move and communicate at different paces.\r\n- Curiosity. Learn what each person prefers instead of guessing.\r\n- Humility. You will learn from staff, families and the people you support.\r\n- Respect. Speak to the person directly and ask before helping.\r\n\r\n### How should I talk about disability and dementia?\r\n\r\nUse respectful, person-first language such as \"person with a disability\" or \"person living with dementia\". Some autistic people prefer \"autistic person\" and others prefer \"person with autism\", so it is fine to ask which they like. Avoid words such as \"suffers from\" or \"handicapped\", and avoid pity or \"miracle\" language.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Write down your questions before a first visit. Good questions about safety, privacy and expectations show that you take the role seriously.\r\n\r\n### What about privacy?\r\n\r\nTreat everything you see and hear about people and families as confidential. Do not take or share photos, names or stories without clear permission from the organisation and the person or their family.\r\n\r\n## Practical concerns\r\n\r\n### How much time should I offer?\r\n\r\nOffer what you can keep up. Predictable, steady help supports people who value routine. You can always increase your commitment later.\r\n\r\n### What if I am not sure I am the right person?\r\n\r\nThat is a normal feeling. Talk it through with the team. It is far better to ask than to stay silent and drop out later, which can leave people without help they expected.\r\n\r\n### Can I volunteer with my professional skills?\r\n\r\nOften yes, when the organisation agrees the scope in advance. Teaching, writing, design and organisation are examples of skills volunteers may share. Clinical treatment and medical advice are different, because they carry professional responsibilities. The UN Volunteers site is a helpful general resource about how volunteering works around the world.\r\n\r\n## A short readiness list\r\n\r\n1. I have read about the Foundation's work and values.\r\n2. I know how much time I can honestly give.\r\n3. I understand that I will follow guidance and supervision.\r\n4. I will respect privacy and use dignified language.\r\n5. I am ready to ask when I am unsure.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation welcomes people who want to give time responsibly. We ask you to begin with the volunteer interest form on the [Get Involved](/get-involved) page, and to read more on the [volunteers page](/volunteers). For general questions about the Foundation, see the [FAQ](/faq) or use the [contact](/contact) page.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/water-safety-and-supervised-swimming-for-young-people.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Water Safety and Supervised Swimming: Common Questions\"\r\nslug: water-safety-and-supervised-swimming-for-young-people\r\ncategory: Life skills\r\nexcerpt: \"Answers to common questions about water safety and supervised swimming for young people with disabilities, with a safety-first approach for families.\"\r\ndescription: \"Answers to common questions about water safety and supervised swimming for young people with disabilities, with a safety-first approach for families.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [water-safety, swimming, supervision, physical-activity]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: care\r\nthumb: care\r\nicon: sparkles\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Water activities should always be supervised by a capable adult who is watching closely.\"\r\n  - \"Introduce water gradually and follow the young person's comfort and signals.\"\r\n  - \"Talk to a doctor or therapist before starting if there are health concerns.\"\r\n  - \"The WHO recommends an average of 60 minutes of physical activity a day for children and adolescents, and water play can be one enjoyable option.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Do young people with disabilities need extra supervision in water?\"\r\n    a: \"Often yes, and any young person around water needs close, undistracted adult supervision. Needs vary by person, so agree a plan with the family and, where possible, a qualified professional before starting.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is swimming a good form of exercise?\"\r\n    a: \"For many people it is an enjoyable way to be active. The WHO recommends an average of 60 minutes of physical activity a day for children and adolescents. Whether swimming suits a particular person is a question for their doctor or therapist.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the young person is afraid of water?\"\r\n    a: \"Go slowly. Let them watch, touch and step in at their own pace. Never force or dunk. Fear is real, and trust matters more than progress.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Physical activity\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n---\r\nWater can be fun, calming and good for building confidence, but it is also a place where safety must come first. The most important rule is close, constant adult supervision, together with a gentle pace that respects the young person. This article answers common questions in general terms. It is not medical advice.\r\n\r\n## Why do families ask about water activities?\r\n\r\nMany young people enjoy the feel of water. It can be a change from the usual routine, a chance to move the body in a different way, and a shared activity with friends. The World Health Organization recommends that children and adolescents average about 60 minutes of physical activity a day, and that adults, including adults living with disability, aim for at least [150 to 300 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) per week. Swimming or water play is one of many ways to move. Walking, cycling, ball games and dancing count too.\r\n\r\n## What does \"supervised\" really mean?\r\n\r\nSupervision is more than being nearby. A supervising adult should:\r\n\r\n- Be within arm's reach when the young person is in or beside deep water.\r\n- Keep eyes on the young person at all times, without phones or other distractions.\r\n- Know how to call for help and what to do in an emergency.\r\n- Know the young person's needs, signals and any advice from their health professional.\r\n\r\nWhere a group is involved, more than one adult may be needed. A qualified swimming teacher or lifeguard is valuable where available.\r\n\r\n## Should we talk to a professional first?\r\n\r\nYes, where possible. Some health conditions, seizures, heart or breathing concerns, and mobility needs affect whether and how water activity is safe. A doctor or therapist can advise on your situation. Please follow their advice and never change or stop medication without them.\r\n\r\n## How can we introduce water gently?\r\n\r\nGo in small steps, and let the young person lead the pace.\r\n\r\n1. Start with looking at water and talking about it.\r\n2. Move to touching the water with hands and feet.\r\n3. Try sitting on a step or shallow area with support.\r\n4. Add short, positive visits before longer ones.\r\n5. Keep the routine predictable, using pictures or words to show what will happen.\r\n6. End each session on a happy note.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Show the sequence in advance: change clothes, enter the water, play, get out, dry, dress. Predictability reduces worry for many young people.\r\n\r\n## What safety habits should always be in place?\r\n\r\n| Habit | Why it matters |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| Adult within reach and watching | Accidents can happen quickly and quietly |\r\n| Clear rules about where to go | Avoids unplanned deep-water entry |\r\n| Safe entry and exit | Reduces slips and falls |\r\n| Checking the space beforehand | Finds hazards early |\r\n| Sun, warmth and hydration care | Keeps the young person comfortable |\r\n| Knowing emergency steps | Enables fast, calm action |\r\n\r\nAdditional points:\r\n\r\n- Never leave a young person alone near any open water, including buckets, tanks, pools, rivers or the sea.\r\n- Ensure the water and surroundings are clean and safe.\r\n- Do not rely on floats or toys as a substitute for an adult.\r\n- Watch for signs of tiredness, cold or distress, and end the session if needed.\r\n- Be aware that some young people may not signal distress in typical ways, so watch closely.\r\n\r\n## What about sensory needs and communication?\r\n\r\nWater brings new sounds, temperatures and sensations. Some young people find echoes or splashing overwhelming. Others seek the feeling of pressure and movement. Notice reactions, and adjust. Quiet times, gentle entry and familiar people help. Agree on a clear signal for \"stop\" or \"I need a break\", such as a card, a gesture or a word, and always respect it.\r\n\r\n## How does swimming connect to life skills?\r\n\r\nWater activities can support wider skills, including following instructions, taking turns, changing clothes independently and caring for personal belongings. These skills link with everyday independence. See our article on [breaking a chore into small teachable steps](/blog/article-breaking-a-chore-into-small-teachable-steps) for one way to teach them.\r\n\r\n## What if a young person does not want to swim?\r\n\r\nThat is fine. Swimming is not for everyone. Other physical activities, such as walking, ball games or dancing, offer benefits too. Choice and comfort matter more than any particular activity.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania includes hydrotherapy and swimming among its public activities, always alongside supervision and respect for each person's comfort. Learn more about how we support independence on our [life skills program page](/programs/program-life-skills), or see moments from our activities in the [gallery](/gallery).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Ways Organisations Can Partner With a Disability Non-Profit\"\r\nslug: ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us\r\ncategory: Partnerships\r\nexcerpt: \"An explainer on four common ways organisations partner with a non-profit: in-kind gifts, technical skills, sponsorship and employee volunteering.\"\r\ndescription: \"An explainer on four common ways organisations partner with a non-profit: in-kind gifts, technical skills, sponsorship and employee volunteering.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [partnerships, sponsorship, volunteering, in-kind]\r\ntemplate: explainer\r\nimage: /photos/blog/ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/ways-organisations-can-partner-with-us-thumb.webp\r\nicon: handshake\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Four common partnership types are in-kind support, technical skills, sponsorship and employee volunteering.\"\r\n  - \"Each type suits different organisations and carries different responsibilities.\"\r\n  - \"The best fit depends on real needs, not on what is easiest to offer.\"\r\n  - \"Any partnership should be agreed clearly and respect the dignity of the people served.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Which type of partnership is best?\"\r\n    a: \"There is no single best type. The right one depends on what the non-profit actually needs and what your organisation can sustain. A conversation about needs and capacity should come before choosing a format.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can a small business partner with a non-profit?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Partnership is not only for large companies. A small business might share a skill, provide materials or support a specific activity over time. What matters is that the offer is realistic and reliable.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is in-kind support better than money?\"\r\n    a: \"Neither is automatically better. In-kind gifts help when they match a real need, but unwanted items can create extra work. Funding gives flexibility. Many partnerships combine both.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Assistive technology\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/assistive-technology\"\r\n  - title: \"United Nations Volunteers\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.unv.org/\"\r\n---\r\nOrganisations usually partner with a non-profit in one of four ways: by giving goods or services (in-kind), by sharing professional skills, by providing sponsorship or funding, or by encouraging employees to volunteer. Each can work well when it matches a real need and is agreed clearly. This guide explains what each involves and how to think about the choice.\r\n\r\n## Why are there different types of partnership?\r\n\r\nNon-profits need many things at once. Some needs are practical, like materials for a classroom. Others need expertise, such as help with planning or communication. Others need steady funding so that programs can continue.\r\n\r\nBecause organisations also differ, partnership comes in different shapes. The World Health Organization notes that [about 16% of people worldwide experience significant disability](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health). Meeting such wide needs usually takes many kinds of contribution, working together.\r\n\r\n## In-kind support\r\n\r\nIn-kind support means giving goods or services instead of money. Examples include materials for learning activities, equipment for exercise sessions, or printing services.\r\n\r\n**Good fit when:** the non-profit has described a specific need and your organisation can supply it reliably.\r\n\r\n**Take care:** unrequested donations can create storage, sorting or safety problems. Equipment for people with disabilities should be suitable for the person using it. The WHO's page on [assistive technology](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/assistive-technology) explains why a good match between a person and a device matters. Always ask before sending anything.\r\n\r\n## Technical and professional skills\r\n\r\nSome partners give expertise instead of goods. This might be help with accounting, design, translation, training materials or digital tools. Skills partnerships can strengthen an organisation for years.\r\n\r\n**Good fit when:** your team has a skill the non-profit lacks and can commit time to see the task through.\r\n\r\n**Take care:** skills should be shared in a way that leaves local people more capable, not more dependent. Agree who owns the finished work and who will maintain it. Health, therapy or clinical guidance should only come from suitably qualified professionals.\r\n\r\n## Sponsorship and funding\r\n\r\nSponsorship means providing money for an activity, a period of work or general running costs. It can be a one-time gift or a repeated commitment.\r\n\r\n**Good fit when:** your organisation prefers to let the non-profit decide how best to use resources, or wants to support a defined activity.\r\n\r\n**Take care:** agree in advance what the money is for, how it will be reported, and how any recognition will be handled. Funding that arrives with too many conditions can distract from the work itself.\r\n\r\n## Employee volunteering\r\n\r\nSome organisations encourage staff to give time. Volunteers might help with events, share a skill or support community activities. The [United Nations Volunteers](https://www.unv.org/) programme shows how widely volunteering is recognised as a way to contribute.\r\n\r\n**Good fit when:** the non-profit has tasks that suit short or occasional help, and there is capacity to prepare and supervise volunteers.\r\n\r\n**Take care:** volunteers working near children, older people or people with disabilities must follow safeguarding rules, and should not be placed in roles that need professional training. Our article on [responsible volunteering in community work](/blog/article-responsible-volunteering-in-community-work) covers this in more depth.\r\n\r\n## How do the four types compare?\r\n\r\n| Type | What is given | Best when | Main thing to agree |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| In-kind | Goods or services | A specific need exists | Suitability and delivery |\r\n| Skills | Professional expertise | A gap in know-how exists | Ownership and follow-through |\r\n| Sponsorship | Funding | Steady support is needed | Purpose and reporting |\r\n| Employee volunteering | Staff time | Tasks suit short-term help | Safeguarding and supervision |\r\n\r\nMany partnerships combine two or more types. For example, a partner might sponsor an activity and also share a professional skill.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Before proposing anything, ask the non-profit what would help most, and be honest about what you can keep doing over time.\r\n\r\n## How do you choose?\r\n\r\nThree questions can help.\r\n\r\n1. **What is the need?** Start with what the non-profit says it needs, not with your preferred gift.\r\n2. **What can we sustain?** A smaller commitment kept for a long time usually helps more than a large one that fades.\r\n3. **Who benefits, and how are they treated?** Confirm that the plan respects people's dignity, privacy and consent.\r\n\r\nOur companion articles on a [partner due-diligence checklist](/blog/article-partner-due-diligence-checklist-both-sides) and on [how a partnership starts](/blog/article-how-a-partnership-starts-and-what-an-agreement-covers) can help with the next steps.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation works in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam, across neuro-diversity awareness, elderly care support, education and therapy guidance, life skills and caregiver training. If you are thinking about which type of partnership might suit your organisation, please [get in touch](/contact) to talk it through.\r\n",
  "../content/blog/when-and-how-to-seek-professional-guidance-faq.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"When and How to Seek Professional Guidance: Questions Answered\"\r\nslug: when-and-how-to-seek-professional-guidance-faq\r\ncategory: Caregiving\r\nexcerpt: \"Plain answers to common questions caregivers ask about when to seek help from doctors, therapists and other professionals, and how to prepare.\"\r\ndescription: \"Plain answers to common questions caregivers ask about when to seek help from doctors, therapists and other professionals, and how to prepare.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [caregiving, professional-help, health, questions]\r\ntemplate: faq\r\nimage: /photos/blog/when-and-how-to-seek-professional-guidance-faq.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/when-and-how-to-seek-professional-guidance-faq-thumb.webp\r\nicon: users-round\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Seek a qualified assessment when you notice a lasting change, a new worry or a loss of skills.\"\r\n  - \"In an emergency, go to the nearest health facility or call emergency services straight away.\"\r\n  - \"Prepare before a visit with notes, questions and a list of medicines, and bring another person if you can.\"\r\n  - \"Only a qualified professional can diagnose, treat or change medicine.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"How do I know it is time to see a professional?\"\r\n    a: \"Seek advice when a change lasts, keeps returning, worries you, or affects daily life. Trust your instincts as a caregiver. You do not have to be sure something is serious before asking.\"\r\n  - q: \"Which professional should I start with?\"\r\n    a: \"A doctor or nurse at a health facility is often a sensible first step. They can examine the person and refer you to specialists such as therapists. Teachers and social workers may also point you to support.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can I decide on a diagnosis myself?\"\r\n    a: \"No. Similar signs can have many causes. Only a qualified professional can carry out an assessment and explain what is happening. Describe what you observe and let them interpret it.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if the person does not want to go?\"\r\n    a: \"Listen to their fears first. Explain the reason in simple words, offer choices about when and how, and consider a familiar person going too. Never force treatment. Ask the professional how to support the visit.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/dementia/\"\r\n  - title: \"Autism\"\r\n    publisher: \"NHS\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/autism/\"\r\n  - title: \"Assistive technology\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/assistive-technology\"\r\n---\r\nCaregivers should seek professional guidance when a change lasts, keeps returning, worries them, or affects daily life, and immediately in an emergency. You do not need to be certain that something is serious before you ask. This article answers common questions in plain language. It is general information and not medical advice.\r\n\r\n## When should I seek help?\r\n\r\nThink about seeking help if you notice any of these:\r\n\r\n- A new change in behaviour, sleep, eating, mobility or mood that continues.\r\n- A loss of skills the person used to have.\r\n- Pain, discomfort or distress that you cannot explain or ease.\r\n- Repeated falls or new safety worries.\r\n- Your own exhaustion, low mood or worry. You count too.\r\n\r\nDo not wait for a crisis. Early conversations are often easier and give more options. The World Health Organization notes that many people with disabilities face barriers to health services, so asking early can matter.\r\n\r\n## What counts as an emergency?\r\n\r\nSudden, severe or frightening changes need urgent help. Go to the nearest health facility or contact emergency services. Do not wait for an appointment and do not rely on advice from an article. If you are unsure whether something is an emergency, treat it as one and ask for help.\r\n\r\n## Who can I turn to?\r\n\r\nDifferent professionals help with different needs.\r\n\r\n| Professional | How they may help |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Doctor or nurse | Examine the person, discuss health concerns, refer to specialists and manage medicine |\r\n| Therapist | Guide exercises and skills that support movement, communication or daily activity |\r\n| Teacher or school staff | Adjust learning and share what they see in the classroom |\r\n| Social worker or counsellor | Advise on family support, wellbeing and practical help |\r\n| Community or faith leader | Offer encouragement and connect families with others |\r\n\r\nEach role is different. A teacher or a community leader cannot replace a medical assessment, but may help you take the next step.\r\n\r\n## How do I prepare for a visit?\r\n\r\nPreparation makes a short visit more useful.\r\n\r\n1. **Write down your concerns.** List what you have noticed, when it started and what makes it better or worse.\r\n2. **Bring your notes.** A simple daily record is very helpful. See [how to keep a simple daily observation log](/blog/article-keeping-a-simple-daily-observation-log).\r\n3. **List medicines and supplements.** Bring the containers or a written list.\r\n4. **Write your questions.** People often forget them in the room.\r\n5. **Bring another person** if you can, for support and a second pair of ears.\r\n6. **Prepare the person:** explain where you are going and why, in simple words.\r\n\r\n> **Practical tip:** Ask, \"Can you explain that in simpler words?\" and \"What should I watch for at home?\" Professionals want you to understand.\r\n\r\n## What should I ask during the visit?\r\n\r\n- What do you think is happening, and what are the next steps?\r\n- What can we do at home to help?\r\n- What signs mean we should come back quickly?\r\n- Is there anyone else who could help, such as a therapist or specialist?\r\n- Can you write the plan down for me?\r\n\r\nAsk about medicine only from the doctor or nurse who prescribed it. Never stop, start or change medicine without their advice.\r\n\r\n## What if I do not understand or agree?\r\n\r\nYou may ask for clarification, or for a second opinion. Do so politely and openly. It is reasonable to want to understand the plan. If language, distance or cost is a barrier, say so, and ask what options exist. Do not assume prices or services, because these vary, and check locally.\r\n\r\n## Should the person be part of the decision?\r\n\r\nYes. A person with a disability or an older person has a right to be heard about their own care. The UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), in force since 2008, is built on dignity, autonomy and participation. Speak to the person, not only about them. Explain what will happen and ask what worries them.\r\n\r\n## What about my own wellbeing?\r\n\r\nCaregiving can be tiring. If you feel overwhelmed, hopeless or unwell, tell a health worker or a trusted person. That is not a failure. Our perspective piece, [why caregivers need support too](/blog/article-why-caregivers-need-support-too), explores this in more depth.\r\n\r\nSharing tasks across relatives can also protect your health. See our [checklist for sharing care tasks across a family](/blog/article-sharing-care-tasks-across-a-family-checklist).\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania is not a clinic and does not diagnose or treat. We offer education and guidance for families, and one of our five program areas is caregiver training. It helps caregivers gain confidence, understand when to ask questions and work respectfully with professionals. Read more on the [caregiver training program page](/programs/program-caregiver-training), or see our [general FAQ](/faq).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/why-caregivers-need-support-too.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Why Caregivers Need Support Too\"\r\nslug: why-caregivers-need-support-too\r\ncategory: Caregiving\r\nexcerpt: \"Caregiving is real work with real limits. A perspective on caregiver stress, self-care without guilt, and why supporting carers supports everyone.\"\r\ndescription: \"Caregiving is real work with real limits. A perspective on caregiver stress, self-care without guilt, and why supporting carers supports everyone.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [caregiving, self-care, wellbeing, support]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/why-caregivers-need-support-too.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/why-caregivers-need-support-too-thumb.webp\r\nicon: users-round\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Caregiving is demanding work, and needing support is not a weakness.\"\r\n  - \"Self-care is part of good care, not a betrayal of the person you look after.\"\r\n  - \"Small, regular rests and shared tasks protect health more than rare big breaks.\"\r\n  - \"Caregivers can ask for help from family, community and qualified professionals.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"Is it selfish to take time for myself?\"\r\n    a: \"No. A caregiver who is exhausted cannot give their best care. Rest, food, sleep and time with other people help you continue. Looking after yourself is part of caring for the person.\"\r\n  - q: \"What are signs that a caregiver needs help?\"\r\n    a: \"Constant tiredness, feeling hopeless or tearful, irritability, withdrawing from friends, or neglecting your own health may all be signs. If you notice them, talk to a trusted person or a health worker.\"\r\n  - q: \"What if I feel resentful sometimes?\"\r\n    a: \"Mixed feelings are common and human. They do not mean you do not love the person. Talk honestly with someone you trust, and seek support if these feelings are heavy or lasting.\"\r\n  - q: \"Where can I find support?\"\r\n    a: \"Start with relatives, neighbours, faith or community groups, and a doctor, nurse or social worker. Training programs for caregivers can also help you build skills and meet others in similar situations.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Dementia\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia\"\r\n  - title: \"Ageing and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ageing-and-health\"\r\n---\r\nPeople who care for a child with a disability, or for an older person living with dementia, are doing skilled, tiring and often unseen work. Our view at Eagle's Wings is simple. Caregivers are not extras in the story of care. They are central to it, and they deserve support of their own.\r\n\r\n## The work that is often unseen\r\n\r\nCare does not end when the visitors go home. It is meals, washing, watching, listening, planning, calming and worrying, day after day. Much of it goes unnoticed because it happens inside the home and is done out of love.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization estimates that [1.3 billion people, about 16% of the world's population,](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health) experience significant disability, and that [more than 55 million people](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia) live with dementia. Behind these numbers stand families, and in many families one or two people carry much of the daily load.\r\n\r\nThis is an opinion piece, and it draws on values, not on stories about specific people. We are not describing anyone's experience. We are describing why a fair approach to care includes the carer.\r\n\r\n## Why is it hard to ask for help?\r\n\r\nMany caregivers hesitate to ask. Some of the reasons are understandable:\r\n\r\n- A feeling that no one else can do it properly.\r\n- Worry about being seen as weak or ungrateful.\r\n- Love and duty, which make rest feel like a betrayal.\r\n- Not knowing what help exists.\r\n\r\nIn many communities, including in Tanzania, families and neighbours have long shared care. That tradition is a strength. But when families are busy, spread out or short of money, one person can end up alone. Then the tradition needs to be renewed, not assumed.\r\n\r\n## Self-care without guilt\r\n\r\nSelf-care is a phrase that can sound like a luxury. It is not. It is maintenance, like feeding a fire so it keeps burning.\r\n\r\n### What self-care can look like\r\n\r\nSelf-care does not need money or free days. It can be small:\r\n\r\n- Eating regular meals and drinking enough water.\r\n- Sleeping when the chance comes.\r\n- Taking a short walk, if you are able.\r\n- Talking with a friend or relative.\r\n- Praying, singing or sitting quietly.\r\n- Seeing a doctor or nurse about your own health.\r\n\r\nThe WHO recommends regular physical activity for adults, at least [150 to 300 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) per week. Take that as a general target and speak to a health professional about what suits your own body.\r\n\r\n### Letting go of guilt\r\n\r\nGuilt often says: \"If I rest, something will go wrong.\" A better thought is: \"If I never rest, I may not be able to go on.\" Rest is part of the plan, not a break from it.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** Feeling tired, sad or resentful at times is common among caregivers. It does not mean you are failing. If these feelings are heavy or last a long time, speak to a doctor, nurse or counsellor.\r\n\r\n## Support is more than advice\r\n\r\nReal support has several forms:\r\n\r\n| Kind of support | What it looks like |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Practical | Someone takes over a meal, a school run or an evening |\r\n| Emotional | Someone listens without judging |\r\n| Informational | Someone explains the condition, the options and the next steps |\r\n| Professional | A doctor, nurse or therapist gives guidance for the individual |\r\n| Skills | Training that builds confidence in daily care |\r\n\r\nNo single person provides all five. That is why sharing matters. Our [checklist for sharing care tasks across a family](/blog/article-sharing-care-tasks-across-a-family-checklist) offers a starting point for that conversation.\r\n\r\n## Why does this matter to the person being cared for?\r\n\r\nA rested, supported caregiver can be patient, attentive and kind. An exhausted caregiver may struggle, through no fault of their own. Supporting the carer therefore protects the person receiving care too. It also supports dignity on both sides, because nobody should be pushed beyond their limits.\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), in force since 2008, is built on dignity, autonomy and inclusion. We believe that the same spirit applies to families. A person with a disability has a right to be valued, and their caregivers deserve to be treated with respect too.\r\n\r\n## What can we each do?\r\n\r\n- **Families:** Notice who is carrying the load. Offer a specific task, not just \"let me know\".\r\n- **Neighbours and community:** Visit, listen and share a meal.\r\n- **Caregivers:** Name one small thing that would help and ask for it.\r\n- **Everyone:** Speak of caregiving as work worth recognising.\r\n\r\nFor practical routines that include rest, see [supporting families with consistent care routines](/blog/article-supporting-families-with-consistent-care-routines). When worries grow, [seeking professional guidance](/blog/article-when-and-how-to-seek-professional-guidance-faq) is a sign of strength.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania was founded in 2025 in Tegeta, Dar es Salaam. We support people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and their families and caregivers. Caregiver training is one of our five program areas, because we believe carers should be equipped, respected and never alone. Learn more on the [caregiver training program page](/programs/program-caregiver-training), or find ways to [get involved](/get-involved).\r\n",
  "../content/blog/why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling.md": "---\r\ntitle: \"Why Consent Matters in Nonprofit Storytelling\"\r\nslug: why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling\r\ncategory: Safeguarding\r\nexcerpt: \"A values-based view on why informed consent, the right to withdraw and dignity must come before any photo, video or story about people we support.\"\r\ndescription: \"A values-based view on why informed consent, the right to withdraw and dignity must come before any photo, video or story about people we support.\"\r\ndate: 2026-09-19\r\nupdated: 2026-09-19\r\nlastUpdated: 2026-09-19\r\nauthor: \"Eagle's Wings Editorial Team\"\r\ntags: [consent, dignity, storytelling, privacy]\r\ntemplate: perspective\r\nimage: /photos/blog/why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling.webp\r\nthumb: /photos/blog/why-consent-matters-in-nonprofit-storytelling-thumb.webp\r\nicon: shield-check\r\nfeatured: false\r\nkeyTakeaways:\r\n  - \"Consent is not a signature on a form. It is an informed, free choice that a person can change later.\"\r\n  - \"Children and adults who may not be able to consent for themselves need a parent, guardian or trusted representative involved, and their own wishes still count.\"\r\n  - \"A good story shows a person's dignity and abilities. It does not use pity or reveal details that could identify or endanger them.\"\r\n  - \"If consent is unclear, the answer is simple: do not publish.\"\r\nfaq:\r\n  - q: \"What is informed consent in storytelling?\"\r\n    a: \"It means a person understands what will be shared, where it will appear, who may see it and for how long, and then freely agrees. They must be able to say no without losing any support or service.\"\r\n  - q: \"Who gives consent for a child?\"\r\n    a: \"Usually a parent or guardian, after a clear explanation. The child should also be asked in a way they can understand, and their wish not to take part should be respected.\"\r\n  - q: \"Can someone change their mind later?\"\r\n    a: \"Yes. Consent can be withdrawn at any time. A responsible organisation makes withdrawal easy, removes the material where it can, explains what has been done, and does not pressure the person to reconsider.\"\r\n  - q: \"Is it acceptable to share a photo without a name?\"\r\n    a: \"Not automatically. A face, a uniform, a place or a story detail can still identify someone to people who know them. Removing a name helps, but permission is still needed before anything is shared.\"\r\nsources:\r\n  - title: \"Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities\"\r\n    publisher: \"United Nations\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html\"\r\n  - title: \"Disability and health\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\"\r\n  - title: \"Violence against children\"\r\n    publisher: \"World Health Organization\"\r\n    url: \"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/violence-against-children\"\r\n---\r\nConsent matters in nonprofit storytelling because the people in the story own their own lives. A photo, a video or a personal account is never just content. It is a piece of someone's identity, and they should decide if it is shared. Real consent is informed, freely given and open to withdrawal.\r\n\r\nThis is a values-based view from the Eagle's Wings Editorial Team. It describes how we believe organisations should behave. It is general guidance, not legal advice.\r\n\r\n## Why is storytelling risky for nonprofits?\r\n\r\nStories move people. That is exactly why they are tempting. A moving image can bring attention and support to a cause. But the person in that image did not choose to become a symbol. They may be a child, an older person living with dementia, or a person with a disability who has faced being talked about instead of listened to.\r\n\r\nThe UN [Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities](https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities.html), adopted in 2006 and in force since 2008, is built on dignity, autonomy and respect for difference. Autonomy includes the right to decide how your image and story are used. If a story takes that choice away, it works against the values the organisation says it holds.\r\n\r\n## What does real consent look like?\r\n\r\nConsent is more than a signature. A form can be signed under pressure, without understanding, or by someone who felt they could not refuse. Real consent has four qualities.\r\n\r\n### It is informed\r\n\r\nThe person knows what will be shared, for what purpose, and where it may appear, such as a website, a social media page or a printed leaflet. Explain in plain language and in the language the person uses at home.\r\n\r\n### It is freely given\r\n\r\nNobody should feel that saying yes is a condition for support, or that saying no will change how they are treated. Ask at a calm moment, not on the day of an activity in front of others.\r\n\r\n### It is specific\r\n\r\nAgreeing to one photo for one purpose is not agreement to every future use. If the plan changes, ask again.\r\n\r\n### It can be withdrawn\r\n\r\nA person can change their mind. A responsible organisation makes it easy to say so, acts quickly, and does not make the person feel guilty.\r\n\r\n## What about children and people who may not be able to consent alone?\r\n\r\nSome people cannot give full, informed consent on their own. This includes children and some adults, for example an older person living with advanced dementia or a person with an intellectual disability. In these cases, ask a parent, guardian or trusted representative, and explain everything just as clearly.\r\n\r\nTheir permission is necessary, but it is not the end of the matter. The person themselves should still be asked in a way they can understand, and their reaction matters. If a child pulls away from the camera or a person seems upset, stop. A guardian's yes does not cancel a person's obvious no.\r\n\r\n> **Good to know:** If you are not sure who has the right to give consent for someone, do not guess. Ask the organisation's leadership and, where needed, seek qualified advice.\r\n\r\n## Dignity in the story itself\r\n\r\nConsent is only the first step. The story also has to be told well.\r\n\r\n- Show people as whole persons with abilities, interests and choices, not only as needs.\r\n- Avoid pity language, such as \"suffering\" or \"helpless\", and avoid \"miracle\" stories.\r\n- Use \"person with a disability\" and \"person living with dementia\".\r\n- Never pair a person's image with claims about their diagnosis or private life unless they have clearly agreed.\r\n- Keep out anything that could identify or endanger them, such as home addresses, school names or daily routes.\r\n\r\nThe World Health Organization warns that children can face violence and harm in many settings. Careless publicity can add to risk, especially where a child's location or circumstances are easy to work out.\r\n\r\n## What if consent is unclear?\r\n\r\nThe rule is simple: if consent is missing, unclear or doubtful, do not publish. A missed opportunity costs very little compared with harm to a person's privacy or safety. Stock images, illustrations, photos of activities where no one is identifiable, and general descriptions written without personal details can all tell a fair story.\r\n\r\n## Why this is about trust, not only rules\r\n\r\nFamilies entrust organisations with people they love. That trust is fragile. When a family sees that permission was asked, respected and easy to withdraw, they can relax and take part fully. When they see the opposite, they may stop coming.\r\n\r\nGood storytelling also produces better stories. When people take part willingly, the result feels warm, respectful and true to life, and readers can tell.\r\n\r\n## What this means at Eagle's Wings\r\n\r\nEagle's Wings Foundation Tanzania works with people with disabilities and challenges, especially children and older people, and their families and caregivers. We believe that respecting people's dignity applies to how we speak about them as much as to how we support them. To learn how personal information is handled, please read our [privacy page](/privacy). If you have a question or wish to withdraw permission for something, [contact us](/contact). You may also like our guide on [protecting families' personal information online](/blog/article-protecting-families-personal-information-online).\r\n",
}
